WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.363
Hey, everybody.

2
00:00:01.363 --> 00:00:02.220
Welcome to the podcast.

3
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.480
My guest today is Rich Diviney.

4
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.490
Rich is a retired Navy SEAL commander.

5
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.760
He's the author of a new book called "The Attributes,"

6
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.840
which is all about the drivers behind human performance.

7
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.270
It's an amazing conversation,

8
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.620
super dense, tons of takeaways.

9
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.250
I really think it's the perfect way to launch into 2021.

10
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.080
So, hit that "Subscribe" button

11
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.330
and please enjoy my conversation with Rich Diviney.

12
00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:35.098 line:15%
All right.

13
00:00:35.098 --> 00:00:35.931 line:15%
We're rolling.

14
00:00:35.931 --> 00:00:36.764 line:15%
Good to see you.

15
00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:37.597 line:15%
Thank you for having me.

16
00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:38.430 line:15%
Yeah, thanks for being here.

17
00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:39.760 line:15%
I'm excited about this.

18
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.290 line:15%
We have a little bit of a tradition

19
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.300
that we started a couple of years ago on the podcast

20
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.070
where we kick off the new year with a Navy SEAL.

21
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.345
People seem to enjoy that.

22
00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:49.178
Yeah.

23
00:00:49.178 --> 00:00:51.650
It started with David Goggins a couple of years ago.

24
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.503
We had Chad right last year.

25
00:00:55.196 --> 00:00:59.660
And you're gonna be the 2021 edition.

26
00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.590
I'm deeply honored.

27
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:01.579
So, thank you.

28
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:02.620
Yeah, thank you for doing this.

29
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.420
And what's interesting about you

30
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.080
among many other many things,

31
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.940
but when you think of Navy SEALs

32
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.500
in this modern culture context,

33
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.490
we think about the movies,

34
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.340
we think about people like Jocko Willink

35
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.570
who is sort of this emblem of leadership.

36
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.680
We think of David Goggins,

37
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.110
who's kind of this emblem of physical and mental discipline,

38
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.100
very alpha-type personalities,

39
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.740
but you're coming from a more analytical pose.

40
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.370
Somebody who's much more interested in the mental game

41
00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.250
versus the physical game.

42
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.920
And given your experience and your stature

43
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.040
in the special ops ecosystem,

44
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.580
somebody who's spent 21 years in the military

45
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.810
as a Navy SEAL, 13 overseas deployments,

46
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.760
serving as commanding officer

47
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.680
of a hereafter to be unnamed elite force within the SEALs.

48
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.650
You have a tremendous amount of experience.

49
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.210
And it leads me,

50
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.920
your perspective, which we're gonna get into,

51
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.920
leads me to conclude that perhaps this popular conception

52
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.260
that we have about the Navy SEALs is a little bit misled.

53
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.470
Mm-hmm. Yeah.

54
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:15.960
Yeah. Well, you're absolutely right.

55
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.560
And I think that it's for two reasons I guess.

56
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.770
First is, you have to,

57
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.569
pop culture has to entertain first and foremost.

58
00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:30.569
And so, any type of story has to highlight the high highs

59
00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:33.690
and the low lows, if you're gonna have drama.

60
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:38.280
And life, no matter what endeavor you're in,

61
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.950
is never just high high and low low.

62
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.950
I mean, a lot of SEAL life is decidedly normal.

63
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:50.020
I remember having family or even special guests

64
00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.470
who wanna come visit the SEAL teams

65
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.303
when I was in the SEAL teams.

66
00:02:53.303 --> 00:02:56.520
And they come and they'd be walking around the teams

67
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.353
and I'd be like,

68
00:02:57.353 --> 00:02:59.110
"Hey, you probably expected to see parachutes everywhere

69
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.911
and guns and all this exciting stuff."

70
00:03:00.911 --> 00:03:01.744
(laughs) Yeah.

71
00:03:01.744 --> 00:03:03.010
And that's not the case.

72
00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.180
This is just a bunch of buildings.

73
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.047
This is where we work.

74
00:03:05.047 --> 00:03:08.370
A lot of our training is offsite in remote areas.

75
00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.790
And I guess I've never watched a movie get made

76
00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.750
other than on the periphery I've seen some movie sets.

77
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.897
But I would imagine from what I know about the experience

78
00:03:16.897 --> 00:03:17.730
it's kinda the same thing.

79
00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.150
I mean, the movie-making process is decidedly slow

80
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.530
and boring for most of it.

81
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.220
You're just looking for that one-minute shot, right?

82
00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.240
And I think that that's what happens in kind of SEAL movies

83
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.610
and even SEAL books to the extent

84
00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.686
that they are as you're recounting the high highs

85
00:03:34.686 --> 00:03:35.519
and the low lows.

86
00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:38.064
And it's more normal than that.

87
00:03:38.064 --> 00:03:38.897
Right.

88
00:03:38.897 --> 00:03:42.150
Yeah, you think you're gonna see guys pounding their chest

89
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.990
and doing hill repeats and all kinds of crazy stuff,

90
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.140
but part of the whole thing is to bleed into society.

91
00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.052
Right?

92
00:03:49.052 --> 00:03:49.885
Yeah,

93
00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:50.718
There's an invisibility aspect to it.

94
00:03:50.718 --> 00:03:53.730
Like you're meant to kind of disappear into the background.

95
00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.597
Absolutely.

96
00:03:54.597 --> 00:03:56.590
And again, SEALs are humans.

97
00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.020
And so, one of the things that I'm fascinated with

98
00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.920
and have always been fascinated with is this idea

99
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.920
that we all consider ourselves fairly average dudes

100
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.090
who just happened to go down an extraordinary path.

101
00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.160
And so, operating in that path,

102
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.340
what are those things that make someone be able to do that

103
00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.765
and do the job consistently, do it well,

104
00:04:21.765 --> 00:04:24.720
and how does that separate from other people?

105
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.210
Because everybody has competencies.

106
00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:31.810
Everybody is a rock star in certain contexts of life

107
00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.744
and a doofus in other contexts.

108
00:04:33.744 --> 00:04:34.577
Right?

109
00:04:34.577 --> 00:04:37.178
There are many contexts in life where I'm a doofus.

110
00:04:37.178 --> 00:04:39.230
And so, and you look at like professional athletes

111
00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.500
and you say the legit like LeBron James

112
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.380
is a master on the basketball court.

113
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.768
I'm sure he would tell you at least 10-

114
00:04:46.768 --> 00:04:47.810
Put him in a swimming pool.

115
00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.160
Yeah, at least 10 different context

116
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.390
inside of which he is a complete doofus.

117
00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:52.363
And he'd probably be the first to admit it.

118
00:04:52.363 --> 00:04:54.190
He's like, "No, no, I'm bad at that."

119
00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.023
Right?

120
00:04:55.023 --> 00:04:56.620
So, I think I've been fascinated

121
00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.270
with what are those aspects that allow humans

122
00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.095
to understand their own potential, their own performance.

123
00:05:01.095 --> 00:05:05.200
And then if they understand it enough, pick the right path.

124
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.270
Sometimes consciously,

125
00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.020
in my case, in many cases with team guys, unconsciously

126
00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.779
because we just found ourselves wanting to do it

127
00:05:10.779 --> 00:05:13.430
and going there and making it through at young ages.

128
00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.410
So, not very self-aware at that point in your life.

129
00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.243
Right.

130
00:05:17.243 --> 00:05:18.076
Well, most people aren't, right?

131
00:05:18.076 --> 00:05:19.190
It's exactly right. Yeah.

132
00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:23.660
We're sort of reacting impulsively to unconscious drivers

133
00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.050
until they reach a certain level of maturity

134
00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.930
where things are either working or not working

135
00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.500
where we engage in that process of evaluation

136
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.285
to try to understand what those drivers are.

137
00:05:32.285 --> 00:05:34.400
Which is really kind of the core of your work.

138
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.330
But you said something interesting,

139
00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:42.180
which was that you and the SEALs

140
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.700
would consider themselves to be average people

141
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.910
who've put themselves in extraordinary situation

142
00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.050
in which to excel and grow.

143
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.750
But it takes a certain type of individual

144
00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.410
to sign up for something like that.

145
00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.320
Like when you talk about the drivers

146
00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.427
that would compel somebody to say,

147
00:05:58.427 --> 00:06:01.190
"That's the life that I wanna pursue for myself."

148
00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.023
I agree.

149
00:06:02.023 --> 00:06:06.310
And I agree, but I'd also say that I would offer

150
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.840
that it also takes a certain mentality

151
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.840
for someone to wanna be a surgeon or an athlete

152
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.930
or a teacher or any profession

153
00:06:17.894 --> 00:06:19.670
that I really honor and respect.

154
00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.390
And I think this is one of the things I really enjoyed

155
00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:27.390
about the teams was that the majority of the guys

156
00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.982
respected hard work and purpose and movement.

157
00:06:31.982 --> 00:06:36.180
And so, there was very little ego

158
00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.560
around people who really did their job well.

159
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.240
I mean, the janitors at our commands who did awesome jobs,

160
00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:44.073
awesome people.

161
00:06:44.073 --> 00:06:45.180
And there was no judgment.

162
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.060
There was no putting oneself above others

163
00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.440
because you really honor and respect someone's purpose,

164
00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.553
dedication, and hard work.

165
00:06:52.553 --> 00:06:57.553
What you start to have trouble with is when you see apathy

166
00:06:57.621 --> 00:07:02.110
or misdirection.

167
00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:03.480
People who just aren't on the path.

168
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.780
They don't really want, there's no drive.

169
00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.910
I mean, I think that's...

170
00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.500
And again, I say have an issue with,

171
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.840
you just you don't resonate with those people as much.

172
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.460
And so, anybody who's picked a path and pursued it

173
00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.070
and done it with deliberacy and consistency and integrity

174
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.460
and I would say success, but not even success.

175
00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.630
As long as they've pursued it, there's respect there.

176
00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:28.960
In terms of the SEAL pathway,

177
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.140
I would certainly think that there are commonalities

178
00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.560
amongst the guys who want to do it.

179
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.320
And I think the process to get there,

180
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.490
to get to BUD/S in the first place is certainly difficult.

181
00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:41.627
So, I always used to say,

182
00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:43.608
"Hey, part of the selection process for BUD/S

183
00:07:43.608 --> 00:07:44.837
is getting there in the first place."

184
00:07:44.837 --> 00:07:46.030
That's the first part.

185
00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.120
Because there's a lot of guys,

186
00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.180
you'll always meet a bunch of,

187
00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.647
you'll always be guys who say,

188
00:07:50.647 --> 00:07:53.370
"Oh yeah, I always wanted to be a SEAL.

189
00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.670
I just add this, I did this and then that,

190
00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:56.503
and then this happened."

191
00:07:56.503 --> 00:07:57.447
And in the back of your mind is like,

192
00:07:57.447 --> 00:07:59.321
"Okay, well, you got deselected."

193
00:07:59.321 --> 00:08:00.420
(laughs) Yeah.

194
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.224
I think that was one of the things that happened.

195
00:08:02.224 --> 00:08:03.057
Right?

196
00:08:03.057 --> 00:08:04.021
You never pursued-

197
00:08:04.021 --> 00:08:04.867
You deselected yourself.

198
00:08:04.867 --> 00:08:05.700
You selected yourself.

199
00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.330
You never pursued it to the two day one

200
00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.090
on the beaches of Coronado.

201
00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:10.923
Right.

202
00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:13.970
And then let the BUD/S process

203
00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:18.970
then throw you into massive challenge.

204
00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.863
And even in that context,

205
00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.825
people are deselecting themselves.

206
00:08:23.825 --> 00:08:27.870
It's rare that somebody gets tossed out, right?

207
00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.980
Like people are just opting out to quit.

208
00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:30.813
It is rare.

209
00:08:30.813 --> 00:08:31.646
Yeah, it happens.

210
00:08:31.646 --> 00:08:33.870
I mean, and I would actually say

211
00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.133
that the process has matured and evolved over the years.

212
00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:39.660
I mean, I went through in '96, right?

213
00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.421
So, it's matured and evolved over the years to do better

214
00:08:43.421 --> 00:08:47.440
at deliberately deselecting those

215
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.310
who might not fit the mold.

216
00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.934
Because again, as the community has gained popularity,

217
00:08:52.934 --> 00:08:57.934
the reasons for which guys have come in have shifted.

218
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.150
And you and I had a little bit of a conversation about this

219
00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.320
before we came on.

220
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.720
But in the mid '90s,

221
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.520
very few people knew what Navy SEALs were

222
00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.890
when me and all my buddies joined.

223
00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.240
We all wanted to be kind of bad asses, invisible warriors

224
00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.600
with like the water or whatever.

225
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:14.650
And then the war started

226
00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:18.420
and suddenly the spec ops holistically and the military,

227
00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:20.860
but spec ops who was doing a lot of the bulk

228
00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.860
of the initial work at least hunting down bad guys,

229
00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.410
suddenly these kids who are seeing that,

230
00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:30.650
they're like, "Oh, wait."

231
00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:31.927
That the reasons changed.

232
00:09:31.927 --> 00:09:33.100
"I wanna go do that.

233
00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:33.933
I wanna...

234
00:09:33.933 --> 00:09:36.710
The towers come down and hey, I wanna go serve my country."

235
00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.020
And that's another reason.

236
00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:39.370
And again, there's no,

237
00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:40.203
both are honorable.

238
00:09:40.203 --> 00:09:41.036
They're just different, right?

239
00:09:41.036 --> 00:09:42.390
There's no judgment on these reasons.

240
00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:47.390
And then of course, as spec ops and the SEALs in particular

241
00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.967
gain popularity, and we're kinda like,

242
00:09:49.967 --> 00:09:52.140
"Oh my gosh, now we're visible,"

243
00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:55.240
that you can know that the reasons are changing again

244
00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.073
on why are guys joining.

245
00:09:56.073 --> 00:09:58.410
So, the community has done a really good job

246
00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.860
at starting to identify what those attributes,

247
00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.630
specifically, okay, what are the attributes

248
00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.460
we're really looking for

249
00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.497
and how do we accurately assess those

250
00:10:07.497 --> 00:10:09.450
and pick the right people?

251
00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.410
And it's not just the guy who can make it through Hell Week

252
00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.280
and not just a guy who could run with a telephone pole.

253
00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.790
It's there'll be more integrity and character

254
00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.620
and things like that and ethos and those guys.

255
00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.720
And so, I think it's been very impressive

256
00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.920
to see them make that evolution.

257
00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.270
Yeah.

258
00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.860
All the conversation seems to be around BUD/S,

259
00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.480
but it's so much more than that.

260
00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:33.313
It is. It is.

261
00:10:33.313 --> 00:10:36.000
I mean, so I always joke,

262
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.092
and this is kind of how I started thinking about the work,

263
00:10:39.092 --> 00:10:43.440
in BUD/S and how we specifically.

264
00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.750
But you carry telephone poles around.

265
00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.080
