00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:50.540
And you did it by dent of realizing that this was important.

532
00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:53.360
I mean, you open the book with this story

533
00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:55.466
of like this experienced SEAL.

534
00:20:55.466 --> 00:20:56.860
I think you had like eight years, right?

535
00:20:56.860 --> 00:21:00.430
Who was having an issue with this very specific

536
00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:03.150
like mission training that you were doing

537
00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.730
where he despite being very good on paper,

538
00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.420
he just couldn't figure out

539
00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.000
how to excel in that environment.

540
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:09.930
And that led you,

541
00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:10.877
I mean, I want you to explain that,

542
00:21:10.877 --> 00:21:13.870
but that kinda led you into thinking more deeply

543
00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:17.220
about why these things weren't matching up

544
00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:20.070
in the way that you thought perhaps they should.

545
00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:25.070
Yeah, I had the privilege of running training,

546
00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:28.710
assessment and selection and training

547
00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:30.560
for one of our really specialized SEAL commands.

548
00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:31.730
And at this particular command,

549
00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:36.040
we would take experienced operators from other commands

550
00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:38.450
and they'd apply to our command.

551
00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.620
And we put those guys through our own selection process.

552
00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:42.453
So, you're talking about guys

553
00:21:42.453 --> 00:21:45.710
who have between five and 10 years of experience already

554
00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:47.930
in the team, successful dudes.

555
00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:50.563
And we were getting about a 50% attrition rate.

556
00:21:51.406 --> 00:21:53.690
And that happens, right?

557
00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:56.150
But the problem was, and when I took over,

558
00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.730
my CEO had said, "Hey, we need to do better articulating

559
00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:01.320
why guys aren't making it through."

560
00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:03.150
Because the best explanation a lot of times

561
00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:04.200
was why you didn't cut it.

562
00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.950
You couldn't do this right or you couldn't do that right.

563
00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:07.004
Right.

564
00:22:07.004 --> 00:22:07.837
But what does that mean?

565
00:22:07.837 --> 00:22:08.670
What does that mean?

566
00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.160
And it was leaving a sour taste in our mouths

567
00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:11.780
because we weren't explaining it properly.

568
00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:15.460
It was leaving a sour taste in the candidate's mouth

569
00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:18.307
because they just they came from this position of,

570
00:22:18.307 --> 00:22:19.500
"Hey, I thought I was doing great.

571
00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.090
I got accepted for this thing.

572
00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.750
Now they're telling me I'm not good enough.

573
00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:23.583
What the heck?"

574
00:22:23.583 --> 00:22:24.612
Right?

575
00:22:24.612 --> 00:22:26.350
And so, they asked me to look at it.

576
00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.950
And so, that's what took me back.

577
00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.930
And I began to look really at the foundation,

578
00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:33.080
the origins of the UDT, the Underwater Demolition Teams

579
00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:35.150
and Draper Kauffman and this idea

580
00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:36.650
that before the allied invasion,

581
00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:41.650
they realized that they needed to have teams of dudes

582
00:22:42.675 --> 00:22:44.700
swim or shore measure,

583
00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.160
basically measure depth, identify obstacles,

584
00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.410
and blow them up, blow paths clear if necessary.

585
00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:53.150
And so, they tapped this guy, Draper Kauffman

586
00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:57.480
who had put together an explosive ordnance school

587
00:22:58.387 --> 00:23:01.760
a few years prior said, "Hey, can you create this unit?"

588
00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:04.330
And Kauffman knew that he needed guys.

589
00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:08.580
He already had, definitely he had run an explosive school.

590
00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.890
So, he already had at his disposal,

591
00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.670
a bunch of guys who knew how to do the job, right?

592
00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:17.150
Who knew how to tie demolition on obstacles and swim

593
00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:18.690
and do whatever that is.

594
00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.210
What he recognized he needed was to figure out

595
00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:22.510
who could do the job,

596
00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:23.920
because these guys would be swimming

597
00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:27.280
into heavily-defended beaches with all these,

598
00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:30.520
a knife and some explosives and swim trunks.

599
00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:32.630
They'd have to adapt on the fly.

600
00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:34.680
They'd have to figure out what was going on.

601
00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:35.920
The environments would change.

602
00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:37.260
It would get ugly.

603
00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:40.250
So, I call it kind of a unconscious genius.

604
00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:41.760
And he said, "Well, I'm gonna start my training

605
00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:45.300
with a week of the most difficult things I can imagine."

606
00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:48.880
And so, he started training with what is now Hell Week

607
00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.076
and basically ran guys through the gambit

608
00:23:52.076 --> 00:23:55.430
of combat simulations, explosives,

609
00:23:55.430 --> 00:23:57.074
some problem-solving this and that.

610
00:23:57.074 --> 00:23:58.110
But it was really just very grueling.

611
00:23:58.110 --> 00:23:59.783
Guys slept for only couple hours.

612
00:24:00.679 --> 00:24:01.512
And-

613
00:24:01.512 --> 00:24:02.960
Is this back when it was the frogman?

614
00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:03.920
That's exactly right.

615
00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:04.937
Yeah.

616
00:24:04.937 --> 00:24:09.937
And he basically didn't run any testing or evaluation

617
00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:11.683
during that process.

618
00:24:11.683 --> 00:24:14.890
The decision to stay or go rested on the candidate.

619
00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:17.127
Would he stay or would he quit in that environment?

620
00:24:17.127 --> 00:24:18.100
And of course, many quit.

621
00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:19.833
It was about a 90% attrition rate.

622
00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:24.490
But what he knew at that point is he had that 10% of people

623
00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:27.390
who he knew could make it through

624
00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.120
when things went completely sideways,

625
00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:33.200
when things got so bad that all you had was yourself,

626
00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.660
maybe your teammate.

627
00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.570
And that was the initiation

628
00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.817
of the original Underwater Demolition, UDT.

629
00:24:39.817 --> 00:24:41.080
And of course, that training evolved.

630
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:45.210
And that Hell Week still is the crucible for BUD/S.

631
00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:46.043
It's not the fifth week.

632
00:24:46.043 --> 00:24:47.320
It's at the first.

633
00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:49.247
But what he was doing was he was looking for attributes.

634
00:24:49.247 --> 00:24:51.610
He was looking for guys who could do the job,

635
00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:53.510
not knew how.

636
00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:58.020
Yeah, that difference between how to do it and can do it

637
00:24:58.020 --> 00:24:59.840
is a pretty wide gap, right?

638
00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:00.673
It absolutely is. Yeah.

639
00:25:00.673 --> 00:25:01.785
Yeah.

640
00:25:01.785 --> 00:25:04.030
But within that, lies this great mystery.

641
00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:09.030
Like what is the path from how to could to can, right?

642
00:25:10.297 --> 00:25:11.130
Right.

643
00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:13.760
And how do you like pin that down

644
00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:15.820
and create a structure around it

645
00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:18.600
rather than just, "Well, he's got what it takes."

646
00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:19.740
Like what does that mean?

647
00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.100
Well, I think part of the process is to reverse it.

648
00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:24.125
It needs to go from could to how.

649
00:25:24.125 --> 00:25:27.290
So, in other words, if you have the attributes,

650
00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:29.087
so, our whole philosophy became,

651
00:25:29.087 --> 00:25:31.450
"Hey, as long as the guy has the attributes

652
00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:33.710
we're looking for, we can teach them how.

653
00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:35.551
We can teach a guy how to shoot.

654
00:25:35.551 --> 00:25:36.670
We can teach a guy how to skydive."

655
00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.930
Now, competence certainly matters.

656
00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:40.891
So, there needs to be a baseline.

657
00:25:40.891 --> 00:25:43.680
But what I tell people who ask me

658
00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:47.070
and organizations who ask me about hiring people,

659
00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.120
I say, well, first look at the context.

660
00:25:49.120 --> 00:25:50.980
So, what's the team?

661
00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:53.050
Because the set of attributes that you need

662
00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:54.430
to be a Navy SEAL is gonna be different

663
00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:57.240
than the set that you need to be an HR person

664
00:25:57.240 --> 00:25:58.900
or a salesperson.

665
00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.680
So, what's the list of attributes that you think

666
00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.150
that are predominant in this type of environment?

667
00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:05.590
Start with that.

668
00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:06.860
And then if you find some,

669
00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.340
so learnability might be one of those things.

670
00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:10.050
Hey, we need someone with high learnability, right?

671
00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:11.050
If someone has high learnability,

672
00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:13.350
it has high on all four of the mental acuity,

673
00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:15.500
you're gonna be able to teach them pretty much anything

674
00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:16.333
you need to teach them.

675
00:26:16.333 --> 00:26:17.930
They can learn almost any skill

676
00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:19.030
and pick it up pretty quickly.

677
00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:20.155
Right?

678
00:26:20.155 --> 00:26:21.696
So, we began to reverse that process and say,

679
00:26:21.696 --> 00:26:26.696
okay, we're gonna still train because training by the way,

680
00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:27.850
is a great environment

681
00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:29.620
inside of which you can tease out attributes

682
00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:30.790
if you put challenge and stress,

683
00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:31.940
if you implement challenge and stress.

684
00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:34.853
So, you almost get periphery training.

685
00:26:35.695 --> 00:26:36.980
And then the process we were running,

686
00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:40.110
I called it like we're training for the periphery, right?

687
00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:41.710
We're actually, we're assessing attributes,

688
00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:44.670
but at the same time, they're learning critical skills

689
00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:46.590
that they need to know to do this job.

690
00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:49.163
And I think teams and businesses can do the same thing.

691
00:26:49.163 --> 00:26:51.870
It just takes some diligence of thought

692
00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:53.724
and it's subjective to the team

693
00:26:53.724 --> 00:26:56.790
and the individual of whatever you're looking for.

694
00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.600
So, how did you begin the process of deconstructing this

695
00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:02.920
to identify what these attributes are?

696
00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:04.420
Because you've come up with this list

697
00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:05.960
and you go through them in the book,

698
00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:10.270
but you had to arrive at these as being the critical ones.

699
00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:11.170
Absolutely.

700
00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:13.710
Well, this started when I was running training.

701
00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:14.780
So, the first thing I did

702
00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:18.850
was I created basically small groups around the command

703
00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:21.210
of five or 10 guys.

704
00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:24.360
And I said, "Hey, write down, make a list

705
00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:28.960
of what you think the key attributes we're looking for are."

706
00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:30.805
And I try to give a quick explanation

707
00:27:30.805 --> 00:27:32.064
of what an actual attribute was.

708
00:27:32.064 --> 00:27:32.897
What an attribute is.

709
00:27:32.897 --> 00:27:33.730
You're gonna define that.

710
00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:34.563
But inevitably the list is going to come up

711
00:27:34.563 --> 00:27:36.010
with both skills and attributes on it

712
00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:38.640
because they get conflated quite a bit.

713
00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:39.830
But I did that first.

714
00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:41.800
And so, we got all those lists.

715
00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:44.510
We had something like 100 or so things.

716
00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:48.480
And obviously called the skill off, all the skills.

717
00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:49.313
Okay, that doesn't...

718
00:27:49.313 --> 00:27:50.900
And put it aside because we know those.

719
00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:54.030
And then looked where some attributes

720
00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:55.460
were similar to other attributes

721
00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:59.560
and came up with a list at the time of 36 attributes.

722
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:00.867
And that was the list we basically said,

723
00:28:00.867 --> 00:28:02.740
"Okay, this is what we're gonna use."

724
00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:04.157
When I got out of the Navy

725
00:28:04.157 --> 00:28:05.760
and I began to talk to businesses,

726
00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.790
I'm thinking about this more deeply and more openly

727
00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:10.920
outside the genre of just special operations,

728
00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:14.540
I said to myself, "Okay, what are those attributes

729
00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:15.820
we need to start looking at?"

730
00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:18.210
And I began to think about performance.

731
00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:19.553
And this is where I really started.

732
00:28:19.553 --> 00:28:20.386
And this is...

733
00:28:20.386 --> 00:28:22.180
So, Andrew Huberman, great friend of mine.

734
00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:23.013
And I know you know him.

735
00:28:23.013 --> 00:28:23.846
He says a lot. (laughs)

736
00:28:23.846 --> 00:28:25.150
All roads lead to Andrew Huberman.

737
00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.500
Every guest that I have on this podcast

738
00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.537
has some sort of entanglement with Andrew Huberman. (laughs)

739
00:28:29.537 --> 00:28:31.832
So, yeah he's a popular guy, right.

740
00:28:31.832 --> 00:28:32.665
Yeah.

741
00:28:32.665 --> 00:28:33.498
So, he says a lot.

742
00:28:33.498 --> 00:28:34.331
Yeah, he's awesome.

743
00:28:34.331 --> 00:28:35.164
I'm actually staying in his place right now.

744
00:28:35.164 --> 00:28:35.997
Oh, cool.

745
00:28:35.997 --> 00:28:39.500
But he and I met right after I got in the military.

746
00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:40.810
And we were at this peak performance thing

747
00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:42.840
where we were kind of helping think about ways

748
00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:46.713
to help executives and CEOs perform at their peak.

749
00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:48.423
Pretty good.

750
00:28:48.423 --> 00:28:49.397
David Goggins was there.

751
00:28:49.397 --> 00:28:50.571
So, he and I got to visit, right?

752
00:28:50.571 --> 00:28:54.730
So, but Andrew and I kind of synergized

753
00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:56.550
because we both started talking.

754
00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.300
What we realized is that neither of us liked

755
00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:01.690
nor were that interested in peak performance.

756
00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:03.210
And the reason was because,

757
00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:04.483
and it's funny because most people say,

758
00:29:04.483 --> 00:29:08.840
"Oh, you SEALs, you guys are the best peak performance

759
00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:09.673
out there.

760
00:29:09.673 --> 00:29:10.800
I mean, you know the secrets of peak performance."

761
00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.170
And I used to disagree with them.

762
00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:13.750
I didn't know exactly why at the time,

763
00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:15.530
but it didn't feel right.

764
00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:16.860
But as I started talking to Andrew,

765
00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:17.693
he and I kinda figured it out.

766
00:29:17.693 --> 00:29:20.370
And the reason is because peak is an apex.

767
00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.470
It's an apex from which we can only come down.

768
00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:26.330
And peak often in most cases has to be prepared for,

769
00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:29.280
has to be scheduled, has to be routinized.

770
00:29:30.807 --> 00:29:31.880
You have to have a routine together.

771
00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:34.680
And so, the examples of the professional football player

772
00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:39.280
spends his entire week preparing to peak on Sunday

773
00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:40.140
for three hours.

774
00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:41.109
Right.

775
00:29:41.109 --> 00:29:41.942
Or the Olympic athlete spends-

776
00:29:41.942 --> 00:29:42.775
Or the the Olympic athletes. yeah.

777
00:29:42.775 --> 00:29:44.312
Decades getting ready for that one day.

778
00:29:44.312 --> 00:29:45.150
That's exactly right.

779
00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.100
We realized we were interested in optimal performance.

780
00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.810
And optimal performance is something different.

781
00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:51.940
Optimal performances is how can I do the very best I can

782
00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:52.773
in the moment?

783
00:29:52.773 --> 00:29:54.240
Whatever that best looks like,

784
00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.540
what is my best and how can I do it?

785
00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:58.240
Sometimes your best is peak.

786
00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:00.220
Sometimes that's flow states and all that stuff.

787
00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:01.610
Sometimes it's your head down

788
00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.090
and you're taking step-by-step.

789
00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:03.990
And that's all you're doing.

790
00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:05.670
It's minute by minute, it's moment by moment.

791
00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:07.720
And it kinda hit me like when I was freezing

792
00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.820
in the surf zone in SEAL training,

793
00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:11.857
there was nothing peak about my performance.

794
00:30:11.857 --> 00:30:12.690
(laughs) Yeah.

795
00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:13.620
I was doing the best I could

796
