Because you've been trained to be that way.

1319
00:48:29.301 --> 00:48:30.134
Some of it is, a bit of it.

1320
00:48:30.134 --> 00:48:31.040
You're always aware of what's going on around you.

1321
00:48:31.040 --> 00:48:31.873
Yeah. Yeah.

1322
00:48:31.873 --> 00:48:32.706
You're right.

1323
00:48:32.706 --> 00:48:33.539
And some of it is middle,

1324
00:48:33.539 --> 00:48:34.830
but like I look at my son, my eldest son,

1325
00:48:34.830 --> 00:48:36.380
he's kinda like me and he's pretty visual.

1326
00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:37.530
And he notices things.

1327
00:48:37.530 --> 00:48:41.980
He's more aware than say his brother

1328
00:48:41.980 --> 00:48:44.567
who is more like my wife who isn't as.

1329
00:48:44.567 --> 00:48:47.500
They're kind of in their head just looking around.

1330
00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:48.333
So, we see that.

1331
00:48:48.333 --> 00:48:49.370
So, and again, there's no judgment.

1332
00:48:49.370 --> 00:48:50.740
It's really where you stand on these.

1333
00:48:50.740 --> 00:48:54.940
I do maintain that all of us have all of these attributes.

1334
00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:57.710
It's just a matter of what levels of each that we have.

1335
00:48:57.710 --> 00:48:59.940
So, for example, I have a higher level

1336
00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:01.740
of situational awareness, say, than my wife.

1337
00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:03.073
We both have situation awareness.

1338
00:49:03.073 --> 00:49:03.990
It's just the level.

1339
00:49:03.990 --> 00:49:06.120
So, that's the first thing.

1340
00:49:06.120 --> 00:49:06.953
Information coming in.

1341
00:49:06.953 --> 00:49:09.120
Once that information comes in to our frontal lobe,

1342
00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:10.760
now it's time for us to process it.

1343
00:49:10.760 --> 00:49:12.170
And that's compartmentalization.

1344
00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:15.198
Compartmentalization is basically three things happening.

1345
00:49:15.198 --> 00:49:19.110
It's assessment, it's prioritization, and focus.

1346
00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:21.740
So, information is coming in and we do assess

1347
00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:24.460
what of this information that I'm noticing

1348
00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:26.163
is important to my current task.

1349
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:28.960
I joke, I love going to New York City.

1350
00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:30.198
I love to ride the subway.

1351
00:49:30.198 --> 00:49:32.820
It's because the subways are thoroughly confusing to me.

1352
00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:34.420
It's an exercise in all of these

1353
00:49:34.420 --> 00:49:36.150
in my mental acuity attributes for me.

1354
00:49:36.150 --> 00:49:38.107
So, I'm going in the subway and I'm saying to myself,

1355
00:49:38.107 --> 00:49:40.420
"Okay, I need to get to Brooklyn.

1356
00:49:40.420 --> 00:49:43.310
Okay, what about the information coming into me

1357
00:49:45.150 --> 00:49:46.290
is important?"

1358
00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:48.970
Obviously the news stand with the newspaper,

1359
00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:49.803
I can discard that.

1360
00:49:49.803 --> 00:49:50.636
That's not important.

1361
00:49:50.636 --> 00:49:52.540
Even though I noticed it, I don't need to.

1362
00:49:52.540 --> 00:49:53.373
I need to notice,

1363
00:49:53.373 --> 00:49:56.260
I need to say, okay, signs, tracks, things like that.

1364
00:49:56.260 --> 00:49:57.450
Maps.

1365
00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:59.200
Because you're so hypervigilant,

1366
00:49:59.200 --> 00:50:03.330
it's more difficult for you to crowd out

1367
00:50:03.330 --> 00:50:04.470
what's not essential

1368
00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:07.754
to focus on the task at hand, which is getting to Brooklyn.

1369
00:50:07.754 --> 00:50:10.600
That certainly takes training.

1370
00:50:10.600 --> 00:50:11.433
Yes.

1371
00:50:11.433 --> 00:50:14.060
Yeah, like the thing is these things don't function

1372
00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:15.450
or operate in isolation.

1373
00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:17.430
Like if you're so hypervigilant,

1374
00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:20.540
it's probably more difficult for you to focus on one thing

1375
00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:23.820
if you're on a crowded street where there's a lot going on.

1376
00:50:23.820 --> 00:50:24.653
You're absolutely right.

1377
00:50:24.653 --> 00:50:25.730
And this is where too much

1378
00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:27.180
can actually be a little bit of a detriment.

1379
00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:31.050
Because hypervigilance to too much of a degree

1380
00:50:31.050 --> 00:50:32.460
leads to stress.

1381
00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.770
Because you're overwhelmed

1382
00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:38.370
or there's too much your tasks switching too quickly.

1383
00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:40.347
So, yeah, you have to manage it.

1384
00:50:40.347 --> 00:50:42.342
And I think certainly you talk about the training.

1385
00:50:42.342 --> 00:50:46.810
My lifestyle for 20 years trained me pretty well

1386
00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:48.280
to have that.

1387
00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:49.560
Have eyes in the back of your head.

1388
00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:51.090
And know what to focus on.

1389
00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:54.844
In fact, special operators in SEALs

1390
00:50:54.844 --> 00:50:59.470
are in large part to a man, very, very good at this

1391
00:50:59.470 --> 00:51:01.267
because the environment just trains you so well.

1392
00:51:01.267 --> 00:51:03.310
And it's about, okay, what's coming in?

1393
00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:06.783
Okay, now from what's coming in, what do I need to focus on?

1394
00:51:06.783 --> 00:51:08.640
What do I need to assess then prioritize?

1395
00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:10.000
What's the out of these,

1396
00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:13.240
okay, these three things are of importance

1397
00:51:13.240 --> 00:51:14.920
to what I'm trying to do, get to Brooklyn, right?

1398
00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:17.250
What's the most important one of that?

1399
00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:20.097
For me, it might be the signs, the track signs, right?

1400
00:51:20.097 --> 00:51:22.173
So, I'm gonna focus on the track signs.

1401
00:51:23.320 --> 00:51:24.790
I should probably look at the map.

1402
00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:26.130
I don't have to worry about the guy

1403
00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:27.940
who is 15 meters behind me. (laughs)

1404
00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:28.773
That's exactly right.

1405
00:51:28.773 --> 00:51:29.750
Yeah, that's exactly right.

1406
00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:31.704
I can tune that person out.

1407
00:51:31.704 --> 00:51:33.240
Then we get into task switching.

1408
00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:36.250
This is where it gets fun and a little bit of tricky

1409
00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:37.520
because task switching,

1410
00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:40.260
again, I talk about it in the book.

1411
00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:42.020
Multitask is a myth, right?

1412
00:51:42.020 --> 00:51:43.560
We know that.

1413
00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:44.393
Most people, however,

1414
00:51:44.393 --> 00:51:46.810
think they're pretty good at it. (laughs)

1415
00:51:46.810 --> 00:51:50.200
But statistically, what happens is we're not multitasking,

1416
00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:52.166
what we're doing is we're task switching.

1417
00:51:52.166 --> 00:51:54.730
And those people who think they're multitasking,

1418
00:51:54.730 --> 00:51:57.240
are actually task switching very inefficiently.

1419
00:51:57.240 --> 00:51:59.370
And studies have shown when you multitask,

1420
00:51:59.370 --> 00:52:01.970
the more you try to multitask,

1421
00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:03.579
the worse you get at the activity you're trying to do.

1422
00:52:03.579 --> 00:52:04.620
Right. Right.

1423
00:52:04.620 --> 00:52:06.510
And it's because your task switching ineffectively.

1424
00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:11.080
Task switching is basically the ability to switch focus

1425
00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:14.040
from one thing to another efficiently and seamlessly.

1426
00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:17.580
We do this all the time inside fixed contexts

1427
00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:18.790
like driving a car, right?

1428
00:52:18.790 --> 00:52:20.040
When we're driving a car,

1429
00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:21.550
at one moment we're steering,

1430
00:52:21.550 --> 00:52:23.100
the next moment we're putting our foot on the brake,

1431
00:52:23.100 --> 00:52:24.250
the next moment we're putting our blinker on.

1432
00:52:24.250 --> 00:52:27.266
So, we're actually task switching inside that context

1433
00:52:27.266 --> 00:52:29.460
pretty seamlessly.

1434
00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:30.323
We do this in life all the time

1435
00:52:30.323 --> 00:52:32.113
because we drive our car

1436
00:52:32.113 --> 00:52:35.000
to the parking lot of the grocery store.

1437
00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:36.310
As soon as we get out of the car,

1438
00:52:36.310 --> 00:52:37.897
we've just swapped contexts

1439
00:52:37.897 --> 00:52:39.370
and our brain is shifting context

1440
00:52:39.370 --> 00:52:40.350
now we're in a parking lot.

1441
00:52:40.350 --> 00:52:41.750
Okay, then you get to the grocery store,

1442
00:52:41.750 --> 00:52:43.010
grocery store new context.

1443
00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:46.061
So, we're task switching naturally all the time.

1444
00:52:46.061 --> 00:52:50.710
The measure of task switching in an individual

1445
00:52:50.710 --> 00:52:53.617
is your comfort and ability to do that efficiently

1446
00:52:53.617 --> 00:52:56.813
between contexts, especially in times of stress.

1447
00:52:57.800 --> 00:53:00.430
I am pretty good at switching tasks.

1448
00:53:00.430 --> 00:53:02.340
And because of my situation awareness,

1449
00:53:02.340 --> 00:53:04.650
when I switch into a task,

1450
00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:07.130
I still maintain an awareness of the environment

1451
00:53:07.130 --> 00:53:09.480
so that I understand if I need to switch again.

1452
00:53:11.388 --> 00:53:12.900
Where that's a detriment to me

1453
00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:16.450
is sometimes I have trouble focusing deeply on something.

1454
00:53:16.450 --> 00:53:20.130
My wife is incredibly, she's incredible at focusing.

1455
00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:21.280
When she gets into something,

1456
00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:24.900
man, she just she drives, she's awesome.

1457
00:53:24.900 --> 00:53:26.140
Things fall off.

1458
00:53:26.140 --> 00:53:27.790
The things that things on the periphery-

1459
00:53:27.790 --> 00:53:31.450
Less hypervigilance, less situational awareness.

1460
00:53:31.450 --> 00:53:32.322
It goes down, yeah.

1461
00:53:32.322 --> 00:53:35.870
And probably not as good on the task switching.

1462
00:53:35.870 --> 00:53:36.860
That's exactly right. Yeah.

1463
00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:41.580
But super, super productive and powerful

1464
00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:43.278
when it comes to focus.

1465
00:53:43.278 --> 00:53:45.014
Way better than I am.

1466
00:53:45.014 --> 00:53:47.312
So, there are pros and cons to this.

1467
00:53:47.312 --> 00:53:49.830
It all depends on what you're using it for.

1468
00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:50.850
And then of course, there's learnability.

1469
00:53:50.850 --> 00:53:52.980
How much are you processing all of this

1470
00:53:52.980 --> 00:53:54.590
and absorbing it into your system,

1471
00:53:54.590 --> 00:53:56.820
so you're not making the same mistakes again.

1472
00:53:56.820 --> 00:54:00.080
Admittedly, if I'm higher on all of those,

1473
00:54:00.080 --> 00:54:01.525
I'm lowest on learnability.

1474
00:54:01.525 --> 00:54:03.820
And what that means is it takes me awhile to learn things.

1475
00:54:03.820 --> 00:54:05.610
I actually, I repeat mistakes more often

1476
00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:07.080
than I'd like to admit.

1477
00:54:07.080 --> 00:54:09.220
We all know people who they learned something

1478
00:54:09.220 --> 00:54:10.150
in the first time they got it.

1479
00:54:10.150 --> 00:54:11.500
The first time they learned it, they pick it up.

1480
00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:12.400
I mean, they got it right.

1481
00:54:12.400 --> 00:54:13.730
That's high learnability.

1482
00:54:13.730 --> 00:54:17.140
And the fact is neither is better nor worse,

1483
00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:18.976
but understanding where you lie on that.

1484
00:54:18.976 --> 00:54:20.570
For me, especially when I was

1485
00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:22.730
in some of the SEAL training evolutions,

1486
00:54:22.730 --> 00:54:23.670
I would stay late.

1487
00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:24.970
I would repeat things in my head.

1488
00:54:24.970 --> 00:54:27.790
I would walk the hallways of the buildings

1489
00:54:27.790 --> 00:54:29.488
we were practicing clearing

1490
00:54:29.488 --> 00:54:33.030
just to make sure I was hammering home

1491
00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:35.640
some of those things that I knew it was took me while

1492
00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:36.602
to pick up.

1493
00:54:36.602 --> 00:54:40.610
So, lower on learnability is not a bad thing.

1494
00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:42.780
Lower on any of this stuff is not a bad thing.

1495
00:54:42.780 --> 00:54:45.750
It's just, okay, how do I adjust my environment

1496
00:54:45.750 --> 00:54:46.820
so that I can affect that.

1497
00:54:46.820 --> 00:54:47.653
Uh-huh.

1498
00:54:47.653 --> 00:54:49.690
And high enough on self-awareness to understand

1499
00:54:49.690 --> 00:54:50.603
that you needed to do that for yourself, right?

1500
00:54:50.603 --> 00:54:51.700
(laughs) Yes.

1501
00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:54.990
Like what's interesting is there's no value judgements

1502
00:54:54.990 --> 00:54:55.823
on any of these.

1503
00:54:55.823 --> 00:54:57.203
We all come with,

1504
00:54:58.534 --> 00:55:00.239
our toggle switch is different

1505
00:55:00.239 --> 00:55:01.880
for all of these different things

1506
00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:04.540
and everybody has their unique framework.

1507
00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:06.130
But I'm interested in this distinction

1508
00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:11.130
between innate disposition and what is trainable.

1509
00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:14.910
Because when I think of one of these attributes,

1510
00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:17.780
I think, well, if somebody is walking around

1511
00:55:17.780 --> 00:55:20.840
with a certain level of resilience or courage

1512
00:55:20.840 --> 00:55:22.900
or situational awareness,

1513
00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:25.500
perhaps some aspect of that,

1514
00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.210
they just came out of the womb with that,

1515
00:55:27.210 --> 00:55:30.230
but they're also a function of their environment

1516
00:55:30.230 --> 00:55:33.610
and their parents and all of the things

1517
00:55:33.610 --> 00:55:37.230
that happened to you as you age.

1518
00:55:37.230 --> 00:55:40.050
So, that has to be an aspect of it too.

1519
00:55:40.050 --> 00:55:42.210
Like what trauma did you survive?

1520
00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:45.160
Or what was your dad or your mom telling you?

1521
00:55:45.160 --> 00:55:49.400
Like don't those contribute to these baselines?

1522
00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:50.233
100%.

1523
00:55:50.233 --> 00:55:53.060
And so, the great news is we can develop attributes.

1524
00:55:53.060 --> 00:55:55.130
We just can't do it the same way we do a skill.

1525
00:55:55.130 --> 00:55:57.320
We can't, if you're impatient,

1526
00:55:57.320 --> 00:55:59.750
I can't sit down and teach you a class on how to be patient

1527
00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:02.670
or how to be resilient or how to be adaptable.

1528
00:56:02.670 --> 00:56:03.503
It has to be,

1529
00:56:03.503 --> 00:56:05.890
to develop an attribute, it has to be self-directed.

1530
00:56:05.890 --> 00:56:07.660
You have to want to do it.

1531
00:56:07.660 --> 00:56:10.300
And you have to oftentimes make a conscious decision

1532
00:56:10.300 --> 00:56:15.100
to affect that attribute even though your natural tendency

1533
00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:16.520
might be opposite, right?

1534
00:56:16.520 --> 00:56:20.050
We all in 2020 developed attributes

1535
00:56:20.050 --> 00:56:21.570
because the environment required us to.

1536
00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:24.510
We all developed our adaptability in 2020.

1537
00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:25.760
But I mean, there's no one,

1538
00:56:25.760 --> 00:56:27.970
I would imagine there's no one who didn't.

1539
00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:30.480
Adaptability was a high-developed attribute in 2020

1540
00:56:30.480 --> 00:56:33.060
because the environment forced that on us.

1541
00:56:33.060 --> 00:56:35.560
Some of us found it fairly easy.

1542
00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:39.060
Those of us who did, probably started high on adaptability.

1543
00:56:39.060 --> 00:56:40.889
Some of us found it more difficult and hard.

1544
00:56:40.889 --> 00:56:43.590
Those people probably were low on adaptability.

1545
00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.790
But no matter who you were, you developed it.

1546
00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:47.770
You became better at being adaptable.

1547
00:56:47.770 --> 00:56:52.633
And if we are hit with some weird stuff in 2021,

1548
00:56:53.495 --> 00:56:55.840
we are all more prepared to handle it now

1549
00:56:55.840 --> 00:56:57.749
because we've developed our adaptivity.

1550
00:56:57.749 --> 00:57:00.110
And I would say resiliency and a bunch of the others too.

1551
00:57:00.110 --> 00:57:03.300
What is the relationship between adaptability,

1552
00:57:03.300 --> 00:57:05.490
and like what's missing from this list

1553
00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:08.500
in my mind is optimism.

1554
00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:11.593
Like people that have an optimistic disposition,

1555
00:57:12.490 --> 00:57:15.100
are generally more adaptable and resilient.

1556
00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:16.220
Yes. Yes.

1557
00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:17.053
So, you're absolutely right.

1558
00:57:17.053 --> 00:57:20.140
Optimism, so I talk about optimism in self-efficacy.

1559
00:57:20.140 --> 00:57:23.530
I actually add it as a component of self-efficacy.

1560
00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:25.560
And the reason why I add it as just a component

1561
00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:28.973
is because optimism on its own is inert.

1562
00:57:29.950 --> 00:57:32.010
You and I can be optimist all day long.

1563
00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:33.853
Hey, you and I can plant a garden

1564
00:57:33.853 --> 00:57:35.377
and decide just to leave it

1565
00:57:35.377 --> 00:57:36.930
and say, "There are no weeds.

1566
00:57:36.930 --> 00:57:37.800
I shall have a bounty."

1567
00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:38.633
Right?

1568
00:57:38.633 --> 00:57:39.466
And do nothing.

1569
00:57:39.466 --> 00:57:41.240
And we'll be disappointed come springtime, right?

1570
00:57:41.240 --> 00:57:43.080
So, optimism on its own is inert.

1571
00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:46.290
Optimism when paired with attributes

1572
00:57:46.290 --> 00:57:47.610
is actually when it's the most effective.

1573
00:57:47.610 --> 00:57:49.660
I pair it in the book, I pair it with self-efficacy.

1574
00:57:49.660 --> 00:57:53.980
Self-efficacy is a combination of confidence, initiative,

1575
00:57:53.980 --> 00:57:54.813
and optimism.

1576
00:57:54.813 --> 00:57:57.950
So, self-efficacy is I believe I can do it

1577
00:57:57.950 --> 00:57:59.560
and I believe as I move through.

1578
00:57:59.560 --> 00:58:03.180
Even though I don't know how exactly, I'll make it happen.

1579
00:58:03.180 --> 00:58:04.257
That's self-efficacy.

1580
00:58:04.257 --> 00:58:08.500
And so, if you look at those three factors of self-efficacy,

1581
00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:11.790
each one on their own doesn't do much confidence on its own.

1582
00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:13.200
It doesn't do much.

1583
00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:16.070
I grew up and my dad was a private pilot.

1584
00:58:16.070 --> 00:58:18.880
So, he took us flying all the time,

1585
00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:20.070
every weekend almost as we could.

1586
00:58:20.070 --> 00:58:22.360
So, I developed this love for flying, my brother and I.

1587
00:58:22.360 --> 00:58:24.292
And we wanted to be Navy pilots.

1588
00:58:24.292 --> 00:58:26.100
That's what drove us to the Navy.

1589
00:58:26.100 --> 00:58:28.170
And my twin brother actually ended up flying the Harrier

1590
00:58:28.170 --> 00:58:29.620
for the Marine Corps.

1591
00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:30.850
I ended up taking a different turn

1592
00:58:30.850 --> 00:58:32.100
and going to SEAL training.

1593
00:58:32.100 --> 00:58:33.430
I love flying.

1594
00:58:33.430 --> 00:58:34.263
I love it.

1595
00:58:34.263 --> 00:58:36.900
I've been in hundreds of airplanes, hundreds of hours.

1596
00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:38.390
If you put me in an airplane,

1597
00:58:38.390 --> 00:58:39.630
I know I could pretty much fly it.

1598
00:58:39.630 --> 00:58:40.603
I know how to fly.

1599
00:58:42.225 --> 00:58:44.120
I've never gotten my pilot's license.

1600
00:58:44.120 --> 00:58:45.230
I've never flown a plane.

1601
00:58:45.230 --> 00:58:46.063
Right?

1602
00:58:46.063 --> 00:58:48.460
So, confidence in my ability to fly is inert, right?

1603
00:58:48.460 --> 00:58:49.720
Initiative is the next thing.

1604
00:58:49.720 --> 00:58:51.530
You need the ability to take the first step.

1605
00:58:51.530 --> 00:58:52.870
Okay?

1606
00:58:52.870 --> 00:58:54.450
Because if you don't have that, you're not going anywhere.

1607
00:58:54.450 --> 00:58:56.030
Initiative has to have purpose, right?

1608
00:58:56.030 --> 00:58:59.108
Because initiative on its own is frenetic energy.

1609
00:58:59.108 --> 00:59:00.653
You put my,

1610
00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:02.373
well, he's not eight years old,

1611
00:59:02.373 --> 00:59:04.460
put an eight-year-old in the driver's seat of a car,

1612
00:59:04.460 --> 00:59:06.470
that kid is gonna have initiative to push that accelerator.

1613
00:59:06.470 --> 00:59:07.339
Right?

1614
00:59:07.339 --> 00:59:08.172
It will be dangerous if he does.

1615
00:59:08.172 --> 00:59:09.770
So, initiative on its own needs to have direction

1616
00:59:09.770 --> 00:59:11.630
and purpose and then of course, optimism.

1617
00:59:11.630 --> 00:59:12.463
Optimism.

1618
00:59:12.463 --> 00:59:13.730
I talk about optimism in a sense

1619
00:59:13.730 --> 00:59:16.660
that it's tempered with realism.

1620
00:59:16.660 --> 00:59:18.640
Because optimism plus realism

1621
00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:20.440
is actually very, very effective.

1622
00:59:20.440 --> 00:59:25.440
Now, realism is necessary because it keeps you prepared.

1623
00:59:25.700 --> 00:59:27.110
I know I can do this.

1624
00:59:27.110 --> 00:59:29.780
I know I can take this long drive across country by myself.

1625
00:59:29.780 --> 00:59:32.087
My realism says I might need some gas along the way.

1626
00:59:32.087 --> 00:59:34.710
I'm gonna put some cans of gas in the car or whatever.

1627
00:59:34.710 --> 00:59:35.977
Realism helps prepare you.

1628
00:59:35.977 --> 00:59:37.540
You just have to be careful

1629
00:59:37.540 --> 00:59:41.303
with realism tipping into pessimism, right?

1630
00:59:41.303 --> 00:59:44.760
Because if you're too realistic, it tips into pessimism.

1631
00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:48.110
But you've gotta be able to objectively analyze risk.

1632
00:59:48.110 --> 00:59:50.060
You have to, because if you have no realism,

1633
00:59:50.060 --> 00:59:51.240
it tips into arrogance.

1634
00:59:51.240 --> 00:59:52.110
Yeah. Right.

1635
00:59:52.110 --> 00:59:53.750
So, optimism has to be tempered.

1636
00:59:53.750 --> 00:59:56.273
So, I think optimism is an attribute,

1637
00:59:57.650 --> 01:00:00.330
but it's an inert one that you have to pair

1638
01:00:00.330 --> 01:00:01.830
for it to be effective.

1639
01:00:01.830 --> 01:00:02.970
That's what I would make.

1640
01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:04.200
That's certainly what experience is for me. (laughs)

1641
01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:05.610
Yeah. Yeah.

1642
01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:08.610
On the topic of arrogance, the one that jumps out,

1643
01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:10.590
and I'm sure you get asked about this all the time,

1644
01:00:10.590 --> 01:00:12.090
is narcissism.

1645
01:00:12.090 --> 01:00:12.923
I do.

1646
01:00:12.923 --> 01:00:13.910
Yeah, that was probably my-

1647
01:00:13.910 --> 01:00:15.608
Everyone wants to talk about that attribute. (laughs)

1648
01:00:15.608 --> 01:00:16.540
And it was my most fun chapter to write,

1649
01:00:16.540 --> 01:00:17.640
to be honest with you.

1650
01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:23.133
Narcissism, when I thought about what drives people,

1651
01:00:24.220 --> 01:00:27.760
I had to go back to why I became a Navy SEAL

1652
01:00:27.760 --> 01:00:28.807
in the first place.

1653
01:00:28.807 --> 01:00:30.591
And I would maintain,

1654
01:00:30.591 --> 01:00:34.450
if you ask any SEAL why he became a Navy SEAL

1655
01:00:34.450 --> 01:00:36.220
and that guy says, "Because I'm a patriot,"

1656
01:00:36.220 --> 01:00:37.380
that guy is lying to you.

1657
01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:38.600
He's not lying to you,

1658
01:00:38.600 --> 01:00:39.900
he's just not telling you the whole truth.

1659
01:00:39.900 --> 01:00:41.800
Because we're all patriots, right?

1660
01:00:41.800 --> 01:00:43.760
I became a SEAL because I wanted to be a bad-ass.

1661
01:00:43.760 --> 01:00:44.593
I wanted to stand out.

1662
01:00:44.593 --> 01:00:45.700
I wanted to see if I could do it.

1663
01:00:45.700 --> 01:00:47.750
I want it to be special, right?

1664
01:00:47.750 --> 01:00:49.150
That's narcissism talking, right?

1665
01:00:49.150 --> 01:00:51.100
So, narcissism is certainly pejorative word.

1666
01:00:51.100 --> 01:00:51.933
And of course there is-

1667
01:00:51.933 --> 01:00:52.766
Well, it's more than that

1668
01:00:52.766 --> 01:00:55.010
because not only did you want that,

1669
01:00:55.010 --> 01:00:58.210
you had a belief that you could do it.

1670
01:00:58.210 --> 01:00:59.043
I did. I did.

1671
01:00:59.043 --> 01:01:00.721
Or at least enough initiative to sign up for it.

1672
01:01:00.721 --> 01:01:01.554
Initiative, yeah.

1673
01:01:01.554 --> 01:01:02.400
So, it's a combination certainly,

1674
01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:05.370
but where I wanted to explore narcissism

1675
01:01:05.370 --> 01:01:08.670
was this idea that narcissism is a human thing.

1676
01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:10.850
Certainly there's narcissistic personality disorder

1677
01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:15.850
and the DSM-5 big psych bile will describe narcissism.

1678
01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:20.300
They'll say I think it lays out like nine descriptions.

1679
01:01:20.300 --> 01:01:21.470
If you have five or more,

1680
01:01:21.470 --> 01:01:23.420
you have a narcissistic personality disorder.

1681
01:01:23.420 --> 01:01:25.720
I mean, and you read these like, "Oh yeah, that's bad."

1682
01:01:25.720 --> 01:01:26.677
Right?

1683
01:01:26.677 --> 01:01:27.510
But then you read them it's like,

1684
01:01:27.510 --> 01:01:30.220
"Wait a second, I actually have a little bit of that, right?

1685
01:01:30.220 --> 01:01:32.300
Oh, I have a little bit of that too."

1686
01:01:32.300 --> 01:01:33.977
When I started reading that, I said to myself,

1687
01:01:33.977 --> 01:01:37.510
"Well, I think we all have a little bit of narcissism."

1688
01:01:37.510 --> 01:01:40.130
Because all of us at some point want to feel special.

1689
01:01:40.130 --> 01:01:42.760
All of us at some point, wanna stand out, be recognized,

1690
01:01:42.760 --> 01:01:45.620
be noticed, beloved, be adored.

1691
01:01:45.620 --> 01:01:48.010
And so, you dig into it and you're like,

1692
01:01:48.010 --> 01:01:49.650
of course, because the science tells us this.

1693
01:01:49.650 --> 01:01:52.620
When we are infants, getting looked at

1694
01:01:52.620 --> 01:01:54.250
and adored by our parents,

1695
01:01:54.250 --> 01:01:57.610
we are getting hit with doses of serotonin and dopamine

1696
01:01:57.610 --> 01:01:58.443
and oxytocin.

1697
01:01:58.443 --> 01:01:59.720
Those three chemicals.

1698
01:01:59.720 --> 01:02:01.220
Two neuro-transmitters, one hormone,

1699
01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:05.150
but powerful combination of this feeling of safety, love,

1700
01:02:05.150 --> 01:02:07.060
and pleasure.

1701
01:02:07.060 --> 01:02:08.042
Right?

1702
01:02:08.042 --> 01:02:08.875
That's the dopamine.

1703
01:02:08.875 --> 01:02:11.610
Just when we're getting paid attention to as an infant,

1704
01:02:11.610 --> 01:02:12.940
that translates to adulthood.

1705
01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:14.451
We want to feel that, right?

1706
01:02:14.451 --> 01:02:17.960
So, I looked at narcissism, I said to myself,

1707
01:02:17.960 --> 01:02:20.210
and I looked at all of my team guy buddies

1708
01:02:20.210 --> 01:02:24.650
and said if none of us had this innate desire,

1709
01:02:24.650 --> 01:02:26.930
just let's see if I can be a bad-ass,

1710
01:02:26.930 --> 01:02:28.360
none of us would have gone down this path.

1711
01:02:28.360 --> 01:02:29.520
Narcissism was a driver.

1712
01:02:29.520 --> 01:02:31.280
And so, I think the reason why I would talk about

1713
01:02:31.280 --> 01:02:36.131
is because I want people to embrace their humanness.

1714
01:02:36.131 --> 01:02:37.800
We are all human.

1715
01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:40.170
We all have the need for these chemicals.

1716
01:02:40.170 --> 01:02:42.100
So, we all have a little bit of narcissism.

1717
01:02:42.100 --> 01:02:44.870
So, the question is, can you effectively use it?

1718
01:02:44.870 --> 01:02:45.900
Can you capitalize it?

1719
01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:48.410
It comes with risk because too much narcissism,

1720
01:02:48.410 --> 01:02:50.880
it's obviously detrimental, one,

1721
01:02:50.880 --> 01:02:55.550
two, narcissism is invisible to the owner. (laughs)

1722
01:02:55.550 --> 01:02:58.300
It's like a vampire staring in the mirror, right?

1723
01:02:58.300 --> 01:02:59.550
Hard to see in ourselves.

1724
01:02:59.550 --> 01:03:04.550
So, the inoculation to narcissism are relationships,

1725
01:03:05.490 --> 01:03:07.320
loving and trusting relationships with people

1726
01:03:07.320 --> 01:03:09.620
who will let us know if we're getting a little

1727
01:03:10.473 --> 01:03:11.630
ahead of our skis.

1728
01:03:11.630 --> 01:03:13.316
My wife does this for me all the time.

1729
01:03:13.316 --> 01:03:14.300
(laughs) Yeah.

1730
01:03:14.300 --> 01:03:15.850
And it's great.

1731
01:03:15.850 --> 01:03:16.683
And I do it for her, right?

1732
01:03:16.683 --> 01:03:17.933
It's a balanced relationship.

1733
01:03:17.933 --> 01:03:18.766
But those...

1734
01:03:18.766 --> 01:03:21.800
And not only my wife, my friends and things like that.

1735
01:03:21.800 --> 01:03:22.887
Are these people gonna say,

1736
01:03:22.887 --> 01:03:25.410
"Hey, okay dial it back a little bit."

1737
01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:28.980
Because but I tell you what, if you have a desire,

1738
01:03:28.980 --> 01:03:31.200
if you're listening to this and you're maybe,

1739
01:03:31.200 --> 01:03:32.250
I don't care what age you are,

1740
01:03:32.250 --> 01:03:33.883
but you have this goal or desire,

1741
01:03:33.883 --> 01:03:36.740
I wanna be a singer or writer or a poet

1742
01:03:36.740 --> 01:03:38.987
or I wanna be an athlete or you wanna stand out,

1743
01:03:38.987 --> 01:03:41.170
you wanna be recognized for that,

1744
01:03:41.170 --> 01:03:42.200
there's nothing wrong with that.

1745
01:03:42.200 --> 01:03:45.200
Use that as a driver because it works.

1746
01:03:45.200 --> 01:03:46.280
Just don't let it get over you.

1747
01:03:46.280 --> 01:03:47.113
Right.

1748
01:03:47.113 --> 01:03:50.570
So, in going through all of these attributes,

1749
01:03:50.570 --> 01:03:53.700
you can develop an understanding of what they are

1750
01:03:53.700 --> 01:03:58.340
and then determine how you fit into all of that.

1751
01:03:58.340 --> 01:04:00.500
Where you excel, where your weaknesses are.

1752
01:04:00.500 --> 01:04:04.320
There's a self-knowledge component to all of this,

1753
01:04:04.320 --> 01:04:08.400
but let's talk about the like the practical application.

1754
01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:11.590
Like once you kind of understand what your drivers are,

1755
01:04:11.590 --> 01:04:14.370
what your attributes are that are motivating your behaviors,

1756
01:04:14.370 --> 01:04:17.360
where you excel, where you need some work,

1757
01:04:17.360 --> 01:04:22.360
how do we translate this knowledge into forward momentum?

1758
01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:24.183
Yeah. Yeah.

1759
01:04:24.183 --> 01:04:26.187
Well, first understand where you wanna go, right?

1760
01:04:26.187 --> 01:04:28.440
And in what context

1761
01:04:28.440 --> 01:04:31.900
do you want to exemplify these attributes?

1762
01:04:31.900 --> 01:04:35.957
So, parenting and home life, you may say,

1763
01:04:35.957 --> 01:04:37.520
"You know what? I think,"

1764
01:04:37.520 --> 01:04:38.510
I have a teenager, right?

1765
01:04:38.510 --> 01:04:40.240
And I have two teenagers

1766
01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:43.410
and I think I need to develop my empathy a little bit.

1767
01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:44.690
I need to be more empathetic.

1768
01:04:44.690 --> 01:04:45.523
Okay?

1769
01:04:45.523 --> 01:04:46.356
I'm gonna work on that.

1770
01:04:46.356 --> 01:04:47.189
I certainly need,

1771
01:04:47.189 --> 01:04:48.022
I'm a patient person.

1772
01:04:48.022 --> 01:04:50.490
I have to hyper develop my patience with teens.

1773
01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:52.600
So, there's the family context.

1774
01:04:52.600 --> 01:04:54.210
There's the business context.

1775
01:04:54.210 --> 01:04:55.043
I'm in this job.

1776
01:04:55.043 --> 01:04:58.670
What are those attributes that my job requires

1777
01:04:58.670 --> 01:05:00.610
that would allow me to excel.

1778
01:05:00.610 --> 01:05:01.660
Likely if you're in the job,

1779
01:05:01.660 --> 01:05:03.120
you already have those attributes,

1780
01:05:03.120 --> 01:05:05.410
but how do I develop them further?

1781
01:05:05.410 --> 01:05:07.077
Or is there an attribute that I don't have a lot of,

1782
01:05:07.077 --> 01:05:09.290
that I do wanna develop, which again can be done.

1783
01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:11.178
You choose that and you focus on it.

1784
01:05:11.178 --> 01:05:15.890
The key to developing attributes is stress, challenge,

1785
01:05:15.890 --> 01:05:16.820
and uncertainty,

1786
01:05:16.820 --> 01:05:19.203
because when they are hyper developed.

1787
01:05:20.740 --> 01:05:25.320
Patience, you have to place yourself into a situation

1788
01:05:25.320 --> 01:05:27.100
of impatience to develop your patience.

1789
01:05:27.100 --> 01:05:28.160
And again, I talk about patience

1790
01:05:28.160 --> 01:05:29.270
as one of the bi-directional ones.

1791
01:05:29.270 --> 01:05:30.230
So, I keep on using it.

1792
01:05:30.230 --> 01:05:31.177
There's nothing wrong with being impatient.

1793
01:05:31.177 --> 01:05:33.540
But do you not also have to put yourself

1794
01:05:33.540 --> 01:05:35.251
in that high-stress environment

1795
01:05:35.251 --> 01:05:38.310
to really get a grip on just where you fall

1796
01:05:38.310 --> 01:05:41.440
in that pecking order with that attribute?

1797
01:05:41.440 --> 01:05:42.498
Ultimately, yes.

1798
01:05:42.498 --> 01:05:45.230
Because that reveals where you really sit with that.

1799
01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:46.557
That is the purest environment.

1800
01:05:46.557 --> 01:05:48.880
What we've done with the book and on the website

1801
01:05:48.880 --> 01:05:49.713
is we've developed an assessment tool.

1802
01:05:49.713 --> 01:05:50.546
You have this tool.

1803
01:05:50.546 --> 01:05:51.662
Yeah, you've got the tool.

1804
01:05:51.662 --> 01:05:52.495
Yeah, the assessment tool.

1805
01:05:52.495 --> 01:05:53.850
So, what we did is we developed this assessment tool

1806
01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:56.810
for grit, mental acuity, and drive.

1807
01:05:56.810 --> 01:06:00.270
We put together questions and then we pushed it out

1808
01:06:00.270 --> 01:06:02.190
to about a thousand people all around the world

1809
01:06:02.190 --> 01:06:03.480
and got data back.

1810
01:06:03.480 --> 01:06:06.260
And that data basically gave us baselines

1811
01:06:06.260 --> 01:06:08.710
that we could use for someone who comes

1812
01:06:08.710 --> 01:06:09.543
and takes the assessment.

1813
01:06:09.543 --> 01:06:12.150
So, what I say about the assessment

1814
01:06:12.150 --> 01:06:14.000
is if you take this assessment,

1815
01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:15.860
what it'll do is it'll tell you

1816
01:06:15.860 --> 01:06:17.670
where you fall on these attributes

1817
01:06:17.670 --> 01:06:20.640
as compared to other people,

1818
01:06:20.640 --> 01:06:22.080
as compared to a group of a thousand.

1819
01:06:22.080 --> 01:06:25.270
And that number will increase as we get the data.

1820
01:06:25.270 --> 01:06:27.080
That's only a start point.

1821
01:06:27.080 --> 01:06:28.120
Okay?

1822
01:06:28.120 --> 01:06:30.700
Because ultimately you as an individual,

1823
01:06:30.700 --> 01:06:32.790
need to ask yourself some questions

1824
01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:35.180
about how you showed up in uncertainty.

1825
01:06:35.180 --> 01:06:37.183
2020 is a perfect-

1826
01:06:39.550 --> 01:06:40.383
Barometer.

1827
01:06:40.383 --> 01:06:41.740
Barometer yes, and example.

1828
01:06:41.740 --> 01:06:43.100
You can kinda look back and say,

1829
01:06:43.100 --> 01:06:47.260
okay, when I was forced to stay home almost overnight,

1830
01:06:47.260 --> 01:06:50.500
and now I'm teaching my son advanced calculus,

1831
01:06:50.500 --> 01:06:51.850
I'm trying to write this book,

1832
01:06:51.850 --> 01:06:53.010
I don't have enough toilet paper,

1833
01:06:53.010 --> 01:06:54.530
I mean, when that all happened,

1834
01:06:54.530 --> 01:06:58.910
how was I on resilience, adaptability, open-mindedness,

1835
01:06:58.910 --> 01:06:59.940
courage.

1836
01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:00.773
How was I on that?

1837
01:07:00.773 --> 01:07:01.633
How was I on task switching?

1838
01:07:01.633 --> 01:07:03.570
Task switching was a huge one.

1839
01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:04.640
I thought I was pretty good.

1840
01:07:04.640 --> 01:07:07.380
I, in fact, I'm a pretty good task switcher.

1841
01:07:07.380 --> 01:07:11.160
Home context, it was a little bit more difficult.

1842
01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:13.090
Whereas my wife was task switching beautifully

1843
01:07:13.090 --> 01:07:15.970
because she had been at home with small kids

1844
01:07:15.970 --> 01:07:17.840
while I was deployed for months on end.

1845
01:07:17.840 --> 01:07:19.850
Moms are usually,

1846
01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:22.250
well, mom's parents who have small kids

1847
01:07:22.250 --> 01:07:24.230
are usually phenomenal task switchers

1848
01:07:24.230 --> 01:07:25.720
because they're just constantly doing it.

1849
01:07:25.720 --> 01:07:27.983
They're constantly exercising that.

1850
01:07:27.983 --> 01:07:30.260
So, the assessment tool is one way

1851
01:07:30.260 --> 01:07:33.000
to get a a little bit of a snapshot, I call it.

1852
01:07:33.000 --> 01:07:35.700
And then asking yourself some deliberate questions.

1853
01:07:35.700 --> 01:07:38.360
By the way, if I'm showing up a little bit low

1854
01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:41.283
on say open-mindedness, why is that?

1855
01:07:42.410 --> 01:07:43.950
Let me think about some other situations

1856
01:07:43.950 --> 01:07:47.740
where my open-mindedness was challenged.

1857
01:07:47.740 --> 01:07:48.830
Okay?

1858
01:07:48.830 --> 01:07:49.663
Let's see.

1859
01:07:49.663 --> 01:07:53.890
I was at an event and someone came up to me

1860
01:07:53.890 --> 01:07:57.060
and began talking to me about political views

1861
01:07:57.060 --> 01:07:59.780
that were the polar opposite of mine. (laughs)

1862
01:07:59.780 --> 01:08:01.650
What was my open-mindedness at that point?

1863
01:08:01.650 --> 01:08:04.540
Those are ways you can start assessing your own barometer.

1864
01:08:04.540 --> 01:08:05.540
But then the other question is,

1865
01:08:05.540 --> 01:08:08.840
okay, do I need to develop this?

1866
01:08:08.840 --> 01:08:10.290
Do I need to or even want to?

1867
01:08:11.210 --> 01:08:13.880
I always say I talked about the leadership attributes.

1868
01:08:13.880 --> 01:08:17.070
There's some professions that are self-directed.

1869
01:08:17.070 --> 01:08:18.988
You don't need a lot of the leadership attributes

1870
01:08:18.988 --> 01:08:20.590
to be in that profession.

1871
01:08:20.590 --> 01:08:21.430
Now, I would maintain

1872
01:08:21.430 --> 01:08:23.420
that probably other aspects of your life,

1873
01:08:23.420 --> 01:08:25.210
you might be a leader, so you might wanna pay attention.

1874
01:08:25.210 --> 01:08:27.850
However, empathy is a good example.

1875
01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:31.540
The amount of empathy needed for a Navy SEAL,

1876
01:08:31.540 --> 01:08:36.220
the level of empathy is not as much as say a nurse, right?

1877
01:08:36.220 --> 01:08:40.060
So, if you're in the nursing profession,

1878
01:08:40.060 --> 01:08:42.213
you might want a little bit more empathy.

1879
01:08:43.082 --> 01:08:43.915
Right.

1880
01:08:43.915 --> 01:08:45.070
Well, too much empathy for a Navy SEAL

1881
01:08:45.070 --> 01:08:46.210
could be quite a hindrance.

1882
01:08:46.210 --> 01:08:47.043
It could be detrimental.

1883
01:08:47.043 --> 01:08:47.876
Yeah.

1884
01:08:47.876 --> 01:08:48.723
You're absolutely right.

1885
01:08:48.723 --> 01:08:50.820
I always talk about Navy SEAL empathy as a dimmer switch.

1886
01:08:50.820 --> 01:08:53.150
Because again, most of us are family guys.

1887
01:08:53.150 --> 01:08:56.013
And so, I always thought about it

1888
01:08:56.013 --> 01:08:59.610
is that you you dial it up and down as you needed to.

1889
01:08:59.610 --> 01:09:02.200
But there were times you needed to dial it way down.

1890
01:09:02.200 --> 01:09:04.150
Because to have it too high

1891
01:09:05.008 --> 01:09:08.650
or even smidgen too high,

1892
01:09:08.650 --> 01:09:10.190
it will affect you.

1893
01:09:10.190 --> 01:09:11.023
Yeah.

1894
01:09:11.023 --> 01:09:12.890
I would think that the assessment tool,

1895
01:09:12.890 --> 01:09:17.670
like getting a really solid picture of where you lie

1896
01:09:17.670 --> 01:09:19.690
on the spectrum of all of these attributes

1897
01:09:19.690 --> 01:09:23.370
would just be such a powerful tool to somebody

1898
01:09:23.370 --> 01:09:27.300
to help them direct their path into the right.

1899
01:09:27.300 --> 01:09:29.850
Like trying to be the square peg into the square hole.

1900
01:09:29.850 --> 01:09:30.837
Right?

1901
01:09:30.837 --> 01:09:31.670
Yes.

1902
01:09:31.670 --> 01:09:32.503
Like what's the best career path?

1903
01:09:32.503 --> 01:09:33.500
Well, here's what I'm dealing with.

1904
01:09:33.500 --> 01:09:38.030
Like here are the careers that actually require excellence

1905
01:09:38.030 --> 01:09:39.840
in these areas where I'm already excelling

1906
01:09:39.840 --> 01:09:40.860
like for a young person

1907
01:09:40.860 --> 01:09:42.280
who's trying to figure things out, right?

1908
01:09:42.280 --> 01:09:43.113
Yeah. Yeah.

1909
01:09:43.113 --> 01:09:45.417
Like you could sell this to ZipRecruiter

1910
01:09:45.417 --> 01:09:48.940
and it could help them match candidates with the right jobs.

1911
01:09:48.940 --> 01:09:50.950
Like it's a very powerful thing, right?

1912
01:09:50.950 --> 01:09:51.783
Absolutely.

1913
01:09:51.783 --> 01:09:54.440
And I get excited about it because of that.

1914
01:09:54.440 --> 01:09:56.740
And the only thing I would offer is if you're younger

1915
01:09:56.740 --> 01:09:57.573
and you're looking at this,

1916
01:09:57.573 --> 01:09:59.170
just realize you're still-

1917
01:09:59.170 --> 01:10:00.003
Malleable.

1918
01:10:00.003 --> 01:10:01.260
Malleable and you probably

1919
01:10:01.260 --> 01:10:02.253
sell a lot of dormant attributes

1920
01:10:02.253 --> 01:10:04.160
because you haven't had a life experience

1921
01:10:04.160 --> 01:10:06.640
or maybe that has thrown you.

1922
01:10:06.640 --> 01:10:09.910
I mean, I had the tremendous fortune of going to BUD/S

1923
01:10:09.910 --> 01:10:11.920
at 22 years old.

1924
01:10:11.920 --> 01:10:15.720
That was an attribute factory. (laughs)

1925
01:10:15.720 --> 01:10:17.840
I mean, you learn in that six months,

1926
01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:19.990
you learn so much about yourself and your attributes.

1927
01:10:19.990 --> 01:10:23.380
And again, a lot of it was I hadn't processed it

1928
01:10:23.380 --> 01:10:24.610
in a way that I could articulate it.

1929
01:10:24.610 --> 01:10:25.710
But you come at,

1930
01:10:25.710 --> 01:10:27.580
they ask how do you feel after BUD/S?

1931
01:10:27.580 --> 01:10:30.540
I mean, you feel so super confident because you just,

1932
01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:31.373
you didn't...

1933
01:10:31.373 --> 01:10:34.820
And it's not because you learned how to be a precision shot

1934
01:10:34.820 --> 01:10:38.900
or you learned how to do halo jumps, right?

1935
01:10:38.900 --> 01:10:40.010
You don't learn that stuff in BUD/S.

1936
01:10:40.010 --> 01:10:41.830
It's because you just learned

1937
01:10:41.830 --> 01:10:46.830
that in some of the most harsh situations on the planet,

1938
01:10:46.910 --> 01:10:48.410
you made it through.

1939
01:10:48.410 --> 01:10:49.780
And that is powerful stuff.

1940
01:10:49.780 --> 01:10:51.080
And that's really less than an attribute.

1941
01:10:51.080 --> 01:10:54.480
So, the younger people just have to recognize

1942
01:10:54.480 --> 01:10:56.230
you may not think you have an attribute,

1943
01:10:56.230 --> 01:10:58.790
but just understand you may not have been tested yet.

1944
01:10:58.790 --> 01:11:00.593
So, don't dismiss yourself just yet.

1945
01:11:01.731 --> 01:11:03.220
But in some ways,

1946
01:11:03.220 --> 01:11:04.053
and I talk about again in the book,

1947
01:11:04.053 --> 01:11:05.690
it's in some ways our values,

1948
01:11:05.690 --> 01:11:06.840
understanding our values start to point

1949
01:11:06.840 --> 01:11:08.480
to some of our attributes.

1950
01:11:08.480 --> 01:11:10.240
If you value competitiveness,

1951
01:11:10.240 --> 01:11:14.100
then you might be higher on the competitiveness scale.

1952
01:11:14.100 --> 01:11:18.870
If you value humor, you might be higher on the humor scale.

1953
01:11:18.870 --> 01:11:20.110
You probably are.

1954
01:11:20.110 --> 01:11:22.530
And so, those are some clues into that.

1955
01:11:22.530 --> 01:11:27.530
So, as somebody who has been studying this for a long time

1956
01:11:28.890 --> 01:11:31.590
and has come up with this framework,

1957
01:11:31.590 --> 01:11:34.490
how has that impacted

1958
01:11:34.490 --> 01:11:36.560
like when you were a Navy SEAL commander,

1959
01:11:36.560 --> 01:11:38.537
how has that impacted the selection process?

1960
01:11:38.537 --> 01:11:41.900
Like because I feel like BUD/S kind of happened

1961
01:11:41.900 --> 01:11:42.880
the way it happened

1962
01:11:42.880 --> 01:11:45.250
and it's sort of perfect in the way that it is.

1963
01:11:45.250 --> 01:11:46.460
Almost by accident

1964
01:11:46.460 --> 01:11:48.910
or just by running so many people through this,

1965
01:11:48.910 --> 01:11:50.686
it just became what it is.

1966
01:11:50.686 --> 01:11:52.940
And it's probably not that different

1967
01:11:52.940 --> 01:11:54.880
than it was 10 years ago.

1968
01:11:54.880 --> 01:11:55.779
I don't know.

1969
01:11:55.779 --> 01:11:59.730
But I would suspect that now coming

1970
01:11:59.730 --> 01:12:03.400
into such a deep understanding of all of these attributes

1971
01:12:03.400 --> 01:12:07.430
would alter how you look at candidates and screen them.

1972
01:12:07.430 --> 01:12:09.523
So, yes.

1973
01:12:09.523 --> 01:12:11.120
And I'm gonna separate them too,

1974
01:12:11.120 --> 01:12:13.430
because the way we...

1975
01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:15.683
What didn't have to change was the training.

1976
01:12:15.683 --> 01:12:18.490
What did have to change was the way we looked at candidates.

1977
01:12:18.490 --> 01:12:20.900
So, in other words, the training we recognized,

1978
01:12:20.900 --> 01:12:22.860
whether it was BUD/S or the training I was running,

1979
01:12:22.860 --> 01:12:24.380
was really quite good.

1980
01:12:24.380 --> 01:12:26.970
There was not much we wanted to or needed to change

1981
01:12:26.970 --> 01:12:29.420
because it was really quite refined and been going on.

1982
01:12:29.420 --> 01:12:32.010
But it had proven successful for decades.

1983
01:12:32.010 --> 01:12:34.410
All we needed to do was change what we were looking at.

1984
01:12:34.410 --> 01:12:36.740
And what that allowed us to do in that environment at least,

1985
01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:40.670
was to A, begin to understand more effectively

1986
01:12:40.670 --> 01:12:44.320
why guys were faltering and not or being successful,

1987
01:12:44.320 --> 01:12:47.320
but it also allowed us to spot the dark horses early on.

1988
01:12:47.320 --> 01:12:52.320
Guys who may not have shown a lot of technical expertise,

1989
01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:54.140
but they had all the attributes we needed.

1990
01:12:54.140 --> 01:12:54.973
And they were like,

1991
01:12:54.973 --> 01:12:56.690
"Okay, I can teach that guy to shoot okay."

1992
01:12:56.690 --> 01:12:58.410
I mean, yeah, he couldn't hit

1993
01:12:58.410 --> 01:12:59.410
the broad side of a barn right now,

1994
01:12:59.410 --> 01:13:01.010
but I could teach him how to shoot.

1995
01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:01.843
That's easy.

1996
01:13:01.843 --> 01:13:03.460
I could do that in a day.

1997
01:13:03.460 --> 01:13:07.560
And this is where I think organizations, companies fail

1998
01:13:07.560 --> 01:13:10.180
because their hiring processes

1999
01:13:10.180 --> 01:13:13.380
are typically designed around skills.

2000
01:13:13.380 --> 01:13:16.470
Here's the resume, which we all know can be very flowery,

2001
01:13:16.470 --> 01:13:19.010
here's all your stats.

2002
01:13:19.010 --> 01:13:20.690
And then let's do a 30-minute interview

2003
01:13:20.690 --> 01:13:21.523
and see how that goes.

2004
01:13:21.523 --> 01:13:22.356
Right?

2005
01:13:22.356 --> 01:13:23.570
And it's funny because even in interviews,

2006
01:13:23.570 --> 01:13:25.510
I was working,

2007
01:13:25.510 --> 01:13:30.040
while I was doing this, I bumped into a guy who ran

2008
01:13:30.040 --> 01:13:31.070
for one of our agencies,

2009
01:13:31.070 --> 01:13:34.120
ran a program where he'd help people

2010
01:13:34.120 --> 01:13:37.730
develop undercover personalities.

2011
01:13:37.730 --> 01:13:39.154
And he and I were talking about this.

2012
01:13:39.154 --> 01:13:40.310
He's like, "Rich, this is so cool."

2013
01:13:40.310 --> 01:13:41.930
Because one of the things we do

2014
01:13:41.930 --> 01:13:44.270
is when we do these undercover personalities,

2015
01:13:44.270 --> 01:13:47.640
we try to make sure we help someone develop something,

2016
01:13:47.640 --> 01:13:50.500
a persona that's congruent with who they really are.

2017
01:13:50.500 --> 01:13:53.430
Because what we found is that no one,

2018
01:13:53.430 --> 01:13:57.920
even the very, very best actors can pretend

2019
01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:00.550
to be someone else for maybe 30 days

2020
01:14:00.550 --> 01:14:03.290
before they revert back to who they really are.

2021
01:14:03.290 --> 01:14:05.070
And oh, by the way, challenge and uncertainty

2022
01:14:05.070 --> 01:14:06.848
will revert you back almost immediately.

2023
01:14:06.848 --> 01:14:10.370
So, very few of us are really, really good actors.

2024
01:14:10.370 --> 01:14:13.560
So, some us may be able to do it for a couple of days.

2025
01:14:13.560 --> 01:14:16.080
Some of us may be able to do it for eight hours every day

2026
01:14:16.080 --> 01:14:16.913
when you go to work.

2027
01:14:16.913 --> 01:14:18.140
Who knows?

2028
01:14:18.140 --> 01:14:20.060
But someone, almost every one of us

2029
01:14:20.060 --> 01:14:21.940
can do it for a 30-minute interview.

2030
01:14:21.940 --> 01:14:23.885
I mean, that's easy, right?

2031
01:14:23.885 --> 01:14:24.718
Right. Right. Right. Right.

2032
01:14:24.718 --> 01:14:26.520
So, you can pretend to be anybody you wanna be

2033
01:14:26.520 --> 01:14:27.480
for a 30-minute interview,

2034
01:14:27.480 --> 01:14:30.370
which is why interviews aren't really good measure.

2035
01:14:30.370 --> 01:14:33.200
I really am supportive at least in the hiring process

2036
01:14:33.200 --> 01:14:34.975
of probationary periods.

2037
01:14:34.975 --> 01:14:36.970
Now, I hate the word probationary, by the way,

2038
01:14:36.970 --> 01:14:38.087
because it's just there's a pejorative to that too.

2039
01:14:38.087 --> 01:14:39.710
So negative. Right.

2040
01:14:39.710 --> 01:14:42.920
But periods where a new hire can spend some time

2041
01:14:42.920 --> 01:14:45.690
in an organization getting the feel of that,

2042
01:14:45.690 --> 01:14:48.170
they are assessing the attributes of the organization

2043
01:14:48.170 --> 01:14:49.003
they wanna be a part of

2044
01:14:49.003 --> 01:14:49.940
or they think they wanna be a part of

2045
01:14:49.940 --> 01:14:52.010
and the organization is getting a chance to see that person

2046
01:14:52.010 --> 01:14:53.800
in different contexts.

2047
01:14:53.800 --> 01:14:56.970
Because again, some of these attributes have to take time

2048
01:14:56.970 --> 01:14:59.340
and context to accurately assess.

2049
01:14:59.340 --> 01:15:00.910
Integrity is one of those.

2050
01:15:00.910 --> 01:15:02.220
Do the right thing.

2051
01:15:02.220 --> 01:15:03.833
And again, I go into what do the right thing is,

2052
01:15:03.833 --> 01:15:05.020
because it's different.

2053
01:15:05.020 --> 01:15:06.970
It's subjective to whatever group you're in.

2054
01:15:06.970 --> 01:15:10.114
But does this person do the right thing

2055
01:15:10.114 --> 01:15:14.290
in front of people on their own out in town

2056
01:15:14.290 --> 01:15:15.650
when things are going bad.

2057
01:15:15.650 --> 01:15:16.510
So, these are all different.

2058
01:15:16.510 --> 01:15:20.340
And by the way, one fail is not necessarily a measure

2059
01:15:20.340 --> 01:15:21.173
of who we are.

2060
01:15:21.173 --> 01:15:23.970
All of us are guilty of sometimes not doing the right thing

2061
01:15:23.970 --> 01:15:26.790
or lacking some adaptability.

2062
01:15:26.790 --> 01:15:28.170
Any one of these attributes,

2063
01:15:28.170 --> 01:15:30.220
any one of us even if we're high on,

2064
01:15:30.220 --> 01:15:31.787
we can find examples of,

2065
01:15:31.787 --> 01:15:36.110
"Oh, I didn't show up with who I really am."

2066
01:15:36.110 --> 01:15:37.830
So, you have to do it over time

2067
01:15:37.830 --> 01:15:40.980
and in a few different contexts

2068
01:15:40.980 --> 01:15:43.490
to actually get a good accurate feel for it

2069
01:15:43.490 --> 01:15:44.323
Right.

2070
01:15:44.323 --> 01:15:45.470
And above and beyond that,

2071
01:15:45.470 --> 01:15:47.010
we were talking before the podcast

2072
01:15:47.010 --> 01:15:49.670
about what happens so frequently

2073
01:15:49.670 --> 01:15:53.700
is people do find themselves in the right job or position.

2074
01:15:53.700 --> 01:15:54.750
They excel at that.

2075
01:15:54.750 --> 01:15:57.940
And then they get promoted out of that core competency

2076
01:15:57.940 --> 01:16:02.300
into a job where their attributes are a mismatch

2077
01:16:02.300 --> 01:16:04.950
for the expectations.

2078
01:16:04.950 --> 01:16:09.700
This is one of the classic problems of leadership.

2079
01:16:09.700 --> 01:16:11.190
This is true again to leadership

2080
01:16:11.190 --> 01:16:15.130
because so often leadership promotions

2081
01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:18.330
are based on successes in an organization

2082
01:16:18.330 --> 01:16:19.720
that are skills-based.

2083
01:16:19.720 --> 01:16:20.920
Someone does really well

2084
01:16:20.920 --> 01:16:22.110
and they they've been at the company for a while,

2085
01:16:22.110 --> 01:16:24.500
they get promoted into a leadership position.

2086
01:16:24.500 --> 01:16:25.333
And I always say,

2087
01:16:25.333 --> 01:16:28.710
and I've actually since I got out of the Navy,

2088
01:16:28.710 --> 01:16:31.410
I have been working in the kind of the leadership space.

2089
01:16:31.410 --> 01:16:33.633
So, I've really been able to dive into it deeply.

2090
01:16:33.633 --> 01:16:36.330
All you SEAL guys end up being consultants

2091
01:16:36.330 --> 01:16:38.716
trying to get businesses to figure out how to run properly.

2092
01:16:38.716 --> 01:16:40.240
(laughs) Yeah, it seems like that.

2093
01:16:40.240 --> 01:16:44.530
But for me, it was certainly a deliberate jump

2094
01:16:44.530 --> 01:16:46.330
into something I wasn't necessarily comfortable with,

2095
01:16:46.330 --> 01:16:48.510
talking in front of people and teaching classes.

2096
01:16:48.510 --> 01:16:53.060
But it was also a chance for me to again, look internally

2097
01:16:53.060 --> 01:16:54.941
and look back at what I did as a leader

2098
01:16:54.941 --> 01:16:57.750
and say, okay, where did I fail?

2099
01:16:57.750 --> 01:16:59.070
Where do I think it felt good?

2100
01:16:59.070 --> 01:17:02.290
One of the things I've realized and I'll call it a truism

2101
01:17:02.290 --> 01:17:05.890
is that you don't get to call yourself a leader, right?

2102
01:17:05.890 --> 01:17:06.723
It's not...

2103
01:17:06.723 --> 01:17:10.343
That we often conflate being in charge with leader, right?

2104
01:17:11.630 --> 01:17:12.750
Being a leader is...

2105
01:17:12.750 --> 01:17:15.060
Saying I'm a leader is like calling yourself funny, right?

2106
01:17:15.060 --> 01:17:18.480
Unless you're making someone laugh, you're not funny.

2107
01:17:18.480 --> 01:17:20.770
Other people designate you a leader.

2108
01:17:20.770 --> 01:17:22.390
Which means leadership is a behavior.

2109
01:17:22.390 --> 01:17:23.223
It's not a position.

2110
01:17:23.223 --> 01:17:24.127
Okay.

2111
01:17:24.127 --> 01:17:26.290
You can be in charge, but other people will decide

2112
01:17:26.290 --> 01:17:28.480
whether or not you are their leader

2113
01:17:28.480 --> 01:17:30.150
in terms of how you behave towards them.

2114
01:17:30.150 --> 01:17:33.370
And so, we begin to look at leadership

2115
01:17:33.370 --> 01:17:35.210
from a behavior standpoint.

2116
01:17:35.210 --> 01:17:39.890
And this is where a lot of promotional processes fail us

2117
01:17:39.890 --> 01:17:40.961
because promotional,

2118
01:17:40.961 --> 01:17:41.794
because a lot of us say,

2119
01:17:41.794 --> 01:17:44.040
"Okay, if you do this and then this, and then this,

2120
01:17:44.040 --> 01:17:45.170
you will promote to this

2121
01:17:45.170 --> 01:17:46.410
and you'll be in charge of a bunch of people."

2122
01:17:46.410 --> 01:17:47.800
Well, okay.

2123
01:17:47.800 --> 01:17:49.610
Just because you're in charge of a bunch of people

2124
01:17:49.610 --> 01:17:50.980
or just because you did this

2125
01:17:50.980 --> 01:17:53.489
and it doesn't make you qualify necessarily to be in charge.

2126
01:17:53.489 --> 01:17:55.240
You just you're in charge

2127
01:17:55.240 --> 01:17:57.537
because you did what they're now doing really well.

2128
01:17:57.537 --> 01:17:59.084
Right. Right.

2129
01:17:59.084 --> 01:18:00.420
Which at that point, you're at risk with-

2130
01:18:00.420 --> 01:18:01.448
It has nothing to do with the other job.

2131
01:18:01.448 --> 01:18:03.550
Nothing to do with leading people, right?

2132
01:18:03.550 --> 01:18:05.410
And it puts you at risk of micro-managing

2133
01:18:05.410 --> 01:18:06.243
because you're gonna look at it as like,

2134
01:18:06.243 --> 01:18:07.599
"No, no, no, that's not the way to do that."

2135
01:18:07.599 --> 01:18:08.432
Right?

2136
01:18:08.432 --> 01:18:13.140
So, oftentimes people are promoted

2137
01:18:13.140 --> 01:18:15.910
out of what they're so good at

2138
01:18:15.910 --> 01:18:17.480
into maybe a position of leadership.

2139
01:18:17.480 --> 01:18:19.840
And it doesn't mean they shouldn't be there.

2140
01:18:19.840 --> 01:18:21.610
What it means is that if that happens,

2141
01:18:21.610 --> 01:18:22.480
you have to recognize

2142
01:18:22.480 --> 01:18:24.430
that your job has fundamentally changed.

2143
01:18:24.430 --> 01:18:29.290
Now you have people in your span of care.

2144
01:18:29.290 --> 01:18:30.180
You are now,

2145
01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:31.960
well, if you look at the leadership attributes,

2146
01:18:31.960 --> 01:18:35.240
you are accountable for the development, the growth,

2147
01:18:35.240 --> 01:18:38.770
the success of the people in your charge, right?

2148
01:18:38.770 --> 01:18:40.979
And it's your job to behave in a way

2149
01:18:40.979 --> 01:18:42.017
that allows them to say,

2150
01:18:42.017 --> 01:18:44.160
"Okay, yeah, I would follow that guy,

2151
01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:45.960
I would follow that girl anywhere."

2152
01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:46.793
Right.

2153
01:18:46.793 --> 01:18:49.920
And that's a completely different set of attributes

2154
01:18:49.920 --> 01:18:51.010
that are required of you.

2155
01:18:51.010 --> 01:18:51.910
It is. Yeah.

2156
01:18:51.910 --> 01:18:55.340
And I would say, I mean, some of those are transferrable.

2157
01:18:55.340 --> 01:18:58.850
I mean, empathy across a lot of the categories

2158
01:18:58.850 --> 01:18:59.683
could be a good thing.

2159
01:18:59.683 --> 01:19:00.870
Accountability is always good.

2160
01:19:00.870 --> 01:19:04.000
But yeah, if you are a master at something,

2161
01:19:04.000 --> 01:19:07.650
a master at a trade and it perhaps it's a singular activity.

2162
01:19:07.650 --> 01:19:10.860
Maybe I don't know, I'm gonna say a graphic designer.

2163
01:19:10.860 --> 01:19:13.060
I don't know how singular that is, but maybe whatever.

2164
01:19:13.060 --> 01:19:14.760
You're working by yourself, right?

2165
01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:18.170
Some of these leadership attributes aren't as important.

2166
01:19:18.170 --> 01:19:20.910
I mean, authenticity, decisiveness, who knows?

2167
01:19:20.910 --> 01:19:25.910
I mean, accountability probably, but I mean selflessness.

2168
01:19:26.240 --> 01:19:27.073
Selflessness.

2169
01:19:27.073 --> 01:19:29.740
Yeah, I mean, I don't know if that...

2170
01:19:29.740 --> 01:19:31.870
Well, you have a great story about that though.

2171
01:19:31.870 --> 01:19:35.890
The story of upon graduation,

2172
01:19:35.890 --> 01:19:37.280
getting called to run the hill.

2173
01:19:37.280 --> 01:19:38.170
Oh yes. Yeah.

2174
01:19:38.170 --> 01:19:39.003
Oh yeah.

2175
01:19:39.003 --> 01:19:39.941
So, okay.

2176
01:19:39.941 --> 01:19:40.888
That illustrates that I think pretty beautifully.

2177
01:19:40.888 --> 01:19:41.721
Yeah, so it does.

2178
01:19:41.721 --> 01:19:44.950
Yeah, so this was in context of trust.

2179
01:19:44.950 --> 01:19:49.130
And so, I think the behaviors that build trust, by the way,

2180
01:19:49.130 --> 01:19:53.852
are very similar to the behaviors that qualify leadership.

2181
01:19:53.852 --> 01:19:56.840
Because again, trust is not just a feeling.

2182
01:19:56.840 --> 01:19:58.270
I feel like I trust this person.

2183
01:19:58.270 --> 01:19:59.820
It's more than that, right?

2184
01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:00.653
It's actually a belief.

2185
01:20:00.653 --> 01:20:04.147
A feeling is just an emotion that's rationalized,

2186
01:20:06.902 --> 01:20:08.600
a human emotion that's what a feeling is.

2187
01:20:08.600 --> 01:20:10.670
A belief is a human emotion that's been rationalized

2188
01:20:10.670 --> 01:20:11.503
or justified.

2189
01:20:11.503 --> 01:20:15.010
So, we make a belief to trust someone.

2190
01:20:15.010 --> 01:20:16.180
You can't make anybody trust you.

2191
01:20:16.180 --> 01:20:17.270
All you can do is behave in a way

2192
01:20:17.270 --> 01:20:19.260
that allows them to make a decision to trust you.

2193
01:20:19.260 --> 01:20:20.560
So, goes leadership.

2194
01:20:20.560 --> 01:20:22.177
So, when I was studying this-

2195
01:20:22.177 --> 01:20:24.350
So, asking somebody, "You need to trust me,"

2196
01:20:24.350 --> 01:20:25.804
is an ineffective strategy.

2197
01:20:25.804 --> 01:20:26.637
Yeah, that's exactly right.

2198
01:20:26.637 --> 01:20:27.483
Yeah.

2199
01:20:27.483 --> 01:20:28.316
"Trust me," or, "I am your leader."

2200
01:20:28.316 --> 01:20:29.149
Those are just,

2201
01:20:29.149 --> 01:20:30.060
if someone says that, run the other direction, right?

2202
01:20:30.060 --> 01:20:32.033
I mean, because that's not good.

2203
01:20:32.930 --> 01:20:34.140
And if someone says, "Trust me,"

2204
01:20:34.140 --> 01:20:35.583
just ask yourself why they're saying that.

2205
01:20:35.583 --> 01:20:37.810
I mean, a fireman comes in the building,

2206
01:20:37.810 --> 01:20:38.900
you got some trust there, right?

2207
01:20:38.900 --> 01:20:39.733
You can believe that.

2208
01:20:39.733 --> 01:20:43.477
But or I guess a Schwartzenegger shows up and says,

2209
01:20:43.477 --> 01:20:45.090
"Come with me if you wanna live," that's another one.

2210
01:20:45.090 --> 01:20:46.071
Right?

2211
01:20:46.071 --> 01:20:49.110
But okay, so we were studying trust.

2212
01:20:49.110 --> 01:20:50.740
I was working for a company

2213
01:20:50.740 --> 01:20:52.060
called the Chapman &amp; Co Institute.

2214
01:20:52.060 --> 01:20:53.530
So, I still do some work with them.

2215
01:20:53.530 --> 01:20:56.520
Wonderful leadership company out of St. Louis.

2216
01:20:56.520 --> 01:20:58.700
We were studying this thing called trust.

2217
01:20:58.700 --> 01:21:03.487
And I remember the story from my own SEAL training.

2218
01:21:03.487 --> 01:21:05.590
And so, in SEAL training, it's three phases.

2219
01:21:05.590 --> 01:21:06.760
First phase, second phase, third phase.

2220
01:21:06.760 --> 01:21:10.030
First phase is a lot of the heavy, hard stuff.

2221
01:21:10.030 --> 01:21:11.897
Hell Week is in there, things like that.

2222
01:21:11.897 --> 01:21:13.710
And you go to second phase and you're doing dive training.

2223
01:21:13.710 --> 01:21:14.543
A lot of scuba stuff's.

2224
01:21:14.543 --> 01:21:16.370
Still hard, but you're learning how to scuba dive.

2225
01:21:16.370 --> 01:21:20.240
And then third phase is weapons and land nav and demolition.

2226
01:21:20.240 --> 01:21:25.160
And so, for the last five or so weeks of third phase,

2227
01:21:25.160 --> 01:21:27.050
you go out to an island, San Clemente.

2228
01:21:27.050 --> 01:21:29.020
We can see it off the coast here.

2229
01:21:29.020 --> 01:21:31.460
San Clemente Island is about 10 miles long.

2230
01:21:31.460 --> 01:21:32.550
It's about a mile wide.

2231
01:21:32.550 --> 01:21:34.268
It's basically owned by the military.

2232
01:21:34.268 --> 01:21:36.500
It's mostly military operations.

2233
01:21:36.500 --> 01:21:38.220
There's an airstrip on the north end.

2234
01:21:38.220 --> 01:21:40.740
SEAL teams go out there and do a lot of live fire

2235
01:21:40.740 --> 01:21:41.573
or demolition.

2236
01:21:41.573 --> 01:21:43.500
SEAL trainees go out there for the last five weeks

2237
01:21:43.500 --> 01:21:47.023
to do their weaponeering and demolition training.

2238
01:21:48.061 --> 01:21:51.010
And then the joke is or the word

2239
01:21:51.010 --> 01:21:52.540
is when you go out to the island as a student

2240
01:21:52.540 --> 01:21:53.373
is when you're on the island,

2241
01:21:53.373 --> 01:21:54.726
no one can hear you scream, right?

2242
01:21:54.726 --> 01:21:55.559
Because-

2243
01:21:55.559 --> 01:21:56.392
No neighbor complaints.

2244
01:21:56.392 --> 01:21:57.862
(laughs) Yeah, and no one hears your screams.

2245
01:21:57.862 --> 01:21:59.420
And so, instructors can screw with you all they want

2246
01:21:59.420 --> 01:22:00.253
and no one notices.

2247
01:22:00.253 --> 01:22:02.300
And even though, I mean, yeah, it's the last five weeks

2248
01:22:02.300 --> 01:22:05.000
of a six-month program, but you're still in SEAL training.

2249
01:22:05.000 --> 01:22:06.440
So, it's still tough.

2250
01:22:06.440 --> 01:22:08.343
So, there at San Clemente Island,

2251
01:22:09.496 --> 01:22:12.529
they're still requiring you to do certain things

2252
01:22:12.529 --> 01:22:14.210
to just live, right?

2253
01:22:14.210 --> 01:22:17.170
So, you have to do PT, some sort of PT evolution

2254
01:22:17.170 --> 01:22:21.345
physical training before you're allowed to go eat a meal.

2255
01:22:21.345 --> 01:22:24.550
So, there were three different things you could do.

2256
01:22:24.550 --> 01:22:26.030
Ironically, we ate three meals a day, right?

2257
01:22:26.030 --> 01:22:28.160
So, one of them was a rope clump.

2258
01:22:28.160 --> 01:22:31.050
So, right outside the chow hall, there was a 65-foot rope.

2259
01:22:31.050 --> 01:22:33.570
They had those at Coronado too.

2260
01:22:33.570 --> 01:22:35.820
But climb up and down the rope with full gear.

2261
01:22:35.820 --> 01:22:37.445
Okay, that's where you go eat, right?

2262
01:22:37.445 --> 01:22:38.278
That's one.

2263
01:22:38.278 --> 01:22:39.780
The other one was a combination,

2264
01:22:39.780 --> 01:22:40.613
and I'll get this wrong,

2265
01:22:40.613 --> 01:22:45.223
but I'll just estimate was something like 50 pushups,

2266
01:22:46.130 --> 01:22:49.360
50 dips, and then like 10 pull-ups or something.

2267
01:22:49.360 --> 01:22:52.490
And if you do those, then you go and eat.

2268
01:22:52.490 --> 01:22:54.860
The third one was what they called the hill run.

2269
01:22:54.860 --> 01:22:58.210
So, right next to the chow hall in the barracks,

2270
01:22:58.210 --> 01:22:59.043
there was a hill.

2271
01:22:59.043 --> 01:23:00.357
And the only way to describe this hill accurately,

2272
01:23:00.357 --> 01:23:02.030
was it was long, tall, and steep.

2273
01:23:02.030 --> 01:23:02.863
Okay?

2274
01:23:02.863 --> 01:23:05.800
And the idea was you stood at the base of this hill

2275
01:23:05.800 --> 01:23:07.700
and the instructor has a stopwatch

2276
01:23:07.700 --> 01:23:08.900
and he says, "Go."

2277
01:23:08.900 --> 01:23:09.733
He hits the stopwatch.

2278
01:23:09.733 --> 01:23:10.566
You have to run to the top of this hill,

2279
01:23:10.566 --> 01:23:11.770
sprint to the top of this hill.

2280
01:23:11.770 --> 01:23:16.760
It's about I would guess 200, 300 yard sprint

2281
01:23:16.760 --> 01:23:17.750
up to the top of this hill.

2282
01:23:17.750 --> 01:23:19.450
There's like a little concrete monument there.

2283
01:23:19.450 --> 01:23:20.830
You tap the monument and come back down.

2284
01:23:20.830 --> 01:23:23.850
So, you had the time with what you had to go up and down

2285
01:23:23.850 --> 01:23:24.683
decreased every week.

2286
01:23:24.683 --> 01:23:26.680
And if you didn't make your time,

2287
01:23:26.680 --> 01:23:27.513
you didn't get to,

2288
01:23:27.513 --> 01:23:28.346
or they never starve.

2289
01:23:28.346 --> 01:23:30.200
You basically had to go get wet and sandy

2290
01:23:30.200 --> 01:23:31.320
and he had to eat your meal outside.

2291
01:23:31.320 --> 01:23:33.290
Which by that time in SEAL training, you're-

2292
01:23:33.290 --> 01:23:34.123
Wet and sandy.

2293
01:23:34.123 --> 01:23:35.260
You're hydrophobic.

2294
01:23:35.260 --> 01:23:37.350
You're wet and sandy as you go to the surf,

2295
01:23:37.350 --> 01:23:38.530
you jump yourself in the surf,

2296
01:23:38.530 --> 01:23:39.680
you just get soaking wet.

2297
01:23:39.680 --> 01:23:41.790
And then you go roll in the sand afterwards.

2298
01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:43.100
It's called sugar cookie, right?

2299
01:23:43.100 --> 01:23:43.933
Awesome.

2300
01:23:43.933 --> 01:23:44.910
Yeah, totally awesome.

2301
01:23:44.910 --> 01:23:46.204
Sugar cookie. (laughs)

2302
01:23:46.204 --> 01:23:48.370
And so, by that time in BUD/S,

2303
01:23:48.370 --> 01:23:49.770
you are truly hydrophobic, right?

2304
01:23:49.770 --> 01:23:51.460
You're just like, look, I'm gonna do this.

2305
01:23:51.460 --> 01:23:53.456
So, that was the hill run.

2306
01:23:53.456 --> 01:23:56.530
They had a modification for punishments.

2307
01:23:56.530 --> 01:23:57.960
They called it the flight.

2308
01:23:57.960 --> 01:24:01.130
So, the flight was the punishment version of the hill run

2309
01:24:01.130 --> 01:24:03.660
which meant now you're at the base of the hill.

2310
01:24:03.660 --> 01:24:05.700
You have all of your H harness gear.

2311
01:24:05.700 --> 01:24:07.690
So, you have your H harness with all of your ammunition.

2312
01:24:07.690 --> 01:24:10.800
So, that's about 30 pounds of stuff.

2313
01:24:10.800 --> 01:24:11.970
And you're at the base.

2314
01:24:11.970 --> 01:24:15.580
Some instructor had painted a line called the flight line.

2315
01:24:15.580 --> 01:24:17.250
Some other statistical instructor had built

2316
01:24:17.250 --> 01:24:18.450
like this mock control tower

2317
01:24:18.450 --> 01:24:21.102
that he could stand up with his megaphone and yell at you.

2318
01:24:21.102 --> 01:24:24.620
You'd stand at this thing and then you take a moving pallet.

2319
01:24:24.620 --> 01:24:26.410
So, you'd see forklifts movies

2320
01:24:26.410 --> 01:24:28.170
these pallets of goods, right?

2321
01:24:28.170 --> 01:24:29.910
The wooden ones are about 30 pounds.

2322
01:24:29.910 --> 01:24:31.580
They also have metal pallets

2323
01:24:31.580 --> 01:24:33.210
which are about 70 pounds or so.

2324
01:24:33.210 --> 01:24:35.060
We of course had the metal version.

2325
01:24:35.060 --> 01:24:36.500
Only the best for SEAL students, right?

2326
01:24:36.500 --> 01:24:40.310
So, you take a metal pallet and you put it on your back

2327
01:24:40.310 --> 01:24:42.132
and the instructor says, "Go,"

2328
01:24:42.132 --> 01:24:44.153
and you have to sprint up the hill.

2329
01:24:44.153 --> 01:24:45.590
Now, you're not sprinting anymore

2330
01:24:45.590 --> 01:24:48.230
because you're carrying about 120 pounds of stuff.

2331
01:24:48.230 --> 01:24:49.063
Right.

2332
01:24:49.063 --> 01:24:50.060
So, you trudging up this hill,

2333
01:24:50.060 --> 01:24:51.150
you hit the monument, come back.

2334
01:24:51.150 --> 01:24:52.680
That's usually reserved for punishments

2335
01:24:52.680 --> 01:24:55.630
if you screwed up whatever.

2336
01:24:55.630 --> 01:24:57.470
If you were lucky, you didn't have to do a lot of flights

2337
01:24:57.470 --> 01:24:58.850
when you're there.

2338
01:24:58.850 --> 01:25:01.274
Some guys had to do a lot because they were wise asses.

2339
01:25:01.274 --> 01:25:02.340
But that's a flight.

2340
01:25:02.340 --> 01:25:04.652
So, it was my class.

2341
01:25:04.652 --> 01:25:07.780
It was like the day before we were getting ready

2342
01:25:07.780 --> 01:25:12.480
to fly back to San Diego to graduate because we were done.

2343
01:25:12.480 --> 01:25:16.330
And we had started with I think 160 people.

2344
01:25:16.330 --> 01:25:17.270
We were down to 38.

2345
01:25:17.270 --> 01:25:18.103
38 of us left.

2346
01:25:18.103 --> 01:25:19.970
We were in the barracks cleaning up,

2347
01:25:19.970 --> 01:25:20.890
just getting ready to think,

2348
01:25:20.890 --> 01:25:22.270
packing and getting things ready to go.

2349
01:25:22.270 --> 01:25:27.000
Really just on top of the world because we're done.

2350
01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:28.210
And from the outside of the barracks,

2351
01:25:28.210 --> 01:25:32.150
we hear, "Class 210, muster on the flight line."

2352
01:25:32.150 --> 01:25:34.007
And all of us go, "Oh, okay."

2353
01:25:34.007 --> 01:25:38.110
So, we all kinda begrudgingly march out there.

2354
01:25:38.110 --> 01:25:38.943
We're in line.

2355
01:25:38.943 --> 01:25:40.130
The instructor gets on the podium.

2356
01:25:40.130 --> 01:25:42.690
This guy's name, it was Instructor Goodman.

2357
01:25:42.690 --> 01:25:43.523
Right?

2358
01:25:43.523 --> 01:25:45.020
Ironically, he was not a good man.

2359
01:25:45.970 --> 01:25:47.977
But Goodman says, "All right."

2360
01:25:47.977 --> 01:25:52.180
And he said, "What's the fastest hill run

2361
01:25:52.180 --> 01:25:54.030
anybody's done so far?"

2362
01:25:54.030 --> 01:25:55.163
And I think I'm just gonna say it was two minutes

2363
01:25:55.163 --> 01:25:55.996
or something.

2364
01:25:56.897 --> 01:25:59.670
He's like, "Okay, we're gonna do flights

2365
01:25:59.670 --> 01:26:02.230
until someone beats that time."

2366
01:26:02.230 --> 01:26:03.820
Now, just to remind reminder, hill run when you do it,

2367
01:26:03.820 --> 01:26:04.653
you're slick.

2368
01:26:04.653 --> 01:26:05.640
You have nothing.

2369
01:26:05.640 --> 01:26:06.473
You just used sprinting up the hill.

2370
01:26:06.473 --> 01:26:07.322
Yeah, no pallets.

2371
01:26:07.322 --> 01:26:10.053
Flight is HRS pallet, right?

2372
01:26:11.510 --> 01:26:14.480
And so, I am not certain what sound I made

2373
01:26:14.480 --> 01:26:18.340
or face I made at that moment that cued Goodman into me.

2374
01:26:18.340 --> 01:26:20.660
But we were all pissed, right?

2375
01:26:20.660 --> 01:26:24.040
But I must have made a sound because he looks at me

2376
01:26:24.040 --> 01:26:26.740
and said, "Hey, Diviney do you have a problem with that?"

2377
01:26:26.740 --> 01:26:31.250
And so, I'm feeling a little bit snarky I guess.

2378
01:26:31.250 --> 01:26:32.367
I step out of line I say,

2379
01:26:32.367 --> 01:26:33.930
"Yeah, I have a problem with that."

2380
01:26:33.930 --> 01:26:35.973
And he's like, "Why do you have a problem with that?"

2381
01:26:35.973 --> 01:26:36.810
I was like, "Because this is stupid.

2382
01:26:36.810 --> 01:26:40.007
Guys are gonna hurt themselves going up the hill.

2383
01:26:40.007 --> 01:26:41.780
It's a stupid idea."

2384
01:26:41.780 --> 01:26:42.613
Right?

2385
01:26:42.613 --> 01:26:43.870
Now at that moment,

2386
01:26:43.870 --> 01:26:46.756
the rest of my guys in my class are dead silent.

2387
01:26:46.756 --> 01:26:47.589
Okay?

2388
01:26:47.589 --> 01:26:48.422
Yeah, let me just interrupt.

2389
01:26:48.422 --> 01:26:51.340
Like I would imagine it's highly unusual

2390
01:26:51.340 --> 01:26:54.330
to challenge your commanding officer in that way.

2391
01:26:54.330 --> 01:26:55.640
Yeah, or the instructor at that point.

2392
01:26:55.640 --> 01:26:56.900
Yes, I would imagine it is.

2393
01:26:56.900 --> 01:26:57.733
I'm not sure.

2394
01:26:57.733 --> 01:26:58.566
I'm not...

2395
01:26:58.566 --> 01:26:59.652
Luckily, I've only gone through BUD/S once,

2396
01:26:59.652 --> 01:27:00.777
so I don't know. (laughs)

2397
01:27:00.777 --> 01:27:02.600
I don't know if it's happened before.

2398
01:27:02.600 --> 01:27:05.010
Yeah, the consequences could be dire.

2399
01:27:05.010 --> 01:27:06.500
I will tell you this.

2400
01:27:06.500 --> 01:27:07.333
The moment I,

2401
01:27:07.333 --> 01:27:10.300
so my classmates were silent.

2402
01:27:10.300 --> 01:27:12.124
Some of them I saw like we're kinda moving away from me

2403
01:27:12.124 --> 01:27:12.957
a little bit.

2404
01:27:12.957 --> 01:27:13.800
They didn't know what was coming down.

2405
01:27:13.800 --> 01:27:15.290
The words left my lips in a bit,

2406
01:27:15.290 --> 01:27:18.420
I was like, "Okay, what the hell did I just do?"

2407
01:27:18.420 --> 01:27:20.600
Because Goodman was silent for a good,

2408
01:27:20.600 --> 01:27:22.490
well, it seemed like hours.

2409
01:27:22.490 --> 01:27:23.399
So, it's probably-

2410
01:27:23.399 --> 01:27:24.232
You're not leaving the island.

2411
01:27:24.232 --> 01:27:25.320
You're gonna be here a little bit longer. (laughs)

2412
01:27:25.320 --> 01:27:26.227
Yeah, I don't know.

2413
01:27:26.227 --> 01:27:27.570
And so finally he speaks up

2414
01:27:27.570 --> 01:27:32.220
and he says, "All right, since Diviney

2415
01:27:32.220 --> 01:27:33.400
has a problem with this,

2416
01:27:33.400 --> 01:27:35.070
what we're gonna do instead

2417
01:27:35.070 --> 01:27:37.510
is we're gonna run back to the barracks.

2418
01:27:37.510 --> 01:27:38.920
We're gonna go to the auditorium

2419
01:27:38.920 --> 01:27:41.600
and we're gonna watch movies for the rest of the afternoon."

2420
01:27:41.600 --> 01:27:43.130
And so, now all of us are silent

2421
01:27:43.130 --> 01:27:45.260
until someone's like smart enough to start moving

2422
01:27:45.260 --> 01:27:46.093
before he changes his mind.

2423
01:27:46.093 --> 01:27:48.990
So, we start running back to the barracks.

2424
01:27:48.990 --> 01:27:50.620
High-fives, I feel great and all that stuff.

2425
01:27:50.620 --> 01:27:51.850
So, we watch movies throughout the afternoon.

2426
01:27:51.850 --> 01:27:53.080
But the reason why I tell that story

2427
01:27:53.080 --> 01:27:54.210
is not because of that event.

2428
01:27:54.210 --> 01:27:58.340
The reason why I tell that story is because 17 years later,

2429
01:27:58.340 --> 01:28:00.040
I run into two guys from my BUD/S class.

2430
01:28:00.040 --> 01:28:01.340
I hadn't seen them since BUD/S.

2431
01:28:01.340 --> 01:28:03.060
I was pretty much an east coast SEAL,

2432
01:28:03.060 --> 01:28:04.560
which means I was in Virginia Beach,

2433
01:28:04.560 --> 01:28:06.230
west coast to San Diego.

2434
01:28:06.230 --> 01:28:08.090
So, sometimes you just don't see guys.

2435
01:28:08.090 --> 01:28:10.890
And I hadn't seen these guys in that long.

2436
01:28:10.890 --> 01:28:11.910
And we were reminiscing.

2437
01:28:11.910 --> 01:28:12.743
It was great to see them.

2438
01:28:12.743 --> 01:28:13.757
And at one point, one of the guys says,

2439
01:28:13.757 --> 01:28:15.510
"Hey, sir, do you remember that time

2440
01:28:15.510 --> 01:28:18.910
you stood up the Goodman on the flight line?"

2441
01:28:18.910 --> 01:28:21.763
Of course I hadn't thought about it, but of course remember.

2442
01:28:21.763 --> 01:28:22.610
And I said, "Yeah."

2443
01:28:22.610 --> 01:28:23.937
Like I like both of them at the points of serve,

2444
01:28:23.937 --> 01:28:25.160
"Man, we'd follow you anywhere.

2445
01:28:25.160 --> 01:28:27.280
We trust you anywhere, anytime."

2446
01:28:27.280 --> 01:28:28.870
And obviously that's nice to hear.

2447
01:28:28.870 --> 01:28:31.240
And these guys are good friends, right?

2448
01:28:31.240 --> 01:28:34.230
But I thought about why does that still exist?

2449
01:28:34.230 --> 01:28:36.900
Well, how does that trust still exist?

2450
01:28:36.900 --> 01:28:38.420
Right?

2451
01:28:38.420 --> 01:28:41.073
After all those years, I mean, they didn't necessarily know,

2452
01:28:41.073 --> 01:28:42.460
I mean, I was still a SEAL.

2453
01:28:42.460 --> 01:28:44.870
So, obviously I was obviously still active,

2454
01:28:44.870 --> 01:28:46.520
but they hadn't served with me.

2455
01:28:46.520 --> 01:28:47.880
So, why does that still exist?

2456
01:28:47.880 --> 01:28:50.230
And what I realized is it came down to these attributes.

2457
01:28:50.230 --> 01:28:53.290
It came down to the fact that I was selfless.

2458
01:28:53.290 --> 01:28:56.300
It came down to the fact that I cared about them.

2459
01:28:56.300 --> 01:29:01.300
The competency of my studentness

2460
01:29:01.390 --> 01:29:03.390
or even my SEALness up to that point

2461
01:29:03.390 --> 01:29:06.400
had nothing to do with why they were saying that.

2462
01:29:06.400 --> 01:29:09.960
What stuck with them over time for me,

2463
01:29:09.960 --> 01:29:12.700
in their minds, as a leader in their minds

2464
01:29:12.700 --> 01:29:14.970
was the selflessness, was the authenticity,

2465
01:29:14.970 --> 01:29:17.390
was the fact that I had integrity in that moment.

2466
01:29:17.390 --> 01:29:18.360
Right?

2467
01:29:18.360 --> 01:29:19.193
That's what stuck with them.

2468
01:29:19.193 --> 01:29:20.280
The attributes stuck with them.

2469
01:29:20.280 --> 01:29:21.113
Right.

2470
01:29:21.113 --> 01:29:22.140
And I think that was really the lesson.

2471
01:29:22.140 --> 01:29:22.973
Right.

2472
01:29:22.973 --> 01:29:25.490
Like the attribute being your first instinct

2473
01:29:25.490 --> 01:29:29.020
was to think about the welfare of the other guys.

2474
01:29:29.020 --> 01:29:31.480
As it would have been a very different scenario

2475
01:29:31.480 --> 01:29:34.040
if you said, "This is stupid because I'm gonna get injured."

2476
01:29:34.040 --> 01:29:34.873
That's true.

2477
01:29:34.873 --> 01:29:35.874
Yeah.

2478
01:29:35.874 --> 01:29:36.856
That would've been stupid to say. (laughs)

2479
01:29:36.856 --> 01:29:37.862
Admittedly, yeah.

2480
01:29:37.862 --> 01:29:39.586
Yeah, and you would have ended up running a lot of hills

2481
01:29:39.586 --> 01:29:40.419
of disrespect. (laughs)

2482
01:29:40.419 --> 01:29:42.500
Yeah, I would imagine I would.

2483
01:29:42.500 --> 01:29:43.333
Yeah.

2484
01:29:43.333 --> 01:29:44.166
But here's the thing.

2485
01:29:44.166 --> 01:29:46.687
People ask me all the time when I tell that story,

2486
01:29:46.687 --> 01:29:48.790
"Hey, do you think Goodman had planned that?"

2487
01:29:48.790 --> 01:29:49.623
Right?

2488
01:29:49.623 --> 01:29:51.100
And what I tell them is this,

2489
01:29:51.100 --> 01:29:52.450
Goodman was actually...

2490
01:29:52.450 --> 01:29:54.270
It's funny, because of BUD/S instructors are like

2491
01:29:54.270 --> 01:29:55.850
they feel like Satan when you're in BUD/S.

2492
01:29:55.850 --> 01:29:56.845
Right?

2493
01:29:56.845 --> 01:29:58.070
But then BUD/S ends and they're like the nicest dudes.

2494
01:29:58.070 --> 01:29:58.970
Because they're just doing a job.

2495
01:29:58.970 --> 01:29:59.970
They're really just pushing you.

2496
01:29:59.970 --> 01:30:01.970
Goodman, wonderful dude.

2497
01:30:01.970 --> 01:30:02.803
I haven't seen him since.

2498
01:30:02.803 --> 01:30:04.550
I don't know where he is or who's doing well,

2499
01:30:04.550 --> 01:30:05.583
but wonderful guy.

2500
01:30:06.658 --> 01:30:10.260
The fact is, I don't know if he had planned.

2501
01:30:10.260 --> 01:30:11.540
I don't know if he'd ever done it before.

2502
01:30:11.540 --> 01:30:15.000
What I do know is that he probably didn't expect

2503
01:30:15.000 --> 01:30:16.130
that response.

2504
01:30:16.130 --> 01:30:18.210
And what I did notice after that

2505
01:30:18.210 --> 01:30:21.180
was the instructors treated me distinctly differently.

2506
01:30:21.180 --> 01:30:24.910
And I believe it's because they saw someone

2507
01:30:24.910 --> 01:30:28.210
who would step out of line if I needed to, even at risk.

2508
01:30:28.210 --> 01:30:29.043
That's selflessness.

2509
01:30:29.043 --> 01:30:32.010
So, selflessness I define it's more than just altruism.

2510
01:30:32.010 --> 01:30:33.440
It's more than just generosity.

2511
01:30:33.440 --> 01:30:35.500
Selflessness involves a risk.

2512
01:30:35.500 --> 01:30:38.773
It involves a cost to the person who's being selfless.

2513
01:30:40.013 --> 01:30:43.060
But you have to calibrate that against chain of command,

2514
01:30:43.060 --> 01:30:45.460
like your allegiance to chain of command, right?

2515
01:30:45.460 --> 01:30:46.293
Right. You do.

2516
01:30:46.293 --> 01:30:49.730
And this is where attributes come in very well.

2517
01:30:49.730 --> 01:30:51.660
They're extremely important

2518
01:30:51.660 --> 01:30:52.920
when you're assessing leadership.

2519
01:30:52.920 --> 01:30:56.550
Because leaders in any organization,

2520
01:30:56.550 --> 01:31:00.210
military specifically have to understand the balance

2521
01:31:00.210 --> 01:31:03.433
between executing the mission as directed and commanded,

2522
01:31:04.460 --> 01:31:07.950
and keeping the welfare of the people in their span of care

2523
01:31:07.950 --> 01:31:08.783
in mind.

2524
01:31:08.783 --> 01:31:09.616
There has to be a balance.

2525
01:31:09.616 --> 01:31:11.520
Unfortunately, the military mission means

2526
01:31:11.520 --> 01:31:13.490
that sometimes one,

2527
01:31:13.490 --> 01:31:17.580
the mission becomes predominant over care.

2528
01:31:17.580 --> 01:31:20.210
But every spec operator, every SEAL note,

2529
01:31:20.210 --> 01:31:21.120
we all sign up for that.

2530
01:31:21.120 --> 01:31:22.222
So, that's not...

2531
01:31:22.222 --> 01:31:23.398
I mean, we go risk our lives.

2532
01:31:23.398 --> 01:31:24.231
That's the thing.

2533
01:31:24.231 --> 01:31:26.300
But let's just put it in a regular business sense.

2534
01:31:26.300 --> 01:31:28.670
Sometimes you will be told to do something

2535
01:31:28.670 --> 01:31:31.110
that's just a bad idea or outright wrong.

2536
01:31:31.110 --> 01:31:31.960
The question is,

2537
01:31:31.960 --> 01:31:33.830
or bad for the people in your span of care.

2538
01:31:33.830 --> 01:31:38.207
The question is, do you have the attributes?

2539
01:31:38.207 --> 01:31:39.860
And I would count integrity in there.

2540
01:31:39.860 --> 01:31:41.790
I would count accountability.

2541
01:31:41.790 --> 01:31:43.450
I would count authenticity.

2542
01:31:43.450 --> 01:31:44.610
I would count selflessness.

2543
01:31:44.610 --> 01:31:46.540
Do you have those?

2544
01:31:46.540 --> 01:31:49.180
The attributes enough so that you can stand up

2545
01:31:49.180 --> 01:31:52.570
when you need to be stood up to the leaders.

2546
01:31:52.570 --> 01:31:55.970
But sometimes it's about also the leaders

2547
01:31:55.970 --> 01:31:58.340
or the stuff coming down is actually the right stuff.

2548
01:31:58.340 --> 01:32:00.470
And it's the people who you're in charge of

2549
01:32:00.470 --> 01:32:01.370
who are complaining.

2550
01:32:01.370 --> 01:32:02.700
Do you have the same attributes?

2551
01:32:02.700 --> 01:32:04.870
Just say, "Hey, guys, no, this is the way it is.

2552
01:32:04.870 --> 01:32:06.050
We're doing this."

2553
01:32:06.050 --> 01:32:11.050
Are you steadfast in conducting that mission, right?

2554
01:32:11.323 --> 01:32:13.520
Because that has to happen too.

2555
01:32:13.520 --> 01:32:15.270
Leadership is tough.

2556
01:32:15.270 --> 01:32:16.583
True leadership is tough.

2557
01:32:17.820 --> 01:32:18.690
No one said it was easy.

2558
01:32:18.690 --> 01:32:19.523
That's why it's hard.

2559
01:32:19.523 --> 01:32:20.498
Right?

2560
01:32:20.498 --> 01:32:21.473
So, but if we think about it,

2561
01:32:21.473 --> 01:32:26.473
if I just ask your listeners to think about a great leader,

2562
01:32:26.580 --> 01:32:29.150
and it could be someone they don't know,

2563
01:32:29.150 --> 01:32:30.300
it could be someone in history,

2564
01:32:30.300 --> 01:32:31.650
but even someone in their own lives.

2565
01:32:31.650 --> 01:32:34.450
Someone in their own lives they consider a leader.

2566
01:32:34.450 --> 01:32:35.283
Right?

2567
01:32:35.283 --> 01:32:36.217
Ask yourself to put,

2568
01:32:37.068 --> 01:32:37.991
I would ask them to-

2569
01:32:37.991 --> 01:32:39.523
What are the attributes to care for that person?

2570
01:32:39.523 --> 01:32:40.410
Yeah, to think about that person.

2571
01:32:40.410 --> 01:32:41.340
Now think about the attributes

2572
01:32:41.340 --> 01:32:42.830
that characterize that person, right?

2573
01:32:42.830 --> 01:32:43.860
It's those attributes.

2574
01:32:43.860 --> 01:32:47.900
It's authenticity, it's decisiveness, it's empathy,

2575
01:32:47.900 --> 01:32:49.960
it's accountability.

2576
01:32:49.960 --> 01:32:51.887
It's all those things.

2577
01:32:51.887 --> 01:32:52.790
And those are behaviors.

2578
01:32:52.790 --> 01:32:54.250
Those aren't skills.

2579
01:32:54.250 --> 01:32:55.380
I always give the example of my dad.

2580
01:32:55.380 --> 01:32:57.315
My dad was a lawyer for 50.

2581
01:32:57.315 --> 01:32:59.023
He still practices law, right?

2582
01:33:00.440 --> 01:33:01.440
He doesn't know much about plumbing.

2583
01:33:01.440 --> 01:33:02.950
It's still didn't stop me from calling him

2584
01:33:02.950 --> 01:33:03.950
when I bought my first house

2585
01:33:03.950 --> 01:33:05.280
and I had troubles with my pipe.

2586
01:33:05.280 --> 01:33:06.480
So, I was like, "Hey, dad, my plumbing is off."

2587
01:33:06.480 --> 01:33:07.820
Well, dad doesn't know it all.

2588
01:33:07.820 --> 01:33:09.790
I just know dad is always gonna be there.

2589
01:33:09.790 --> 01:33:10.623
He's gonna listen.

2590
01:33:10.623 --> 01:33:11.560
He's gonna help me solve the problem.

2591
01:33:11.560 --> 01:33:12.794
He's gonna be a leader.

2592
01:33:12.794 --> 01:33:13.660
And that's what leaders do.

2593
01:33:13.660 --> 01:33:14.620
Yeah.

2594
01:33:14.620 --> 01:33:18.804
The other characteristic of the story you just told

2595
01:33:18.804 --> 01:33:21.000
is vulnerability.

2596
01:33:21.000 --> 01:33:24.410
Like you put yourself in a vulnerable situation

2597
01:33:24.410 --> 01:33:25.243
by doing that.

2598
01:33:25.243 --> 01:33:28.770
So, how does vulnerability play into the equation?

2599
01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:32.900
Like on the attributes scale or in the context

2600
01:33:32.900 --> 01:33:35.010
of being an effective leader?

2601
01:33:35.010 --> 01:33:37.620
Yeah, it does in both cases.

2602
01:33:37.620 --> 01:33:40.490
Although I put vulnerability in the team ability category,

2603
01:33:40.490 --> 01:33:41.323
it's not an attribute

2604
01:33:41.323 --> 01:33:43.463
because I actually lumped vulnerability into humility.

2605
01:33:43.463 --> 01:33:45.800
I think humility is vulnerability.

2606
01:33:45.800 --> 01:33:46.633
That's what it is.

2607
01:33:46.633 --> 01:33:48.208
It's vulnerability expressed.

2608
01:33:48.208 --> 01:33:49.966
Hmm, I never thought about it that way.

2609
01:33:49.966 --> 01:33:51.800
Vulnerability is incredibly important.

2610
01:33:51.800 --> 01:33:55.800
And it's because in any team, in any high-performing team,

2611
01:33:55.800 --> 01:33:58.160
what has to happen is something

2612
01:33:58.160 --> 01:34:00.000
that I call dynamic subordination.

2613
01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:01.730
So, dynamic subordination is this concept

2614
01:34:01.730 --> 01:34:04.365
where actually the story behind that

2615
01:34:04.365 --> 01:34:07.880
was a bunch of CEOs had asked me

2616
01:34:07.880 --> 01:34:10.550
to draw the task organization for a high-performing team.

2617
01:34:10.550 --> 01:34:12.210
Hey, what does that look like in a SEAL team?

2618
01:34:12.210 --> 01:34:13.850
And I had some options,

2619
01:34:13.850 --> 01:34:16.580
but like the pyramid that we all know didn't make sense.

2620
01:34:16.580 --> 01:34:18.090
That's I'm in charge.

2621
01:34:18.090 --> 01:34:18.980
You do what I say, right?

2622
01:34:18.980 --> 01:34:20.090
That's the classic.

2623
01:34:20.090 --> 01:34:21.170
Basically how every business

2624
01:34:21.170 --> 01:34:22.760
and the military is structured, right?

2625
01:34:22.760 --> 01:34:25.290
Then you have the flat model which is like became popular.

2626
01:34:25.290 --> 01:34:27.206
Like hey, we're decentralizing everything.

2627
01:34:27.206 --> 01:34:28.570
Everybody's equal.

2628
01:34:28.570 --> 01:34:30.090
We all make decisions.

2629
01:34:30.090 --> 01:34:31.850
But even then what happens is silos.

2630
01:34:31.850 --> 01:34:33.500
Because if you have a flat line,

2631
01:34:33.500 --> 01:34:35.260
the right end of that line makes a decision.

2632
01:34:35.260 --> 01:34:37.770
The left end doesn't maybe know what's going on.

2633
01:34:37.770 --> 01:34:39.410
So, that doesn't happen on a performing team.

2634
01:34:39.410 --> 01:34:40.930
Then of course you have the upside-down pyramid,

2635
01:34:40.930 --> 01:34:41.763
which is great.

2636
01:34:41.763 --> 01:34:43.877
It's kind of the Greenfield's philosophical models.

2637
01:34:43.877 --> 01:34:44.710
"Hey, I'm your leader.

2638
01:34:44.710 --> 01:34:45.860
I work for you."

2639
01:34:45.860 --> 01:34:48.500
Cool, but it puts a lot of burden on that leader, right?

2640
01:34:48.500 --> 01:34:49.840
So, actually largely in frustration,

2641
01:34:49.840 --> 01:34:52.334
I drew a blob on the whiteboard.

2642
01:34:52.334 --> 01:34:55.930
And I said, "Where do you think the leader sits in here?"

2643
01:34:55.930 --> 01:35:00.470
And I got answers like left side, right side, middle.

2644
01:35:00.470 --> 01:35:02.820
And basically said, "No, all of you are right."

2645
01:35:03.920 --> 01:35:06.110
The leader is wherever the leader needs to be in the moment.

2646
01:35:06.110 --> 01:35:06.943
Right?

2647
01:35:06.943 --> 01:35:08.110
So, in dynamics subornation what that means

2648
01:35:08.110 --> 01:35:10.160
is high-performing teams understand

2649
01:35:10.160 --> 01:35:12.270
that challenge, uncertainty, and problems

2650
01:35:12.270 --> 01:35:14.480
can come from any angle at any moment.

2651
01:35:14.480 --> 01:35:18.660
And in that moment, the person who is the most capable

2652
01:35:18.660 --> 01:35:21.260
and competent and closest,

2653
01:35:21.260 --> 01:35:23.530
steps up and takes charge, and everybody follows.

2654
01:35:23.530 --> 01:35:26.683
It's a dynamic swap between leader and follower.

2655
01:35:28.240 --> 01:35:29.830
It happens as the environment changes.

2656
01:35:29.830 --> 01:35:31.440
Because once the environment changes again,

2657
01:35:31.440 --> 01:35:32.273
then someone...

2658
01:35:32.273 --> 01:35:37.273
This was so apparent in the SEAL teams.

2659
01:35:37.300 --> 01:35:38.133
So apparent.

2660
01:35:38.133 --> 01:35:41.020
I mean, it was incredible to work with guys,

2661
01:35:41.020 --> 01:35:43.661
especially when you're working with guys and you just know,

2662
01:35:43.661 --> 01:35:45.400
I mean, you know them, you know their silhouettes.

2663
01:35:45.400 --> 01:35:46.620
That's how well you know them.

2664
01:35:46.620 --> 01:35:49.680
And things happen and solutions just...

2665
01:35:49.680 --> 01:35:50.880
People are just attacking the problem.

2666
01:35:50.880 --> 01:35:52.930
I mean, suddenly it's my record guy who needs,

2667
01:35:52.930 --> 01:35:54.510
we're all, okay, what does he need?

2668
01:35:54.510 --> 01:35:55.430
Suddenly it's the assaulter.

2669
01:35:55.430 --> 01:35:56.263
Suddenly it's me.

2670
01:35:56.263 --> 01:35:58.610
I have to coordinate whatever.

2671
01:35:58.610 --> 01:36:01.660
The rapid swamping was so apparent.

2672
01:36:01.660 --> 01:36:04.560
But I'll just give everybody a real world example of this.

2673
01:36:05.660 --> 01:36:07.810
We all know in a commercial airliner

2674
01:36:07.810 --> 01:36:10.440
that the captain of that airplane is in charge.

2675
01:36:10.440 --> 01:36:12.770
There's no debate that that captain

2676
01:36:12.770 --> 01:36:14.310
is in charge of aircraft.

2677
01:36:14.310 --> 01:36:16.710
If on taxi out to the runway,

2678
01:36:16.710 --> 01:36:18.990
that captain gets called by the maintenance officer

2679
01:36:18.990 --> 01:36:19.823
and the maintenance officer says,

2680
01:36:19.823 --> 01:36:20.900
"Hey, there's a problem with your aircraft.

2681
01:36:20.900 --> 01:36:22.230
You have to turn around."

2682
01:36:22.230 --> 01:36:25.100
No captain worth their wings is gonna ignore that.

2683
01:36:25.100 --> 01:36:26.790
That captain will immediately subordinate

2684
01:36:26.790 --> 01:36:28.860
to that maintenance officer and turn that aircraft around.

2685
01:36:28.860 --> 01:36:29.693
Okay?

2686
01:36:29.693 --> 01:36:31.490
Aircraft turns around and gets back to the gate.

2687
01:36:31.490 --> 01:36:32.560
Now they have to deep-plane.

2688
01:36:32.560 --> 01:36:34.510
Captain doesn't take charge of deep-planing either.

2689
01:36:34.510 --> 01:36:36.040
Now, the flight attendant's in charge.

2690
01:36:36.040 --> 01:36:36.873
Right?

2691
01:36:36.873 --> 01:36:39.960
And so, this is an example of dynamic subordination.

2692
01:36:39.960 --> 01:36:41.720
It actually, in high-performing teams,

2693
01:36:41.720 --> 01:36:43.650
in very effective teams, that happens all the time.

2694
01:36:43.650 --> 01:36:44.730
What does that take?

2695
01:36:44.730 --> 01:36:46.020
That takes vulnerability.

2696
01:36:46.020 --> 01:36:48.740
Vulnerability though, is not just the stigma

2697
01:36:48.740 --> 01:36:49.960
of showing your weaknesses.

2698
01:36:49.960 --> 01:36:53.450
Vulnerability is showing your weaknesses and your strengths.

2699
01:36:53.450 --> 01:36:55.270
Because teammates need to understand

2700
01:36:55.270 --> 01:36:57.360
where they can lean on you

2701
01:36:57.360 --> 01:36:58.740
because they are your strengths,

2702
01:36:58.740 --> 01:37:01.204
and where you're gonna be leaning on them.

2703
01:37:01.204 --> 01:37:04.570
So, vulnerability works in the team aspect.

2704
01:37:04.570 --> 01:37:06.330
And then as a leader,

2705
01:37:06.330 --> 01:37:09.340
vulnerability works because it shows people

2706
01:37:09.340 --> 01:37:11.390
who are in your span of care,

2707
01:37:11.390 --> 01:37:12.350
that you don't know it all.

2708
01:37:12.350 --> 01:37:13.440
It shows humility.

2709
01:37:13.440 --> 01:37:14.417
It shows that they're needed.

2710
01:37:14.417 --> 01:37:17.900
I remember, I mean, another thing I love about the teams

2711
01:37:17.900 --> 01:37:20.470
is everybody just understood their jobs

2712
01:37:20.470 --> 01:37:21.630
and everybody stepped up.

2713
01:37:21.630 --> 01:37:26.030
And this idea like I needed,

2714
01:37:26.030 --> 01:37:28.586
I can't do what the sniper does.

2715
01:37:28.586 --> 01:37:29.941
I can't do what the assaulter does.

2716
01:37:29.941 --> 01:37:31.490
I can't do what the breacher does.

2717
01:37:31.490 --> 01:37:33.230
I need them.

2718
01:37:33.230 --> 01:37:36.000
They need me to be able to know what I do.

2719
01:37:36.000 --> 01:37:37.020
Vulnerability helps-

2720
01:37:37.020 --> 01:37:38.304
You may need to feel respected

2721
01:37:38.304 --> 01:37:41.550
and they need to have agency

2722
01:37:41.550 --> 01:37:43.420
over their own department basically.

2723
01:37:43.420 --> 01:37:44.450
Yeah.

2724
01:37:44.450 --> 01:37:46.880
And feel like what they do and their presence matters.

2725
01:37:46.880 --> 01:37:47.713
Right?

2726
01:37:47.713 --> 01:37:49.860
They are an important effective part of that team.

2727
01:37:49.860 --> 01:37:52.020
That's where vulnerability really helps

2728
01:37:52.020 --> 01:37:53.250
in a leadership aspect.

2729
01:37:53.250 --> 01:37:57.280
So, when you show aspects of vulnerability,

2730
01:37:57.280 --> 01:37:59.367
such as third phase where you are saying,

2731
01:37:59.367 --> 01:38:02.800
"Hey, one of my jobs as an officer right now

2732
01:38:02.800 --> 01:38:04.120
is to make sure that I'm looking out

2733
01:38:04.120 --> 01:38:05.740
for the welfare of my guys."

2734
01:38:05.740 --> 01:38:06.573
Okay?

2735
01:38:06.573 --> 01:38:11.480
I'm gonna step up and show that I care about that

2736
01:38:11.480 --> 01:38:12.590
even if it's at risk to me.

2737
01:38:12.590 --> 01:38:14.170
I literally thought that I was gonna be running hills

2738
01:38:14.170 --> 01:38:16.803
by myself for the rest of the day.

2739
01:38:18.250 --> 01:38:20.720
But again, for me, it was a lesson

2740
01:38:20.720 --> 01:38:23.610
that I enjoyed the moment,

2741
01:38:23.610 --> 01:38:24.660
but it was interesting

2742
01:38:24.660 --> 01:38:26.740
how I didn't really process that fully

2743
01:38:26.740 --> 01:38:28.610
until I actually started thinking about trust and leadership

2744
01:38:28.610 --> 01:38:30.240
later on like 20-plus years later.

2745
01:38:30.240 --> 01:38:34.500
So, but yeah, I think it's a really interesting point.

2746
01:38:34.500 --> 01:38:35.333
Yeah.

2747
01:38:35.333 --> 01:38:40.150
So, much of this seems to be about matching the attributes

2748
01:38:41.150 --> 01:38:44.100
with the demands of the job.

2749
01:38:44.100 --> 01:38:47.690
Obviously in the context of the SEALs,

2750
01:38:47.690 --> 01:38:50.410
if you're doing underwater demolition

2751
01:38:50.410 --> 01:38:51.350
in the middle of the night,

2752
01:38:51.350 --> 01:38:52.220
that's very different

2753
01:38:52.220 --> 01:38:55.460
than being on presidential security detail.

2754
01:38:55.460 --> 01:38:59.210
You're both SEALs, those jobs are extremely different,

2755
01:38:59.210 --> 01:39:02.100
and there's gonna be a core competency

2756
01:39:02.100 --> 01:39:04.430
or set of overlapping attributes

2757
01:39:04.430 --> 01:39:07.220
that those individuals are gonna share.

2758
01:39:07.220 --> 01:39:08.350
But at the same time,

2759
01:39:08.350 --> 01:39:11.590
it's recognizing that there are many other attributes

2760
01:39:11.590 --> 01:39:12.890
that are at play here.

2761
01:39:12.890 --> 01:39:16.130
So, in thinking about it in that way,

2762
01:39:16.130 --> 01:39:17.620
is there,

2763
01:39:17.620 --> 01:39:19.320
like what are the crown jewels?

2764
01:39:19.320 --> 01:39:20.743
Like in the SEAL context,

2765
01:39:20.743 --> 01:39:22.513
like of all of these attributes,

2766
01:39:22.513 --> 01:39:24.420
there's gotta be a hierarchy

2767
01:39:24.420 --> 01:39:26.530
of which ones are more important than others.

2768
01:39:26.530 --> 01:39:27.470
In the SEAL context?

2769
01:39:27.470 --> 01:39:28.303
Yeah.

2770
01:39:28.303 --> 01:39:29.348
Yes.

2771
01:39:29.348 --> 01:39:33.610
I would say that the grit attributes are pretty high up.

2772
01:39:33.610 --> 01:39:35.580
I would say the,

2773
01:39:35.580 --> 01:39:39.190
I would say a few of the drive attributes are pretty high up

2774
01:39:39.190 --> 01:39:41.210
especially like something like cunning.

2775
01:39:41.210 --> 01:39:42.720
Cunning is an enormously powerful.

2776
01:39:42.720 --> 01:39:44.490
And most SEALs you meet,

2777
01:39:44.490 --> 01:39:47.170
the success of the Navy SEAL teams

2778
01:39:47.170 --> 01:39:48.630
is largely based on cunning.

2779
01:39:48.630 --> 01:39:50.590
It's not because of a super muscle-

2780
01:39:50.590 --> 01:39:52.300
Cunning can have a pejorative.

2781
01:39:52.300 --> 01:39:53.759
It can. It can.

2782
01:39:53.759 --> 01:39:54.592
Interesting.

2783
01:39:54.592 --> 01:39:56.380
But cunning really as I kinda define on the book

2784
01:39:56.380 --> 01:40:00.380
means the ability to look outside rules and boundaries.

2785
01:40:00.380 --> 01:40:02.310
Basically think outside the box, right?

2786
01:40:02.310 --> 01:40:04.410
Because we are all subject

2787
01:40:04.410 --> 01:40:08.390
to what's called functional fixedness or fixedness.

2788
01:40:08.390 --> 01:40:13.390
And this is this idea that we are drawn to see boundaries

2789
01:40:14.250 --> 01:40:16.050
that may or may not exist.

2790
01:40:16.050 --> 01:40:16.883
Okay?

2791
01:40:16.883 --> 01:40:21.400
So, we look at a problem and suddenly we place either,

2792
01:40:21.400 --> 01:40:23.330
we look at boundaries that we're given

2793
01:40:23.330 --> 01:40:25.230
or we place imaginary boundaries

2794
01:40:25.230 --> 01:40:27.220
that we think are real on it

2795
01:40:27.220 --> 01:40:32.220
versus we say, okay, what about this?

2796
01:40:32.250 --> 01:40:34.560
First of all, are these rules real or are they imagined?

2797
01:40:34.560 --> 01:40:36.840
If they are real, if I break them, what happens?

2798
01:40:36.840 --> 01:40:40.740
So, the example I give is a medieval one, a fantasy one.

2799
01:40:40.740 --> 01:40:44.470
Because I always I used to tell this to my guys too,

2800
01:40:44.470 --> 01:40:46.450
to describe the difference between us

2801
01:40:46.450 --> 01:40:50.190
and maybe some other people.

2802
01:40:50.190 --> 01:40:53.860
And I would say, okay, imagine you'd get dropped

2803
01:40:53.860 --> 01:40:56.180
into a fantasy medieval world

2804
01:40:56.180 --> 01:40:58.590
and there's a princess in a tower

2805
01:40:58.590 --> 01:40:59.790
guarded by a dragon, right?

2806
01:40:59.790 --> 01:41:02.430
And the king wants that princess rescued.

2807
01:41:02.430 --> 01:41:06.506
And the king has sent night after night to slay that dragon

2808
01:41:06.506 --> 01:41:08.200
and rescue the princess.

2809
01:41:08.200 --> 01:41:12.250
And night after night, has been killed by the dragon, right?

2810
01:41:12.250 --> 01:41:13.430
You drop a special operator.

2811
01:41:13.430 --> 01:41:15.010
And when I say, special operator, I don't mean just SEAL.

2812
01:41:15.010 --> 01:41:19.240
I mean, spec operator, SF guy or Greenbrae,

2813
01:41:19.240 --> 01:41:20.490
ranger, whatever.

2814
01:41:20.490 --> 01:41:21.400
Put him in the problem.

2815
01:41:21.400 --> 01:41:24.427
And first thing that guy asks is, "Hey, what's the mission?"

2816
01:41:24.427 --> 01:41:25.567
"Save the princess."

2817
01:41:25.567 --> 01:41:28.260
"Well, who gives a damn about the dragon, right?

2818
01:41:28.260 --> 01:41:29.720
I'm gonna find a way to save the princess

2819
01:41:29.720 --> 01:41:30.970
without hitting the dragon

2820
01:41:30.970 --> 01:41:32.990
because I don't wanna hit the dragon, right?

2821
01:41:32.990 --> 01:41:34.113
That dragon will kill me, right?"

2822
01:41:34.113 --> 01:41:38.950
So, by design, special operations was created

2823
01:41:39.810 --> 01:41:43.330
to think outside the box, to frustrate and agitate,

2824
01:41:43.330 --> 01:41:45.750
to find ways around that people we're thinking about.

2825
01:41:45.750 --> 01:41:46.583
That's one of the things

2826
01:41:46.583 --> 01:41:48.570
that drew me to spec ops in the first place,

2827
01:41:48.570 --> 01:41:51.051
was this idea of being cunning and invisibility.

2828
01:41:51.051 --> 01:41:54.260
And like, okay, can you sneak around?

2829
01:41:54.260 --> 01:41:55.440
So, when I was growing up,

2830
01:41:55.440 --> 01:41:57.200
I grew up in a town in Connecticut.

2831
01:41:57.200 --> 01:41:58.587
And I worked...

2832
01:41:58.587 --> 01:42:01.090
One of my jobs I worked at a Marina.

2833
01:42:01.090 --> 01:42:03.880
And the security guard at the Marina,

2834
01:42:03.880 --> 01:42:06.350
he'd basically come on at like 5:00 in the evening

2835
01:42:06.350 --> 01:42:07.310
and he'd stay the whole night

2836
01:42:07.310 --> 01:42:10.533
and leave at like 8:00 in the morning once we came back.

2837
01:42:11.785 --> 01:42:13.360
The guy's name was Ed Stoleng.

2838
01:42:13.360 --> 01:42:16.230
And Ed, man, he was a Marine veteran.

2839
01:42:16.230 --> 01:42:17.490
He was in World War II.

2840
01:42:17.490 --> 01:42:19.380
He was at Iwo Jima.

2841
01:42:19.380 --> 01:42:20.610
And we would sit there

2842
01:42:20.610 --> 01:42:22.167
and he'd tell us the stories of Iwo Jima.

2843
01:42:22.167 --> 01:42:23.770
And this guy, I mean, he'd bring us,

2844
01:42:23.770 --> 01:42:25.650
I was a teenager and he'd bring me to tears.

2845
01:42:25.650 --> 01:42:27.330
I mean, this guy was the bravest.

2846
01:42:27.330 --> 01:42:30.040
To this day, he's one of the bravest men I've ever met.

2847
01:42:30.040 --> 01:42:31.810
And one of the things I remember him saying,

2848
01:42:31.810 --> 01:42:36.690
he's like, "Rich, it's hard to describe the feeling

2849
01:42:38.180 --> 01:42:42.020
when you're advancing, right?

2850
01:42:42.020 --> 01:42:45.090
And you're in a line of cover, say folders or whatever.

2851
01:42:45.090 --> 01:42:48.040
And there's a open area that you have to go across

2852
01:42:48.040 --> 01:42:49.610
to get to the next line of cover.

2853
01:42:49.610 --> 01:42:52.180
And the first wave goes,

2854
01:42:52.180 --> 01:42:55.110
and almost all of them get mowed down, right?

2855
01:42:55.110 --> 01:42:56.510
And you're the second wave."

2856
01:42:57.630 --> 01:42:58.770
And I thought about that.

2857
01:42:58.770 --> 01:42:59.893
And first of all, I was just like,

2858
01:42:59.893 --> 01:43:01.210
and this is why I get chills talking about him

2859
01:43:01.210 --> 01:43:02.420
because he's just...

2860
01:43:02.420 --> 01:43:04.120
And I haven't talked to him.

2861
01:43:04.120 --> 01:43:07.240
He died several years ago and we were able to say hi

2862
01:43:07.240 --> 01:43:08.073
to his sons.

2863
01:43:08.073 --> 01:43:09.843
His sons were just as awesome.

2864
01:43:11.010 --> 01:43:13.170
But I remember thinking that I was like,

2865
01:43:13.170 --> 01:43:16.050
I don't know if I have an interest in being the second wave.

2866
01:43:16.050 --> 01:43:17.880
I have an interest in sneaking around

2867
01:43:17.880 --> 01:43:20.820
and like killing the machine gunner

2868
01:43:20.820 --> 01:43:22.320
before he ever sees me coming.

2869
01:43:23.610 --> 01:43:24.930
I just think that for me,

2870
01:43:24.930 --> 01:43:28.100
that was the attraction of special operators.

2871
01:43:28.100 --> 01:43:30.550
Can you find a way around

2872
01:43:30.550 --> 01:43:31.780
to actually accomplish something

2873
01:43:31.780 --> 01:43:34.273
that needs to be accomplished

2874
01:43:34.273 --> 01:43:36.888
with minimal life loss or whatever.

2875
01:43:36.888 --> 01:43:38.100
And so, I think that, so cunning is an important one.

2876
01:43:38.100 --> 01:43:39.300
So, I would say cunning.

2877
01:43:40.172 --> 01:43:45.172
I would say most of the team ability ones,

2878
01:43:47.360 --> 01:43:49.440
humor incredibly important.

2879
01:43:49.440 --> 01:43:50.400
I'll dive into.

2880
01:43:50.400 --> 01:43:51.240
That's an interesting one.

2881
01:43:51.240 --> 01:43:52.500
Yeah.

2882
01:43:52.500 --> 01:43:53.500
