2883
01:43:53.500 --> 01:43:54.570
I'll just finish the question.

2884
01:43:54.570 --> 01:43:56.590
I think for the leadership ones,

2885
01:43:56.590 --> 01:44:00.273
I think decisiveness, I think accountability,

2886
01:44:02.386 --> 01:44:03.219
they're all pretty important.

2887
01:44:03.219 --> 01:44:04.338
I think so.

2888
01:44:04.338 --> 01:44:05.340
Yeah, I mean, those would seem self-evident.

2889
01:44:05.340 --> 01:44:06.513
Yeah.

2890
01:44:06.513 --> 01:44:07.346
And it goes-

2891
01:44:07.346 --> 01:44:10.360
The humor one is a little more unexpected.

2892
01:44:10.360 --> 01:44:11.193
The humor?

2893
01:44:11.193 --> 01:44:12.271
Yeah.

2894
01:44:12.271 --> 01:44:15.170
Yeah, so humor, so we all need to take a bow

2895
01:44:15.170 --> 01:44:16.417
to all the comedians of the world

2896
01:44:16.417 --> 01:44:18.610
because they do such a great service.

2897
01:44:18.610 --> 01:44:22.070
And the reason is because humor is an unconscious

2898
01:44:25.140 --> 01:44:26.420
or is an involuntary,

2899
01:44:26.420 --> 01:44:28.920
laughing is an involuntary response, right?

2900
01:44:28.920 --> 01:44:32.960
And when we laugh, what happens is we get jolted

2901
01:44:32.960 --> 01:44:35.190
with three chemicals,

2902
01:44:35.190 --> 01:44:36.860
two neuro-transmitters and one hormone.

2903
01:44:36.860 --> 01:44:37.970
We get jolted with dopamine,

2904
01:44:37.970 --> 01:44:41.080
which we all know, powerful pleasure chemical

2905
01:44:42.350 --> 01:44:44.670
that's one of the most powerful in the world, right?

2906
01:44:44.670 --> 01:44:49.360
We get jolted with endorphins, which is what masks pain.

2907
01:44:49.360 --> 01:44:50.298
Right?

2908
01:44:50.298 --> 01:44:51.131
All of us who do.

2909
01:44:51.131 --> 01:44:52.260
I mean, you especially and endurance runners know this.

2910
01:44:52.260 --> 01:44:53.790
Runners high, that's endorphins.

2911
01:44:53.790 --> 01:44:55.530
It's basically by evolutionary design,

2912
01:44:55.530 --> 01:44:59.330
it's our body saying, "Okay, humans are endurance creatures.

2913
01:44:59.330 --> 01:45:00.163
You need to keep going.

2914
01:45:00.163 --> 01:45:02.740
So, I'm gonna flood you with these opiates

2915
01:45:02.740 --> 01:45:03.573
to make you feel better."

2916
01:45:03.573 --> 01:45:06.420
Which is interesting because they didn't know really,

2917
01:45:06.420 --> 01:45:07.890
we had endorphins.

2918
01:45:07.890 --> 01:45:09.840
I think it was the late '60s, mid '70s,

2919
01:45:09.840 --> 01:45:11.610
they were studying drug addiction.

2920
01:45:11.610 --> 01:45:15.645
And they found opiate receptors in the brain.

2921
01:45:15.645 --> 01:45:16.478
And then they said,

2922
01:45:16.478 --> 01:45:19.120
"Well, why the heck do human brains have opiate receptors?"

2923
01:45:19.120 --> 01:45:21.080
Well, the answer is because the human body

2924
01:45:21.080 --> 01:45:22.156
makes its own opiates. (laughs)

2925
01:45:22.156 --> 01:45:22.989
Opiates around. Yeah.

2926
01:45:22.989 --> 01:45:24.120
Enter endorphins, right?

2927
01:45:24.120 --> 01:45:24.953
That's endorphins.

2928
01:45:24.953 --> 01:45:29.220
They're the human bodies opiates that mask pain.

2929
01:45:29.220 --> 01:45:31.690
So, we get a dopamine, we get endorphins

2930
01:45:31.690 --> 01:45:33.060
and then we get oxytocin.

2931
01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:37.160
Oxytocin, a hormone, but guys like Huberman will say

2932
01:45:37.160 --> 01:45:41.390
it's almost a neurotransmitter hormone.

2933
01:45:41.390 --> 01:45:43.000
Neurotransmitters and hormones,

2934
01:45:43.000 --> 01:45:43.833
just to break it down,

2935
01:45:43.833 --> 01:45:47.930
neurotransmitters are like the fast flash, right?

2936
01:45:47.930 --> 01:45:49.870
They enter into our system very rapidly

2937
01:45:49.870 --> 01:45:51.230
and they dissipate very rapidly.

2938
01:45:51.230 --> 01:45:52.740
Hormones on the other hand,

2939
01:45:52.740 --> 01:45:54.850
enter into our system a little bit slowly,

2940
01:45:54.850 --> 01:45:56.162
but they last a lot.

2941
01:45:56.162 --> 01:45:57.820
They're like the fire burning into the night.

2942
01:45:57.820 --> 01:46:01.370
So, the neurotransmitters are like the fuel on the fire

2943
01:46:01.370 --> 01:46:03.870
and then the hormones are like the wood

2944
01:46:03.870 --> 01:46:04.760
that keeps it burning, right?

2945
01:46:04.760 --> 01:46:07.500
So oxytocin, it's in between,

2946
01:46:07.500 --> 01:46:08.650
but it definitely lasts longer.

2947
01:46:08.650 --> 01:46:09.483
That is the love.

2948
01:46:09.483 --> 01:46:10.700
It's known as the love hormone.

2949
01:46:10.700 --> 01:46:13.970
It's the feeling of safety and connection and love.

2950
01:46:13.970 --> 01:46:17.940
We exchange oxytocin in really engaging conversations,

2951
01:46:17.940 --> 01:46:19.950
in physical contact.

2952
01:46:19.950 --> 01:46:23.180
When we experience or effect acts of kindness

2953
01:46:23.180 --> 01:46:25.240
between human beings, oxytocin is created.

2954
01:46:25.240 --> 01:46:26.073
Right?

2955
01:46:26.073 --> 01:46:28.020
So, when we laugh, all three of those chemicals

2956
01:46:28.020 --> 01:46:29.780
are pumped into our system

2957
01:46:29.780 --> 01:46:31.340
and we have no control over it.

2958
01:46:31.340 --> 01:46:33.060
It's why we all feel good when we laugh.

2959
01:46:33.060 --> 01:46:34.660
Laughing makes us feel good.

2960
01:46:34.660 --> 01:46:36.550
So, why is that important?

2961
01:46:36.550 --> 01:46:38.900
And why does every single high-performing team

2962
01:46:38.900 --> 01:46:41.850
I've ever encountered have at least one class clown?

2963
01:46:41.850 --> 01:46:46.840
It's because when we're in pain and misery, humor is a hack.

2964
01:46:46.840 --> 01:46:49.040
It's a hack into keeping ongoing, right?

2965
01:46:49.040 --> 01:46:51.920
We get slapped with these three chemicals.

2966
01:46:51.920 --> 01:46:53.923
So, one of the things I didn't say about dopamine,

2967
01:46:53.923 --> 01:46:57.170
it kind of falls into that courage attribute.

2968
01:46:57.170 --> 01:46:58.200
Courage is really interesting.

2969
01:46:58.200 --> 01:47:01.070
This is where Huberman and I really geeked out.

2970
01:47:01.070 --> 01:47:02.340
The act of courage, you have to,

2971
01:47:02.340 --> 01:47:06.390
moving into our fear gives us a dopamine reward.

2972
01:47:06.390 --> 01:47:08.367
We decide to move in, we get a dopamine reward.

2973
01:47:08.367 --> 01:47:11.860
So, that dopamine reward is designed by evolution

2974
01:47:11.860 --> 01:47:13.470
to tell us, "Hey, keep going.

2975
01:47:13.470 --> 01:47:14.650
This is good, right?"

2976
01:47:14.650 --> 01:47:16.470
It's not necessarily a constraint

2977
01:47:16.470 --> 01:47:18.400
as to when you actually reach the goal.

2978
01:47:18.400 --> 01:47:19.760
It's as you take steps.

2979
01:47:19.760 --> 01:47:22.150
So, as we take step-by-step, we get dopamine reward.

2980
01:47:22.150 --> 01:47:24.170
So, it's encouraging us to keep on going.

2981
01:47:24.170 --> 01:47:26.286
So, we think about when we're in stress and pain,

2982
01:47:26.286 --> 01:47:27.870
we're getting all three of those.

2983
01:47:27.870 --> 01:47:29.273
So, the story I'll tell you is this

2984
01:47:29.273 --> 01:47:31.810
because I remember it was, it was Hell Week.

2985
01:47:31.810 --> 01:47:32.860
And we were sitting in the surfs

2986
01:47:32.860 --> 01:47:34.390
and we were going through surf torture.

2987
01:47:34.390 --> 01:47:35.412
All right?

2988
01:47:35.412 --> 01:47:36.900
And so, you probably know what surf torture is.

2989
01:47:36.900 --> 01:47:37.733
For those who don't,

2990
01:47:37.733 --> 01:47:39.900
surf torture is when you as a BUD/S student,

2991
01:47:39.900 --> 01:47:41.842
you link your arms up in the Pacific Ocean

2992
01:47:41.842 --> 01:47:45.250
there in Coronado, which is like I think low 60s.

2993
01:47:45.250 --> 01:47:47.540
We did a November, so it's probably high 50s.

2994
01:47:47.540 --> 01:47:48.670
And you basically link your arms

2995
01:47:48.670 --> 01:47:52.360
and you go to about ankle deep and then you lay down.

2996
01:47:52.360 --> 01:47:54.990
And then the waves crash over you and then they recede.

2997
01:47:54.990 --> 01:47:55.823
And they crash.

2998
01:47:55.823 --> 01:47:57.698
And it is coldest thing you'll ever do.

2999
01:47:57.698 --> 01:47:58.830
You're there like all night, right?

3000
01:47:58.830 --> 01:47:59.890
Well, they actually time it.

3001
01:47:59.890 --> 01:48:02.140
They have stopwatches there, so you don't get hypothermic.

3002
01:48:02.140 --> 01:48:04.780
So, it feels like all night as a student, right?

3003
01:48:04.780 --> 01:48:06.610
But anyway, we're in,

3004
01:48:06.610 --> 01:48:08.680
and it's usually at night, right?

3005
01:48:08.680 --> 01:48:10.000
So, we're in,

3006
01:48:10.000 --> 01:48:10.870
we're getting surf-tortured.

3007
01:48:10.870 --> 01:48:11.920
This is during Hell Week.

3008
01:48:11.920 --> 01:48:14.470
And as usual, the instructors, because they're...

3009
01:48:14.470 --> 01:48:16.070
It's funny when you see it on the outside.

3010
01:48:16.070 --> 01:48:17.450
It feels sadistic when you're in it.

3011
01:48:17.450 --> 01:48:20.250
They will drive a van onto the beach.

3012
01:48:20.250 --> 01:48:21.510
And the guy will get out with his megaphone.

3013
01:48:21.510 --> 01:48:24.900
He says, "Okay, anyone who wants to quit right now?

3014
01:48:24.900 --> 01:48:28.260
I have hot chocolate and I have warm blankets

3015
01:48:28.260 --> 01:48:31.750
and I have donuts in the van for anybody quits right now."

3016
01:48:31.750 --> 01:48:33.050
It's kind of like "The Survivor" thing, right?

3017
01:48:33.050 --> 01:48:34.977
You offer food for anybody who wants to quit.

3018
01:48:34.977 --> 01:48:36.060
And a lot of people quit.

3019
01:48:36.060 --> 01:48:39.080
But I remember him saying that, right?

3020
01:48:39.080 --> 01:48:40.660
He pulled the van and he said that.

3021
01:48:40.660 --> 01:48:41.785
And the guy next to me,

3022
01:48:41.785 --> 01:48:44.120
they guy to my right immediately pipes up.

3023
01:48:44.120 --> 01:48:47.520
He's like, "Hey, do you have any chocolate glaze donuts?

3024
01:48:47.520 --> 01:48:48.930
Because if you don't have any chocolate glaze donuts,

3025
01:48:48.930 --> 01:48:50.210
I'm not quitting."

3026
01:48:50.210 --> 01:48:51.990
And I burst out laughing.

3027
01:48:51.990 --> 01:48:52.840
He's laughing, I'm laughing.

3028
01:48:52.840 --> 01:48:54.670
I thought it was hilarious, right?

3029
01:48:54.670 --> 01:48:57.740
And immediately, I knew this guy's gonna make it.

3030
01:48:57.740 --> 01:48:59.600
Because he could make a joke.

3031
01:48:59.600 --> 01:49:00.910
He could find the funny, right?

3032
01:49:00.910 --> 01:49:02.710
I knew I was gonna make it because I was laughing.

3033
01:49:02.710 --> 01:49:05.430
I look over to my left, the guy next to me to my left,

3034
01:49:05.430 --> 01:49:07.410
that guy, his face hasn't even moved.

3035
01:49:07.410 --> 01:49:10.200
He's like he's lost in his misery.

3036
01:49:10.200 --> 01:49:11.337
Didn't even hear the joke, right?

3037
01:49:11.337 --> 01:49:12.780
And I said to myself-

3038
01:49:12.780 --> 01:49:14.203
He's not gonna make it.

3039
01:49:14.203 --> 01:49:15.036
He'll ring the bell.

3040
01:49:15.036 --> 01:49:15.870
This guy is like five minutes later, he rang the bell.

3041
01:49:15.870 --> 01:49:16.950
Right?

3042
01:49:16.950 --> 01:49:17.930
So, what happened there?

3043
01:49:17.930 --> 01:49:20.200
So, just in that moment, what happened?

3044
01:49:20.200 --> 01:49:22.820
That my buddy cracked a joke, right?

3045
01:49:22.820 --> 01:49:26.770
I was immediately and involuntarily flooded with dopamine

3046
01:49:26.770 --> 01:49:28.850
which is a chemical that tells me to keep going.

3047
01:49:28.850 --> 01:49:30.070
This is good.

3048
01:49:30.070 --> 01:49:32.247
Endorphins, which is a chemical that says,

3049
01:49:32.247 --> 01:49:33.960
"Actually, this doesn't feel that bad, right?"

3050
01:49:33.960 --> 01:49:35.510
I'm masking my pain a little bit.

3051
01:49:35.510 --> 01:49:37.690
And then oxytocin, I'm connected to this guy over there.

3052
01:49:37.690 --> 01:49:38.620
Right?

3053
01:49:38.620 --> 01:49:39.708
It's a courage check.

3054
01:49:39.708 --> 01:49:42.040
It's a hack into keeping going.

3055
01:49:42.040 --> 01:49:43.560
If we think about the pandemic,

3056
01:49:43.560 --> 01:49:44.577
I would imagine those of us

3057
01:49:44.577 --> 01:49:46.713
who were able to find some funny,

3058
01:49:48.180 --> 01:49:49.500
actually started feeling better.

3059
01:49:49.500 --> 01:49:51.777
It's why cancer patients report,

3060
01:49:51.777 --> 01:49:54.777
"Hey, I just started focusing on funny movies.

3061
01:49:54.777 --> 01:49:56.050
So, I started laughing more."

3062
01:49:56.050 --> 01:49:56.883
Why?

3063
01:49:56.883 --> 01:49:58.450
Because it's pushing all those chemicals.

3064
01:49:58.450 --> 01:50:00.190
It's causing you to keep going.

3065
01:50:00.190 --> 01:50:02.510
Oh, by the way, I'm sure any scientists

3066
01:50:02.510 --> 01:50:04.160
could say the whole host of other chemicals

3067
01:50:04.160 --> 01:50:06.147
that it's producing.

3068
01:50:06.147 --> 01:50:06.980
Right.

3069
01:50:06.980 --> 01:50:09.500
And I would think a means of developing

3070
01:50:09.500 --> 01:50:11.840
a bit of anti-fragility also.

3071
01:50:11.840 --> 01:50:13.580
Like you get this reset, right?

3072
01:50:13.580 --> 01:50:14.413
Totally.

3073
01:50:14.413 --> 01:50:15.900
That allows you to like hit a baseline

3074
01:50:15.900 --> 01:50:18.180
and maybe push through to another gear.

3075
01:50:18.180 --> 01:50:19.013
Yeah.

3076
01:50:19.013 --> 01:50:20.670
And I think one of the things about anti-fragility

3077
01:50:20.670 --> 01:50:24.920
is the ability to effectively recover and reframe.

3078
01:50:24.920 --> 01:50:26.730
So, anti-fragility is based on the idea

3079
01:50:26.730 --> 01:50:28.070
of being able to look,

3080
01:50:28.070 --> 01:50:30.550
especially if it's a traumatic event or challenge,

3081
01:50:30.550 --> 01:50:33.950
can you look back and can you understand

3082
01:50:33.950 --> 01:50:38.950
and learn effectively from that experience positively?

3083
01:50:39.530 --> 01:50:40.410
Right.

3084
01:50:40.410 --> 01:50:41.670
And you do that in a couple of ways.

3085
01:50:41.670 --> 01:50:42.503
First way you do it

3086
01:50:42.503 --> 01:50:44.560
is you actually you ask better questions.

3087
01:50:44.560 --> 01:50:46.840
So, I talk about this idea that again,

3088
01:50:46.840 --> 01:50:49.780
high-performing teams, high-performing humans,

3089
01:50:49.780 --> 01:50:51.120
they consistently do this.

3090
01:50:51.120 --> 01:50:54.190
We are neurologically designed to ask questions

3091
01:50:54.190 --> 01:50:55.023
about our environment.

3092
01:50:55.023 --> 01:50:57.550
That's what we do to make sense of the world, right?

3093
01:50:57.550 --> 01:50:59.310
We're doing it oftentimes unconsciously.

3094
01:50:59.310 --> 01:51:00.930
However, we can take control

3095
01:51:00.930 --> 01:51:03.160
and sometimes consciously control this.

3096
01:51:03.160 --> 01:51:05.180
The problem is a lot of people are guilty of,

3097
01:51:05.180 --> 01:51:06.670
and I've been guilty of this too,

3098
01:51:06.670 --> 01:51:07.990
asking the wrong questions.

3099
01:51:07.990 --> 01:51:10.410
Things like, "Why does this always happen to me?

3100
01:51:10.410 --> 01:51:11.530
What's wrong with me?

3101
01:51:11.530 --> 01:51:12.600
Why am I so bad at this?"

3102
01:51:12.600 --> 01:51:13.700
Right?

3103
01:51:13.700 --> 01:51:16.960
As soon as we place a question into our forebrain,

3104
01:51:16.960 --> 01:51:18.190
our brain will start coming with answers.

3105
01:51:18.190 --> 01:51:19.040
I do this experiment.

3106
01:51:19.040 --> 01:51:20.490
I could ask you any question right now

3107
01:51:20.490 --> 01:51:22.260
and I can say, okay, write down this question.

3108
01:51:22.260 --> 01:51:25.130
How can I double my income in the next six months?

3109
01:51:25.130 --> 01:51:26.450
And I'll give you 30 seconds.

3110
01:51:26.450 --> 01:51:28.410
Write down anything that pops into your brain.

3111
01:51:28.410 --> 01:51:29.390
Okay?

3112
01:51:29.390 --> 01:51:30.480
If I give you 30 seconds,

3113
01:51:30.480 --> 01:51:33.380
you're probably get a list of say five things.

3114
01:51:33.380 --> 01:51:35.060
Now, it doesn't really matter what those five things.

3115
01:51:35.060 --> 01:51:40.060
Some may be inane, some maybe bad ideas.

3116
01:51:40.150 --> 01:51:41.983
Selling your kidney is not a good idea, right?

3117
01:51:41.983 --> 01:51:43.903
Some are likely to be practical.

3118
01:51:45.140 --> 01:51:47.800
The point is as soon as you lodge that question

3119
01:51:47.800 --> 01:51:48.633
into your brain,

3120
01:51:48.633 --> 01:51:50.160
your brain begins to answer it, right?

3121
01:51:50.160 --> 01:51:52.190
This happens to us when we ask ourselves questions.

3122
01:51:52.190 --> 01:51:53.240
Why am I so bad at this?

3123
01:51:53.240 --> 01:51:55.260
Your brain starts to pollute your brain

3124
01:51:55.260 --> 01:51:56.470
with why you were so bad at this,

3125
01:51:56.470 --> 01:51:59.970
versus what are some of the things I learned?

3126
01:51:59.970 --> 01:52:01.310
How can I be better?

3127
01:52:01.310 --> 01:52:03.680
And so, part of the resiliency process

3128
01:52:03.680 --> 01:52:06.330
is the ability to ask better questions

3129
01:52:06.330 --> 01:52:08.310
as you look back on that experience.

3130
01:52:08.310 --> 01:52:10.590
Humor, laughing about something

3131
01:52:10.590 --> 01:52:12.545
helps reframe those questions.

3132
01:52:12.545 --> 01:52:15.350
If you are at the point where you can laugh about something,

3133
01:52:15.350 --> 01:52:17.980
you're in a perfect position to ask better questions

3134
01:52:17.980 --> 01:52:19.900
about it and say, okay, how can I learn?

3135
01:52:19.900 --> 01:52:20.760
How can I grow from this?

3136
01:52:20.760 --> 01:52:24.140
And that's the seeds of anti-fragility.

3137
01:52:24.140 --> 01:52:25.970
Well, I think that's a good segue

3138
01:52:25.970 --> 01:52:29.510
into how we begin to think about 2021.

3139
01:52:29.510 --> 01:52:33.530
Like we've emerged from 2020 and the shitshow it's been.

3140
01:52:33.530 --> 01:52:34.710
That's right. Yeah.

3141
01:52:34.710 --> 01:52:36.390
And all the stress that it's carried

3142
01:52:36.390 --> 01:52:38.710
in various ways for people.

3143
01:52:38.710 --> 01:52:41.317
So, I think it would be beneficial.

3144
01:52:41.317 --> 01:52:45.250
I don't wanna do the kind of tropey set

3145
01:52:45.250 --> 01:52:46.780
new year's resolutions.

3146
01:52:46.780 --> 01:52:50.440
I'd rather focus on how we can reframe

3147
01:52:50.440 --> 01:52:53.910
how we think about this stress that we carried in 2020

3148
01:52:54.870 --> 01:52:59.870
and use that as a launchpad or leverage for growth in 2021.

3149
01:53:02.230 --> 01:53:03.063
Yeah.

3150
01:53:05.330 --> 01:53:06.380
That's a big one.

3151
01:53:06.380 --> 01:53:07.537
Yeah.

3152
01:53:07.537 --> 01:53:08.370
Well, no, and it's an important one.

3153
01:53:08.370 --> 01:53:09.929
And this is what's on everyone's mind though, right?

3154
01:53:09.929 --> 01:53:10.762
It is. Yeah, it is.

3155
01:53:10.762 --> 01:53:15.320
Like it's early January and we gotta get a grip on.

3156
01:53:15.320 --> 01:53:19.110
We can't just keep in this static situation.

3157
01:53:19.110 --> 01:53:21.130
A lot of people feel like they've just had their feet

3158
01:53:21.130 --> 01:53:22.623
in cement for the last year.

3159
01:53:22.623 --> 01:53:24.005
Totally. Totally.

3160
01:53:24.005 --> 01:53:24.838
Yeah.

3161
01:53:24.838 --> 01:53:26.920
Well, so the first point is to understand

3162
01:53:26.920 --> 01:53:29.080
that you have made it through.

3163
01:53:29.080 --> 01:53:29.937
Okay?

3164
01:53:29.937 --> 01:53:32.260
So, you've actually grown because of it.

3165
01:53:32.260 --> 01:53:34.220
So, one better question to ask

3166
01:53:34.220 --> 01:53:36.140
is how have I grown from this?

3167
01:53:36.140 --> 01:53:37.700
Again, that's a subjective question.

3168
01:53:37.700 --> 01:53:39.430
So, I can't answer it for people.

3169
01:53:39.430 --> 01:53:42.500
I will say fairly ubiquitously,

3170
01:53:42.500 --> 01:53:44.550
that most of us have worked very effectively

3171
01:53:44.550 --> 01:53:46.100
on our task switching attribute

3172
01:53:46.100 --> 01:53:47.743
and our adaptability attribute.

3173
01:53:48.640 --> 01:53:52.610
Resilience might be something we need to help ourselves with

3174
01:53:52.610 --> 01:53:54.900
by saying, okay, what are some of the positives

3175
01:53:54.900 --> 01:53:56.840
that came out of 2020?

3176
01:53:56.840 --> 01:53:59.342
And how is my life better because of it?

3177
01:53:59.342 --> 01:54:00.175
So, that's a great question.

3178
01:54:00.175 --> 01:54:04.670
How is my life better now in January 2021

3179
01:54:04.670 --> 01:54:07.090
than it was in January 2020?

3180
01:54:07.090 --> 01:54:09.340
Admittedly, that might take some thought, right?

3181
01:54:09.340 --> 01:54:11.370
But you will come up with answers.

3182
01:54:11.370 --> 01:54:15.350
One of the best ways to put yourself in the proper state,

3183
01:54:15.350 --> 01:54:16.183
and I love this question,

3184
01:54:16.183 --> 01:54:18.150
is just ask what you're grateful for.

3185
01:54:18.150 --> 01:54:22.740
Gratitude also is an enormously powerful

3186
01:54:22.740 --> 01:54:24.420
chemical combination.

3187
01:54:24.420 --> 01:54:27.530
When you are truly grateful, you're getting oxytocin,

3188
01:54:27.530 --> 01:54:29.983
you're getting DHA, you're getting dopamine.

3189
01:54:31.060 --> 01:54:33.800
Asking yourselves, "What am I grateful for now,"

3190
01:54:33.800 --> 01:54:35.490
is a great way in.

3191
01:54:35.490 --> 01:54:36.760
So, then you say,

3192
01:54:36.760 --> 01:54:38.554
okay, what are some of those things I learned?

3193
01:54:38.554 --> 01:54:41.713
What are the things I have to think about going into 2021?

3194
01:54:41.713 --> 01:54:43.050
Okay, well, it's gonna be uncertain.

3195
01:54:43.050 --> 01:54:44.200
We know that.

3196
01:54:44.200 --> 01:54:46.320
So, when I think about my grit attributes,

3197
01:54:46.320 --> 01:54:47.350
I'm gonna need a little bit of courage.

3198
01:54:47.350 --> 01:54:49.080
If I like I'm low on courage,

3199
01:54:49.080 --> 01:54:51.290
I should probably try to develop that a little bit.

3200
01:54:51.290 --> 01:54:54.050
I'm definitely gonna need to have adaptability.

3201
01:54:54.050 --> 01:54:54.883
Perseverance.

3202
01:54:54.883 --> 01:54:56.250
Okay, I have some goals.

3203
01:54:56.250 --> 01:54:59.750
Obviously my goals might've been derailed in January 2020.

3204
01:54:59.750 --> 01:55:01.380
Okay, now what do I do?

3205
01:55:01.380 --> 01:55:03.510
What are the things I can do to persevere

3206
01:55:03.510 --> 01:55:06.060
and affect my goals in 2021 no matter what happens?

3207
01:55:06.060 --> 01:55:10.000
And how am I going to adapt to do that?

3208
01:55:10.000 --> 01:55:13.290
I think an enormously important,

3209
01:55:13.290 --> 01:55:15.810
in fact, if I were to scale them I'd probably say

3210
01:55:15.810 --> 01:55:17.490
one of the most important attributes

3211
01:55:17.490 --> 01:55:20.730
that we can all focus on in 2021 is open-mindedness.

3212
01:55:20.730 --> 01:55:25.590
Open-mindedness again, the closed mind is not driven.

3213
01:55:25.590 --> 01:55:27.080
Because the closed mind is certain.

3214
01:55:27.080 --> 01:55:29.380
And certain minds aren't curious

3215
01:55:29.380 --> 01:55:31.490
and they're not seeking what's next.

3216
01:55:31.490 --> 01:55:33.720
They weren't seeking what could be.

3217
01:55:33.720 --> 01:55:37.670
And if 2020 taught us anything, is that we don't know.

3218
01:55:37.670 --> 01:55:39.690
We don't know what's coming down the pike.

3219
01:55:39.690 --> 01:55:43.750
And if we're open-minded enough to start understanding,

3220
01:55:43.750 --> 01:55:44.730
okay, I'm gonna take...

3221
01:55:44.730 --> 01:55:47.570
Now, this is a passive active.

3222
01:55:47.570 --> 01:55:52.450
Optimism is, I would call optimism proactive pathway.

3223
01:55:52.450 --> 01:55:54.100
Open-mindedness is a passive pathway

3224
01:55:54.100 --> 01:55:54.933
where you're saying,

3225
01:55:54.933 --> 01:55:58.270
okay, I am going to be open to other ideas, viewpoints,

3226
01:55:58.270 --> 01:55:59.103
situations,

3227
01:55:59.103 --> 01:56:03.330
so that I can try to look at them from a positive,

3228
01:56:03.330 --> 01:56:04.210
not necessarily positive,

3229
01:56:04.210 --> 01:56:08.900
but a proactive and effective lens.

3230
01:56:08.900 --> 01:56:12.080
But there's between making that decision

3231
01:56:12.080 --> 01:56:14.444
and actually effectuating it, right?

3232
01:56:14.444 --> 01:56:15.357
That's right.

3233
01:56:15.357 --> 01:56:16.190
You can say, "I'm open-minded,"

3234
01:56:16.190 --> 01:56:17.723
but then you find yourself in a situation

3235
01:56:17.723 --> 01:56:20.080
and you're very much not open-minded.

3236
01:56:20.080 --> 01:56:20.913
Yeah. Yeah.

3237
01:56:20.913 --> 01:56:24.060
And I think we all found ourselves in that situation

3238
01:56:24.060 --> 01:56:24.893
in 2020.

3239
01:56:24.893 --> 01:56:27.480
And again, I come back to this idea of asking questions.

3240
01:56:27.480 --> 01:56:30.020
Ultimately, it comes down to the questions we ask ourselves.

3241
01:56:30.020 --> 01:56:31.740
If we find ourselves in a situation

3242
01:56:31.740 --> 01:56:36.320
where we're feeling like, okay, this person I'm talking to

3243
01:56:36.320 --> 01:56:39.803
seems to be of complete opposite political beliefs than me.

3244
01:56:41.060 --> 01:56:42.323
How might I be wrong?

3245
01:56:43.600 --> 01:56:45.910
What might this person be feeling?

3246
01:56:45.910 --> 01:56:48.700
What might this person be experiencing that I'm not?

3247
01:56:48.700 --> 01:56:50.920
You start to tap into empathy certainly.

3248
01:56:50.920 --> 01:56:55.090
But again, empathy is about feeling what that person feels.

3249
01:56:55.090 --> 01:56:56.970
That's a little bit more of a leap,

3250
01:56:56.970 --> 01:57:00.610
but you can certainly start having a perspective

3251
01:57:00.610 --> 01:57:01.780
without necessarily feeling.

3252
01:57:01.780 --> 01:57:02.613
And I think-

3253
01:57:02.613 --> 01:57:03.720
Curiosity.

3254
01:57:03.720 --> 01:57:04.553
Curiosity.

3255
01:57:04.553 --> 01:57:06.430
Curiosity is the buttress to open-mindedness.

3256
01:57:06.430 --> 01:57:07.263
Absolutely.

3257
01:57:07.263 --> 01:57:09.360
But I say, some people are more naturally curious

3258
01:57:09.360 --> 01:57:10.580
than others.

3259
01:57:10.580 --> 01:57:13.550
And most of those people are automatically open-minded.

3260
01:57:13.550 --> 01:57:15.980
So, the reason why I didn't put curiosity there

3261
01:57:15.980 --> 01:57:18.640
is because I think open-mindedness can be accessed

3262
01:57:18.640 --> 01:57:22.260
by almost anybody just by asking the right question.

3263
01:57:22.260 --> 01:57:23.200
Can I just...

3264
01:57:23.200 --> 01:57:28.200
Let me give this person the situation, this event a chance.

3265
01:57:28.894 --> 01:57:33.577
And let me start seeing what might be positive about that.

3266
01:57:33.577 --> 01:57:35.327
Let me see it in a different light.

3267
01:57:36.380 --> 01:57:38.160
Take myself out of my own perspective.

3268
01:57:38.160 --> 01:57:43.160
If I'm not this 47-year-old male, former Navy SEAL, author

3269
01:57:46.350 --> 01:57:47.760
living in Virginia,

3270
01:57:47.760 --> 01:57:49.730
if I'm not that person,

3271
01:57:49.730 --> 01:57:50.980
then how does this look?

3272
01:57:50.980 --> 01:57:51.813
Right?

3273
01:57:51.813 --> 01:57:55.454
And those are really powerful questions to ask

3274
01:57:55.454 --> 01:57:58.810
and ones that can help open your mind quite a bit.

3275
01:57:58.810 --> 01:58:02.893
So, it's a proactive approach to a passive.

3276
01:58:03.865 --> 01:58:05.510
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

3277
01:58:05.510 --> 01:58:10.510
I also think here we are in 2021, we did survive 2020.

3278
01:58:11.570 --> 01:58:13.453
So, no matter how difficult it was

3279
01:58:13.453 --> 01:58:15.160
to reflect back on that

3280
01:58:15.160 --> 01:58:18.180
and realize that maybe you have a little bit more resilience

3281
01:58:18.180 --> 01:58:19.880
than perhaps you imagined, right?

3282
01:58:19.880 --> 01:58:21.770
100%. 100%.

3283
01:58:21.770 --> 01:58:23.630
And again, humans are designed to be resilient.

3284
01:58:23.630 --> 01:58:26.960
That's why we've evolved and survived as a species.

3285
01:58:26.960 --> 01:58:28.303
We're designed to be that way.

3286
01:58:28.303 --> 01:58:32.220
We can effectively speed it up.

3287
01:58:32.220 --> 01:58:34.110
And we can also effectively grow from it.

3288
01:58:34.110 --> 01:58:36.381
That's transferring to anti-fragility.

3289
01:58:36.381 --> 01:58:37.660
But we are all here.

3290
01:58:37.660 --> 01:58:38.680
We're all here.

3291
01:58:38.680 --> 01:58:41.400
We're all operating.

3292
01:58:41.400 --> 01:58:43.070
We're all in our lives.

3293
01:58:43.070 --> 01:58:46.320
Admittedly, some of us might be in worse positions

3294
01:58:46.320 --> 01:58:48.030
than we were at the beginning of 2020,

3295
01:58:48.030 --> 01:58:51.190
but again, sometimes you get thrown down the hill.

3296
01:58:51.190 --> 01:58:53.540
And when you stand up and dust yourself off,

3297
01:58:53.540 --> 01:58:55.650
you're like, "Oh my gosh, I'm I'm way further down

3298
01:58:55.650 --> 01:58:56.483
than I was before.

3299
01:58:56.483 --> 01:58:57.500
I gotta climb again."

3300
01:58:57.500 --> 01:58:58.827
But the fact is you can do it.

3301
01:58:58.827 --> 01:59:00.977
And we can do 2021.

3302
01:59:00.977 --> 01:59:04.779
And I think if we are effectively able to understand

3303
01:59:04.779 --> 01:59:08.140
and dissect the lessons

3304
01:59:08.140 --> 01:59:11.570
2020 taught each one of us individually,

3305
01:59:11.570 --> 01:59:14.540
we are all in a position where we can crush 2021.

3306
01:59:14.540 --> 01:59:15.580
I really believe that.

3307
01:59:15.580 --> 01:59:16.620
I really do.

3308
01:59:16.620 --> 01:59:18.170
Because we've been through some stuff

3309
01:59:18.170 --> 01:59:20.930
that historically, is so unique.

3310
01:59:20.930 --> 01:59:22.175
And that's something

3311
01:59:22.175 --> 01:59:25.040
to just give ourselves a quick pat on the back for.

3312
01:59:25.040 --> 01:59:29.160
What is the process of performing that dissection now

3313
01:59:29.160 --> 01:59:31.780
for somebody who's never done anything like that?

3314
01:59:31.780 --> 01:59:32.613
Yeah.

3315
01:59:32.613 --> 01:59:34.421
Well, so the first process is to put yourself

3316
01:59:34.421 --> 01:59:38.043
in a calm state, right?

3317
01:59:38.043 --> 01:59:43.010
You wanna try to take emotion out of the equation.

3318
01:59:43.010 --> 01:59:44.670
Very difficult, admittedly, right?

3319
01:59:44.670 --> 01:59:47.120
But emotion tends to blur our,

3320
01:59:47.120 --> 01:59:49.370
it's more limbic than it is for our brain.

3321
01:59:49.370 --> 01:59:52.132
So, it blurs our logical ability to dissect.

3322
01:59:52.132 --> 01:59:55.110
So, to the extent possible, take the emotion out of it.

3323
01:59:55.110 --> 01:59:56.090
From that position,

3324
01:59:56.090 --> 01:59:57.850
begin to ask yourself some questions about it.

3325
01:59:57.850 --> 01:59:59.380
Okay?

3326
01:59:59.380 --> 02:00:00.213
What happened?

3327
02:00:01.690 --> 02:00:02.633
How did I respond?

3328
02:00:03.860 --> 02:00:07.930
Was that response effective or ineffective?

3329
02:00:07.930 --> 02:00:09.820
What can I learn from that?

3330
02:00:09.820 --> 02:00:11.680
And then how can I grow from it?

3331
02:00:11.680 --> 02:00:12.513
And then how can I...

3332
02:00:12.513 --> 02:00:13.940
And then from that list, then you say,

3333
02:00:13.940 --> 02:00:15.020
okay, how can I transfer that

3334
02:00:15.020 --> 02:00:18.110
to what I wanna accomplish in 2021?

3335
02:00:18.110 --> 02:00:19.108
That's a start.

3336
02:00:19.108 --> 02:00:19.990
That's that sounds like

3337
02:00:19.990 --> 02:00:22.893
what we call the four step in AA. (laughs)

3338
02:00:22.893 --> 02:00:24.392
(laughs) Maybe so. Maybe so.

3339
02:00:24.392 --> 02:00:25.225
The inventory.

3340
02:00:25.225 --> 02:00:26.060
Yeah.

3341
02:00:26.060 --> 02:00:26.893
Is there,

3342
02:00:27.890 --> 02:00:30.683
in the process of learning about all these attributes,

3343
02:00:31.580 --> 02:00:33.540
is there an attribute that comes to mind

3344
02:00:33.540 --> 02:00:35.590
that's like the bastard stepchild?

3345
02:00:35.590 --> 02:00:40.080
Like the often overlooked and underappreciated attribute

3346
02:00:40.080 --> 02:00:41.660
that you realize like

3347
02:00:41.660 --> 02:00:43.818
we should be paying more attention to this.

3348
02:00:43.818 --> 02:00:45.840
Well, I mean, we already talked about narcissism.

3349
02:00:45.840 --> 02:00:48.160
And the reason is because narcissism can be so dangerous,

3350
02:00:48.160 --> 02:00:49.610
but it counts to be powerful.

3351
02:00:50.910 --> 02:00:51.980
No, I wouldn't say that,

3352
02:00:51.980 --> 02:00:56.450
but I think what I would call out would be the three others

3353
02:00:56.450 --> 02:00:58.243
that I talk about in the book.

3354
02:00:58.243 --> 02:01:00.770
The four category or the five categories

3355
02:01:00.770 --> 02:01:02.910
outline 22 attributes.

3356
02:01:02.910 --> 02:01:04.870
But the title of the book is 25 attributes, right?

3357
02:01:04.870 --> 02:01:06.280
So, I talk about the others,

3358
02:01:06.280 --> 02:01:08.760
which are patience and competitiveness

3359
02:01:08.760 --> 02:01:12.283
and fear of rejection.

3360
02:01:13.150 --> 02:01:15.490
When I started to look at those as attributes,

3361
02:01:15.490 --> 02:01:19.410
I began to discover that they were unique

3362
02:01:19.410 --> 02:01:20.243
from the other ones.

3363
02:01:20.243 --> 02:01:21.640
Because while the other ones,

3364
02:01:21.640 --> 02:01:23.500
if you put on a sliding scale,

3365
02:01:23.500 --> 02:01:26.810
most of them, it could be argued that more is better.

3366
02:01:26.810 --> 02:01:28.310
Obviously, we could make an argument

3367
02:01:28.310 --> 02:01:30.130
against that for narcissists and things like that.

3368
02:01:30.130 --> 02:01:33.340
But for the most part, you could say more is better.

3369
02:01:33.340 --> 02:01:36.430
But those are the three, competitiveness, patience,

3370
02:01:36.430 --> 02:01:37.800
and fear of rejection.

3371
02:01:37.800 --> 02:01:40.293
I wasn't getting the same answer, right?

3372
02:01:41.190 --> 02:01:44.007
Impatience can be just as powerful as patience.

3373
02:01:44.007 --> 02:01:47.290
And there are very super successful people

3374
02:01:47.290 --> 02:01:50.310
who are inpatient and it works very well for them.

3375
02:01:50.310 --> 02:01:54.300
Fear of rejection, this idea that I care what people think

3376
02:01:54.300 --> 02:01:56.090
to the extent that I'm gonna push myself

3377
02:01:56.090 --> 02:01:57.090
beyond my boundaries.

3378
02:01:57.090 --> 02:01:59.900
This is why a lot of SEALs do what they do.

3379
02:01:59.900 --> 02:02:00.733
Some of us,

3380
02:02:00.733 --> 02:02:01.566
I write in the book,

3381
02:02:01.566 --> 02:02:02.399
I don't like heights, right?

3382
02:02:02.399 --> 02:02:03.830
Skydiving was always a challenge for me.

3383
02:02:03.830 --> 02:02:04.950
I skydived every time.

3384
02:02:04.950 --> 02:02:07.280
I did hundreds of skydives because everybody else was

3385
02:02:07.280 --> 02:02:09.950
and I was not going to be left behind.

3386
02:02:09.950 --> 02:02:11.330
And I was not gonna let them down.

3387
02:02:11.330 --> 02:02:12.510
I cared what they thought.

3388
02:02:12.510 --> 02:02:13.343
So, why the guys who,

3389
02:02:13.343 --> 02:02:14.690
the SEALs who don't like scuba diving

3390
02:02:14.690 --> 02:02:15.980
still get underwater, right?

3391
02:02:15.980 --> 02:02:17.830
So, fear of rejection can be powerful,

3392
02:02:17.830 --> 02:02:21.470
but insouciance, I don't care what other people think

3393
02:02:21.470 --> 02:02:22.780
is just as powerful.

3394
02:02:22.780 --> 02:02:26.180
We all know those iconoclasts who just broke from the pack

3395
02:02:26.180 --> 02:02:28.160
and they didn't care what anybody thought, right?

3396
02:02:28.160 --> 02:02:29.670
Those people are very powerful too.

3397
02:02:29.670 --> 02:02:30.503
They can be.

3398
02:02:30.503 --> 02:02:32.321
So, the idea is where you fall on the scale.

3399
02:02:32.321 --> 02:02:34.150
The other one is competitiveness.

3400
02:02:34.150 --> 02:02:38.410
It's very often in the peak performance world

3401
02:02:38.410 --> 02:02:39.243
or the performance world

3402
02:02:39.243 --> 02:02:40.810
that competitiveness is looked at

3403
02:02:41.660 --> 02:02:44.290
as a very, very powerful trait to have.

3404
02:02:44.290 --> 02:02:47.350
And that competitive gene is really essential.

3405
02:02:47.350 --> 02:02:49.580
And I don't disagree with that.

3406
02:02:49.580 --> 02:02:51.710
What I disagree with is the implied corollary,

3407
02:02:51.710 --> 02:02:54.490
which is non-competitiveness is bad, right?

3408
02:02:54.490 --> 02:02:55.480
And I had to,

3409
02:02:55.480 --> 02:02:57.070
a lot of this stuff I did a lot of self-reflection for.

3410
02:02:57.070 --> 02:02:57.903
Right?

3411
02:02:57.903 --> 02:02:59.380
I am not a competitive person. (laughs)

3412
02:02:59.380 --> 02:03:01.200
I never have been.

3413
02:03:01.200 --> 02:03:03.760
When I played sports in high school, I really played too.

3414
02:03:03.760 --> 02:03:06.620
I played lacrosse, which was my main sport.

3415
02:03:06.620 --> 02:03:08.610
And then I did track basically to get in shape for lacrosse.

3416
02:03:08.610 --> 02:03:10.030
Right?

3417
02:03:10.030 --> 02:03:12.180
I love the game and I love playing.

3418
02:03:12.180 --> 02:03:13.320
I love the intricacy of the game.

3419
02:03:13.320 --> 02:03:14.160
I love the stick work.

3420
02:03:14.160 --> 02:03:14.993
I love that.

3421
02:03:14.993 --> 02:03:16.440
I didn't really care if we won or lost.

3422
02:03:16.440 --> 02:03:19.520
I didn't find myself emotionally moved either direction.

3423
02:03:19.520 --> 02:03:20.910
But being part of the team-

3424
02:03:20.910 --> 02:03:22.950
Being part of the team, I liked all that stuff,

3425
02:03:22.950 --> 02:03:24.940
but the winning or losing piece,

3426
02:03:24.940 --> 02:03:26.970
it didn't affect me the way I saw

3427
02:03:26.970 --> 02:03:27.803
it was affecting other people.

3428
02:03:27.803 --> 02:03:28.636
And I fought for a while.

3429
02:03:28.636 --> 02:03:30.930
I was like, "Oh boy, I think this might be a problem."

3430
02:03:30.930 --> 02:03:31.984
Right?

3431
02:03:31.984 --> 02:03:32.817
Especially when I started thinking about SEAL training,

3432
02:03:32.817 --> 02:03:34.330
it's like I'm not competitive.

3433
02:03:34.330 --> 02:03:35.420
Is this a problem?

3434
02:03:35.420 --> 02:03:37.790
What I realized is in SEAL training,

3435
02:03:37.790 --> 02:03:40.980
BUD/S favors neither the competitive gene

3436
02:03:40.980 --> 02:03:41.993
or the non-competitive.

3437
02:03:41.993 --> 02:03:43.120
That's interesting.

3438
02:03:43.120 --> 02:03:44.765
And one example of that are the two awards

3439
02:03:44.765 --> 02:03:45.937
that are given at the end of BUD/S,

3440
02:03:45.937 --> 02:03:48.390
are two awards given him a BUD/S class.

3441
02:03:48.390 --> 02:03:49.860
One is the Honor Man.

3442
02:03:49.860 --> 02:03:52.700
The Honor Man is the award for the guy

3443
02:03:52.700 --> 02:03:54.930
who has the best scores in everything.

3444
02:03:54.930 --> 02:03:58.000
Fastest runs, best on course time, fastest swims.

3445
02:03:58.000 --> 02:04:00.130
It favors that competitive gene, right?

3446
02:04:00.130 --> 02:04:02.080
You're basically given award for the best scores.

3447
02:04:02.080 --> 02:04:04.120
The other reward is the Fire in the Gut.

3448
02:04:04.120 --> 02:04:06.270
Fire in the Gut award is given to the person

3449
02:04:06.270 --> 02:04:08.112
who showed the most grit and drive

3450
02:04:08.112 --> 02:04:09.660
and perseverance through BUD/S.

3451
02:04:09.660 --> 02:04:11.280
Often that guy who wins that

3452
02:04:11.280 --> 02:04:13.000
has some of the lowest scores, right?

3453
02:04:13.000 --> 02:04:15.540
So, you don't win the Fire in the Gut.

3454
02:04:15.540 --> 02:04:16.373
It's earned.

3455
02:04:16.373 --> 02:04:18.900
And it's based on a vote of the instructors and students.

3456
02:04:18.900 --> 02:04:21.090
So, what that told me was the SEAL teams,

3457
02:04:21.090 --> 02:04:22.970
and I think any high-performing team

3458
02:04:22.970 --> 02:04:26.680
does extraordinarily well with both polarities.

3459
02:04:26.680 --> 02:04:30.290
Because the competitive mind is extraordinarily adept

3460
02:04:30.290 --> 02:04:32.250
and powerful at looking at a situation,

3461
02:04:32.250 --> 02:04:34.527
especially one with boundaries and rules and saying,

3462
02:04:34.527 --> 02:04:36.647
"Okay, how can I win in this situation?"

3463
02:04:37.540 --> 02:04:39.950
Whereas the non-competitive mind, my mind,

3464
02:04:39.950 --> 02:04:43.275
says, "I don't feel like playing that game.

3465
02:04:43.275 --> 02:04:45.810
What's the other game we can play that's different.

3466
02:04:45.810 --> 02:04:47.106
The hard worker game.

3467
02:04:47.106 --> 02:04:48.760
What's a different pathway?

3468
02:04:48.760 --> 02:04:51.590
I literally find myself looking at the pack of people

3469
02:04:51.590 --> 02:04:53.570
and saying, I'm not really interested in competing.

3470
02:04:53.570 --> 02:04:54.650
And they're all doing great work.

3471
02:04:54.650 --> 02:04:55.593
I'm gonna do this.

3472
02:04:56.740 --> 02:04:58.410
When you're talking about special operations,

3473
02:04:58.410 --> 02:04:59.243
that's powerful.

3474
02:04:59.243 --> 02:05:00.510
I think when you're talking about business,

3475
02:05:00.510 --> 02:05:01.343
that's very powerful.

3476
02:05:01.343 --> 02:05:02.780
Because in business aspects,

3477
02:05:02.780 --> 02:05:04.170
certainly there are times,

3478
02:05:04.170 --> 02:05:05.320
there are aspects of business

3479
02:05:05.320 --> 02:05:07.330
where you have to be competitive.

3480
02:05:07.330 --> 02:05:08.600
But there are also aspects of business

3481
02:05:08.600 --> 02:05:11.220
where you think outside the box

3482
02:05:11.220 --> 02:05:12.953
and kinda thinking about disruption

3483
02:05:12.953 --> 02:05:16.710
and different things to do and not competitiveness

3484
02:05:16.710 --> 02:05:18.400
or non-competitiveness is really important.

3485
02:05:18.400 --> 02:05:21.303
So, both, what I say is that both,

3486
02:05:22.600 --> 02:05:26.430
for all three, both polarities can contribute

3487
02:05:26.430 --> 02:05:28.520
to high performance.

3488
02:05:28.520 --> 02:05:31.380
And if you have teams that honor both,

3489
02:05:31.380 --> 02:05:33.790
you have a really incredibly high-performing team.

3490
02:05:33.790 --> 02:05:36.610
And the last example I give on that is my wife and I.

3491
02:05:36.610 --> 02:05:39.330
I am typically a very patient person.

3492
02:05:39.330 --> 02:05:41.430
My wife is not a patient person.

3493
02:05:41.430 --> 02:05:43.060
That's her default.

3494
02:05:43.060 --> 02:05:44.440
It works beautifully.

3495
02:05:44.440 --> 02:05:45.390
We've been married 20 years.

3496
02:05:45.390 --> 02:05:46.320
It's worked beautifully, right?

3497
02:05:46.320 --> 02:05:50.030
Because when the situation requires patience,

3498
02:05:50.030 --> 02:05:51.360
I get pushed to the front.

3499
02:05:51.360 --> 02:05:52.193
I step up.

3500
02:05:52.193 --> 02:05:53.026
I shouldn't say I get pushed.

3501
02:05:53.026 --> 02:05:54.090
I step up to the front, right?

3502
02:05:54.090 --> 02:05:56.470
When the situation requires impatience,

3503
02:05:56.470 --> 02:05:58.060
she steps up and takes lead.

3504
02:05:58.060 --> 02:05:59.840
And it's that dynamic swap.

3505
02:05:59.840 --> 02:06:02.110
It's dynamic subordination in terms of patience.

3506
02:06:02.110 --> 02:06:02.943
Yeah.

3507
02:06:02.943 --> 02:06:04.910
Yeah, it makes me think that another application

3508
02:06:04.910 --> 02:06:06.300
of the assessment tool

3509
02:06:06.300 --> 02:06:10.590
is to figure out how people pair up in dating.

3510
02:06:10.590 --> 02:06:11.423
Right?

3511
02:06:11.423 --> 02:06:15.350
Like if you could figure out

3512
02:06:15.350 --> 02:06:19.100
which attributes match with other attributes

3513
02:06:19.100 --> 02:06:21.420
in terms of compatibility in a relationship. (laughs)

3514
02:06:21.420 --> 02:06:22.472
Yeah.

3515
02:06:22.472 --> 02:06:23.780
And I would just offer a few dates.

3516
02:06:23.780 --> 02:06:24.613
Give it a while.

3517
02:06:24.613 --> 02:06:27.426
It can't just be one or two days. (laughs)

3518
02:06:27.426 --> 02:06:32.426
It has to be shorter than marriage, of course,

3519
02:06:36.500 --> 02:06:38.800
but longer than just a few dates, right?

3520
02:06:38.800 --> 02:06:39.860
So, you have to,

3521
02:06:39.860 --> 02:06:41.930
I dunno what that secret sauce is.

3522
02:06:41.930 --> 02:06:44.390
Again, I met my wife in Hawaii.

3523
02:06:44.390 --> 02:06:45.860
My first duty station was Hawaii.

3524
02:06:45.860 --> 02:06:50.140
I met her and we went on one date and then I left.

3525
02:06:50.140 --> 02:06:51.480
I moved to Virginia.

3526
02:06:51.480 --> 02:06:55.540
And so, our relationship for the first three months

3527
02:06:55.540 --> 02:06:56.690
was all letters and phone calls.

3528
02:06:56.690 --> 02:06:57.980
We literally wrote each other letters.

3529
02:06:57.980 --> 02:07:00.840
And then she finally came and visited me a few times.

3530
02:07:00.840 --> 02:07:03.220
And then I flew out six months later and proposed to her.

3531
02:07:03.220 --> 02:07:04.307
Right?

3532
02:07:04.307 --> 02:07:05.140
So, so I-

3533
02:07:05.140 --> 02:07:05.973
Wow.

3534
02:07:05.973 --> 02:07:06.806
Yeah.

3535
02:07:06.806 --> 02:07:07.639
And we've been married for 20 years.

3536
02:07:07.639 --> 02:07:11.400
So, something went right there.

3537
02:07:11.400 --> 02:07:15.909
So, I can't necessarily prescribe timelines responsibly,

3538
02:07:15.909 --> 02:07:17.077
but...

3539
02:07:17.077 --> 02:07:19.377
And then we know of people who they date for years

3540
02:07:19.377 --> 02:07:20.210
and you see us.

3541
02:07:20.210 --> 02:07:21.190
Wow, we have to make room for a little fairy dust

3542
02:07:21.190 --> 02:07:22.250
in all of this, right?

3543
02:07:22.250 --> 02:07:23.083
I agree.

3544
02:07:23.083 --> 02:07:24.020
Yeah. Yeah.

3545
02:07:24.020 --> 02:07:26.010
As much as we love Andrew Huberman,

3546
02:07:26.010 --> 02:07:27.660
like there's a mystical aspect in these things as well.

3547
02:07:27.660 --> 02:07:29.200
Well, so you're absolutely right.

3548
02:07:29.200 --> 02:07:30.140
And what I'll tell you is,

3549
02:07:30.140 --> 02:07:31.680
so when I was a kid,

3550
02:07:31.680 --> 02:07:35.370
I was just before high school or just getting high school,

3551
02:07:35.370 --> 02:07:38.390
my mom handed me a book called "The Key to Yourself."

3552
02:07:38.390 --> 02:07:39.650
It's written by Venice Bloodworth.

3553
02:07:39.650 --> 02:07:41.277
It was written in the '50s.

3554
02:07:41.277 --> 02:07:44.600
"The Key to Yourself" is a book that explained

3555
02:07:44.600 --> 02:07:48.400
the law of attraction and visualizing and all that stuff.

3556
02:07:48.400 --> 02:07:51.740
And I was enamored with this stuff.

3557
02:07:51.740 --> 02:07:53.330
I read that book over and over again.

3558
02:07:53.330 --> 02:07:55.370
I began to read everything I could

3559
02:07:55.370 --> 02:07:56.730
on the powerless of the conscious mind

3560
02:07:56.730 --> 02:07:58.670
and law of attraction and things like that.

3561
02:07:58.670 --> 02:08:00.840
And I began to, my brother too,

3562
02:08:00.840 --> 02:08:04.830
and I began to practice it and start writing things down

3563
02:08:04.830 --> 02:08:06.890
and visualizing and things like that.

3564
02:08:06.890 --> 02:08:08.890
And it started working for me.

3565
02:08:08.890 --> 02:08:10.150
I mean, and the first thing that happened

3566
02:08:10.150 --> 02:08:11.750
was like I wanted a Jeep.

3567
02:08:11.750 --> 02:08:15.060
I wanted a Jeep CJ-7 as a high school car.

3568
02:08:15.060 --> 02:08:16.050
And my brother did too.

3569
02:08:16.050 --> 02:08:16.987
And so, we visualized that

3570
02:08:16.987 --> 02:08:19.130
and I could just picture myself driving it.

3571
02:08:19.130 --> 02:08:22.540
Senior year, I got a Jeep, a 1984 Jeep CJ-7.

3572
02:08:22.540 --> 02:08:23.610
Powerful manifester.

3573
02:08:23.610 --> 02:08:24.790
I drive it to this day.

3574
02:08:24.790 --> 02:08:27.000
It's at the airport right now waiting for me.

3575
02:08:27.000 --> 02:08:27.970
I've kept that car.

3576
02:08:27.970 --> 02:08:28.803
Right?

3577
02:08:28.803 --> 02:08:31.780
Same thing happened when I went to college

3578
02:08:31.780 --> 02:08:33.190
and I wanted to get an ROTC scholarship.

3579
02:08:33.190 --> 02:08:34.100
I began to visualize it.

3580
02:08:34.100 --> 02:08:37.010
Same thing happened when I wanted to be a SEAL

3581
02:08:37.010 --> 02:08:38.790
and the selection was really tough.

3582
02:08:38.790 --> 02:08:41.350
And they were only selecting a few people.

3583
02:08:41.350 --> 02:08:45.240
So, well, I'm neither here to promote

3584
02:08:45.240 --> 02:08:47.210
or purport the efficacy of metaphysics.

3585
02:08:47.210 --> 02:08:48.043
Right?

3586
02:08:48.043 --> 02:08:52.487
I do believe that this idea of visualization,

3587
02:08:54.760 --> 02:08:58.279
of positive thinking, of optimism works.

3588
02:08:58.279 --> 02:09:01.150
I believe there's stuff yet to be studied

3589
02:09:01.150 --> 02:09:02.580
in terms of why it works.

3590
02:09:02.580 --> 02:09:04.070
I would say one practical,

3591
02:09:04.070 --> 02:09:06.100
just because I do like to try to put some science

3592
02:09:06.100 --> 02:09:07.050
around some of it,

3593
02:09:07.050 --> 02:09:09.270
is that if we just look at this 11 million bits

3594
02:09:09.270 --> 02:09:13.390
of information that comes into our systems every second.

3595
02:09:13.390 --> 02:09:15.340
When we decide on something,

3596
02:09:15.340 --> 02:09:17.590
when we write something down or decide on a goal,

3597
02:09:17.590 --> 02:09:19.877
suddenly that's telling our brains, our forebrains,

3598
02:09:19.877 --> 02:09:22.270
"Okay, notice things about that."

3599
02:09:22.270 --> 02:09:23.430
Same thing when you buy a car

3600
02:09:23.430 --> 02:09:24.980
and suddenly that car is everywhere.

3601
02:09:24.980 --> 02:09:26.390
It's like, "Oh my God, did everybody buy this car?

3602
02:09:26.390 --> 02:09:27.223
It's everywhere."

3603
02:09:27.223 --> 02:09:28.640
You hadn't noticed that car before.

3604
02:09:28.640 --> 02:09:30.460
It's because you just put that in your forebrain.

3605
02:09:30.460 --> 02:09:31.980
You basically told your forebrain

3606
02:09:31.980 --> 02:09:34.150
out of that 11 million bits,

3607
02:09:34.150 --> 02:09:36.248
if something along this line comes, I want you to notice it.

3608
02:09:36.248 --> 02:09:37.081
Right?

3609
02:09:37.081 --> 02:09:39.280
So, I think the power of visualization,

3610
02:09:39.280 --> 02:09:42.180
I think the power of positive thinking, optimism.

3611
02:09:42.180 --> 02:09:44.840
And I'm really a big believer on writing things down.

3612
02:09:44.840 --> 02:09:47.150
I think writing goals down,

3613
02:09:47.150 --> 02:09:49.630
and as specifically as you can get,

3614
02:09:49.630 --> 02:09:52.270
I think is a powerful evolution

3615
02:09:52.270 --> 02:09:56.090
because there's a merger of the physical

3616
02:09:56.090 --> 02:09:57.250
and the mental kinda cements.

3617
02:09:57.250 --> 02:09:59.087
And I've done that my whole life.

3618
02:09:59.087 --> 02:09:59.920
Yeah, it makes it real.

3619
02:09:59.920 --> 02:10:00.753
It makes it real.

3620
02:10:00.753 --> 02:10:01.586
Yeah.

3621
02:10:01.586 --> 02:10:02.419
And I tell the story.

3622
02:10:02.419 --> 02:10:03.490
I did that for my...

3623
02:10:03.490 --> 02:10:04.956
I wrote down at one point.

3624
02:10:04.956 --> 02:10:08.230
I was like I really wanna meet one of my dreams.

3625
02:10:08.230 --> 02:10:09.150
I wrote down.

3626
02:10:09.150 --> 02:10:14.150
And I still, I had these small notepad sheets,

3627
02:10:14.230 --> 02:10:15.340
the real small ones.

3628
02:10:15.340 --> 02:10:16.950
And I started writing down

3629
02:10:16.950 --> 02:10:19.140
just what I thought my perfect woman,

3630
02:10:19.140 --> 02:10:20.550
just description of my perfect woman.

3631
02:10:20.550 --> 02:10:22.740
And it took about four of these things

3632
02:10:22.740 --> 02:10:23.920
to write down all the things.

3633
02:10:23.920 --> 02:10:25.940
And I just wrote it down and put it away,

3634
02:10:25.940 --> 02:10:26.773
put it in a drawer.

3635
02:10:26.773 --> 02:10:28.490
And I remember the date that I was on,

3636
02:10:28.490 --> 02:10:30.230
my first date with my wife,

3637
02:10:30.230 --> 02:10:31.600
and I remember we were talking

3638
02:10:31.600 --> 02:10:32.777
and in the back of my mind, I was like,

3639
02:10:32.777 --> 02:10:35.870
"Oh my God, these things are kinda clicking off."

3640
02:10:35.870 --> 02:10:37.280
And I like, and it just worked out.

3641
02:10:37.280 --> 02:10:38.127
I still have those sheets by the way.

3642
02:10:38.127 --> 02:10:39.940
And I showed it to her years later.

3643
02:10:39.940 --> 02:10:41.410
I said this and she was blown away.

3644
02:10:41.410 --> 02:10:43.300
But I think understanding that,

3645
02:10:43.300 --> 02:10:44.270
getting that clear in your head

3646
02:10:44.270 --> 02:10:46.990
helps put that lodge that into your brain

3647
02:10:46.990 --> 02:10:48.720
Powerful creator, Rich Diviney.

3648
02:10:48.720 --> 02:10:50.242
Well, thank you. (laughs)

3649
02:10:50.242 --> 02:10:51.075
Yeah.

3650
02:10:51.075 --> 02:10:52.030
All right, we gotta lay on this plane,

3651
02:10:52.030 --> 02:10:57.030
but I'm not gonna let you go without asking you,

3652
02:10:57.250 --> 02:11:00.120
I know you can't speak to the specifics

3653
02:11:00.120 --> 02:11:02.000
of any of the operations that you've been on,

3654
02:11:02.000 --> 02:11:06.200
but I'm interested if you could share any experiences

3655
02:11:06.200 --> 02:11:08.780
that you've had that you're comfortable sharing

3656
02:11:08.780 --> 02:11:12.730
that maybe shifted your perception while serving

3657
02:11:12.730 --> 02:11:15.410
or kind of informed the work that you do now.

3658
02:11:15.410 --> 02:11:16.460
Absolutely. Yeah.

3659
02:11:16.460 --> 02:11:17.993
I'll tell you one story.

3660
02:11:19.120 --> 02:11:21.860
And nothing...

3661
02:11:21.860 --> 02:11:23.638
It's an operation and nothing happened, right?.

3662
02:11:23.638 --> 02:11:25.190
Nothing combat happened.

3663
02:11:25.190 --> 02:11:30.190
So, we were tasked with an operation where we,

3664
02:11:30.440 --> 02:11:31.740
so we got some of these back up.

3665
02:11:31.740 --> 02:11:33.180
We got some intelligence on something

3666
02:11:33.180 --> 02:11:37.563
that was gonna happen in the village during the daytime.

3667
02:11:38.530 --> 02:11:39.410
In Afghanistan or?

3668
02:11:39.410 --> 02:11:40.243
This was Iraq.

3669
02:11:40.243 --> 02:11:41.284
Yeah.

3670
02:11:41.284 --> 02:11:43.800
And so, when you get something like that,

3671
02:11:43.800 --> 02:11:45.880
you say, okay, what do we wanna effect

3672
02:11:45.880 --> 02:11:47.300
and how can we affect that?

3673
02:11:47.300 --> 02:11:48.770
Sometimes that means you okay, what you're gonna do

3674
02:11:48.770 --> 02:11:50.900
what's called a remain-over-day operation.

3675
02:11:50.900 --> 02:11:54.130
So, what that means is you're gonna find a place on the map,

3676
02:11:54.130 --> 02:11:55.510
you're gonna go in at night.

3677
02:11:55.510 --> 02:11:57.350
You're gonna put yourself there

3678
02:11:57.350 --> 02:12:00.360
in a position where you can watch at effective needed.

3679
02:12:00.360 --> 02:12:02.300
And you'd just sit there all day and just watch.

3680
02:12:02.300 --> 02:12:03.510
It's kinda like a recon mission,

3681
02:12:03.510 --> 02:12:06.390
but you have hopefully an objective if something happens.

3682
02:12:06.390 --> 02:12:08.540
And then, affected if effective.

3683
02:12:08.540 --> 02:12:09.710
So, we had one of these things.

3684
02:12:09.710 --> 02:12:12.020
We had intelligence that something was gonna happen.

3685
02:12:12.020 --> 02:12:12.980
So, we looked at it and said,

3686
02:12:12.980 --> 02:12:14.780
okay, this was in a village in Iraq.

3687
02:12:14.780 --> 02:12:17.400
And we picked a compound that we were in.

3688
02:12:17.400 --> 02:12:18.890
Okay, we'll sit here.

3689
02:12:18.890 --> 02:12:19.723
It's a good visibility.

3690
02:12:19.723 --> 02:12:21.110
We can put everybody hide.

3691
02:12:21.110 --> 02:12:22.300
No one knows we're there.

3692
02:12:22.300 --> 02:12:26.420
So, we go in, in the wee hours of the morning is still dark.

3693
02:12:26.420 --> 02:12:28.350
We descend into this compound silently.

3694
02:12:28.350 --> 02:12:32.060
Of course, these people have no clue that we're coming

3695
02:12:32.060 --> 02:12:33.200
and now we're gonna be there all day.

3696
02:12:33.200 --> 02:12:35.960
So, it's the multi-generational houses.

3697
02:12:35.960 --> 02:12:38.420
So, you have grandmas down to babies.

3698
02:12:38.420 --> 02:12:39.940
So, we're making sure everybody's positioned,

3699
02:12:39.940 --> 02:12:41.570
all the civilians are safe.

3700
02:12:41.570 --> 02:12:43.500
We're positioning our guys so they can see all that stuff.

3701
02:12:43.500 --> 02:12:45.738
I'm in charge of the mission on the OIC.

3702
02:12:45.738 --> 02:12:48.030
And while I'm walking around,

3703
02:12:48.030 --> 02:12:49.250
I'm noticing new this little girl.

3704
02:12:49.250 --> 02:12:50.300
She's following me around.

3705
02:12:50.300 --> 02:12:52.600
This little girl was probably six years old, six or seven.

3706
02:12:52.600 --> 02:12:53.433
She's following me around.

3707
02:12:53.433 --> 02:12:55.940
She's trying to tell me something, but I'm working,

3708
02:12:55.940 --> 02:12:58.670
so I can't stop to address it just yet.

3709
02:12:58.670 --> 02:13:01.100
But once everything is settled down,

3710
02:13:01.100 --> 02:13:04.140
I brought the terp over.

3711
02:13:04.140 --> 02:13:04.973
I said, "Hey, can you tell me

3712
02:13:04.973 --> 02:13:07.200
what this little girl is saying?"

3713
02:13:07.200 --> 02:13:09.390
And so, he asked her and she says,

3714
02:13:09.390 --> 02:13:10.390
she tells him.

3715
02:13:10.390 --> 02:13:12.610
And he says, "Yeah, she said you remind her

3716
02:13:12.610 --> 02:13:16.090
of some movie star, some Iraqi movie star.

3717
02:13:16.090 --> 02:13:18.970
And she wants to know if you can play a game with her,

3718
02:13:18.970 --> 02:13:20.000
if you wanna play a game with her."

3719
02:13:20.000 --> 02:13:21.796
And so I said, "Sure."

3720
02:13:21.796 --> 02:13:24.450
Those types of missions can often be very boring

3721
02:13:24.450 --> 02:13:25.840
until something happens.

3722
02:13:25.840 --> 02:13:28.510
So, we knew we were in the long haul, just gonna settle in.

3723
02:13:28.510 --> 02:13:29.343
Yeah, sure.

3724
02:13:29.343 --> 02:13:30.780
So, she runs into a room, she grabs this game,

3725
02:13:30.780 --> 02:13:31.780
she comes out.

3726
02:13:31.780 --> 02:13:33.470
And we start playing.

3727
02:13:33.470 --> 02:13:34.861
And the only way, I don't know,

3728
02:13:34.861 --> 02:13:39.250
I mean, this ages us out a little bit,

3729
02:13:39.250 --> 02:13:41.250
but I don't know if you remember a game called Tribulation.

3730
02:13:41.250 --> 02:13:43.450
But this game, it's a math game.

3731
02:13:43.450 --> 02:13:45.160
It's like you have numbers and you have to,

3732
02:13:45.160 --> 02:13:47.430
you get a number and you have to find out

3733
02:13:47.430 --> 02:13:48.800
how to multiply to it or whatever.

3734
02:13:48.800 --> 02:13:50.880
It was kind of an Iraqi version of this.

3735
02:13:50.880 --> 02:13:53.460
It was something having to do with math.

3736
02:13:53.460 --> 02:13:54.490
And we start playing this game.

3737
02:13:54.490 --> 02:13:56.810
And two things happen that I notice.

3738
02:13:56.810 --> 02:13:59.900
The first thing I noticed is that the terp got up and left.

3739
02:13:59.900 --> 02:14:01.110
I didn't even notice he left.

3740
02:14:01.110 --> 02:14:02.290
Okay?

3741
02:14:02.290 --> 02:14:03.540
Because now it's just two human beings having the experience

3742
02:14:03.540 --> 02:14:05.930
from just playing the game that this little girl.

3743
02:14:05.930 --> 02:14:07.260
Then the second thing I noticed

3744
02:14:07.260 --> 02:14:10.030
was this girl was incredibly smart.

3745
02:14:10.030 --> 02:14:10.863
I mean, she was kicking my ass at this thing.

3746
02:14:10.863 --> 02:14:13.600
I mean, she was incredibly bright and smart

3747
02:14:13.600 --> 02:14:15.513
and just really with it.

3748
02:14:15.513 --> 02:14:19.550
And so, we play this game throughout the day.

3749
02:14:19.550 --> 02:14:22.550
But she points at which I had to get up and work.

3750
02:14:22.550 --> 02:14:23.383
I work and then she comes back.

3751
02:14:23.383 --> 02:14:25.450
And then what we were looking to have happen, never happened

3752
02:14:25.450 --> 02:14:27.670
and end up being a pretty boring day.

3753
02:14:27.670 --> 02:14:29.530
So, we play periodically throughout the day.

3754
02:14:29.530 --> 02:14:30.460
Now the sun goes down.

3755
02:14:30.460 --> 02:14:31.970
We wait until it's time.

3756
02:14:31.970 --> 02:14:33.660
And then we're getting ready to leave

3757
02:14:33.660 --> 02:14:35.810
and go back to our pickup zone

3758
02:14:35.810 --> 02:14:36.950
where the helicopters can get us.

3759
02:14:36.950 --> 02:14:38.330
So, we get everything together.

3760
02:14:38.330 --> 02:14:39.720
We're up and moving, working.

3761
02:14:39.720 --> 02:14:40.970
This girl is following me around again

3762
02:14:40.970 --> 02:14:42.950
trying to tell me something.

3763
02:14:42.950 --> 02:14:45.180
Finally, we're getting ready to step out and leave.

3764
02:14:45.180 --> 02:14:46.277
And I bring the terp over and I say,

3765
02:14:46.277 --> 02:14:49.440
"Hey, can you tell me what she's saying?"

3766
02:14:49.440 --> 02:14:51.900
He asked her and he says, "Hey, she's wondering

3767
02:14:51.900 --> 02:14:54.220
if you can come back tomorrow and play with her?"

3768
02:14:54.220 --> 02:14:57.307
And at that point, I realized you ask yourself,

3769
02:14:57.307 --> 02:15:00.723
"Okay, is the truth the best thing here right now?"

3770
02:15:00.723 --> 02:15:03.000
And so, I remember getting on one of my knees

3771
02:15:03.000 --> 02:15:04.100
and I put my hands on her shoulders.

3772
02:15:04.100 --> 02:15:08.442
And I said, "Tell her that if I'm ever back here again,

3773
02:15:08.442 --> 02:15:10.327
I promise I'll come play with her."

3774
02:15:11.674 --> 02:15:13.270
And he told her, and that seemed to satisfy her.

3775
02:15:13.270 --> 02:15:15.210
I hugged her and we left.

3776
02:15:15.210 --> 02:15:16.470
And I never went back.

3777
02:15:16.470 --> 02:15:17.670
And I'm glad I never went back.

3778
02:15:17.670 --> 02:15:21.540
Because if spec ops guys have to go to the same place twice,

3779
02:15:21.540 --> 02:15:23.310
then you know it's probably not a good thing.

3780
02:15:23.310 --> 02:15:24.143
Right?

3781
02:15:24.143 --> 02:15:29.143
And so, as I went through my career and I have processed it,

3782
02:15:29.760 --> 02:15:32.983
I realized everything that we did out there,

3783
02:15:34.820 --> 02:15:36.480
I with my troop and team,

3784
02:15:36.480 --> 02:15:37.580
I have no regrets.

3785
02:15:37.580 --> 02:15:38.450
We acted honorably.

3786
02:15:38.450 --> 02:15:39.660
We act with integrity.

3787
02:15:39.660 --> 02:15:40.493
I don't lose sleep.

3788
02:15:40.493 --> 02:15:42.350
I'm fine with everything, which I'm fortunate.

3789
02:15:42.350 --> 02:15:44.400
I know that's not the case for everybody.

3790
02:15:45.560 --> 02:15:47.110
Because war is tough.

3791
02:15:47.110 --> 02:15:47.970
I think about,

3792
02:15:47.970 --> 02:15:49.810
I see that girls face a lot.

3793
02:15:49.810 --> 02:15:50.643
Like all the time.

3794
02:15:50.643 --> 02:15:51.560
I can still picture her face.

3795
02:15:51.560 --> 02:15:53.760
And I wonder if she's okay.

3796
02:15:53.760 --> 02:15:54.650
I wonder how she's doing.

3797
02:15:54.650 --> 02:15:56.510
I wonder where she is.

3798
02:15:56.510 --> 02:15:57.500
And I wonder...

3799
02:15:57.500 --> 02:15:58.800
And she was so smart.

3800
02:15:58.800 --> 02:16:01.820
I wonder, did she ever,

3801
02:16:01.820 --> 02:16:06.070
was she ever able to affect that intelligence,

3802
02:16:06.070 --> 02:16:09.240
that talent, that just the beauty inside of her.

3803
02:16:09.240 --> 02:16:11.580
And then I wonder about all these other kids.

3804
02:16:11.580 --> 02:16:13.468
It doesn't have to be at a town in Iraq.

3805
02:16:13.468 --> 02:16:16.123
It can be a kid in LA.

3806
02:16:16.123 --> 02:16:17.853
It can be a kid in New York.

3807
02:16:17.853 --> 02:16:22.540
I mean, how many of those kids are out there

3808
02:16:22.540 --> 02:16:27.110
that don't have the resources or some of the tools

3809
02:16:27.110 --> 02:16:29.470
to be able to start realizing their own potential.

3810
02:16:29.470 --> 02:16:30.960
And so, I think a lot about it.

3811
02:16:30.960 --> 02:16:32.140
I call it finding Einstein.

3812
02:16:32.140 --> 02:16:33.620
Like where's our next Einstein.

3813
02:16:33.620 --> 02:16:37.100
This girl could have been or could be our next Einstein.

3814
02:16:37.100 --> 02:16:40.823
I am certainly not it, but I'm really, really,

3815
02:16:42.190 --> 02:16:43.800
I get excited about human potential.

3816
02:16:43.800 --> 02:16:45.000
This idea that we as humans

3817
02:16:45.000 --> 02:16:47.020
can imagine something that doesn't exist

3818
02:16:47.020 --> 02:16:50.053
and then bring it into an existence.

3819
02:16:51.190 --> 02:16:52.247
And so, that type of,

3820
02:16:52.247 --> 02:16:53.440
and we do that,

3821
02:16:53.440 --> 02:16:55.717
that type of evolution, that type of process

3822
02:16:55.717 --> 02:16:58.540
is just very inspiring to me.

3823
02:16:58.540 --> 02:17:01.540
And those people who can help us evolve

3824
02:17:01.540 --> 02:17:03.247
are inspiring as well.

3825
02:17:03.247 --> 02:17:04.620
And so, that's really, for me,

3826
02:17:04.620 --> 02:17:07.100
it's all about human potential human performance.

3827
02:17:07.100 --> 02:17:09.290
Can I think about things I've learned

3828
02:17:09.290 --> 02:17:13.130
to help people start to explore their own potential,

3829
02:17:13.130 --> 02:17:14.128
find their own potential

3830
02:17:14.128 --> 02:17:15.190
if their parents

3831
02:17:15.190 --> 02:17:18.930
or if they're in the lives of young people,

3832
02:17:18.930 --> 02:17:20.970
can they help those young people start to explore?

3833
02:17:20.970 --> 02:17:23.051
Can we start drawing out some of those Einstein's

3834
02:17:23.051 --> 02:17:26.490
because ultimately, we can do great things.

3835
02:17:26.490 --> 02:17:28.490
And there are some leaders.

3836
02:17:28.490 --> 02:17:30.360
And I don't know if this little girl was one of them,

3837
02:17:30.360 --> 02:17:31.540
but that's a-

3838
02:17:31.540 --> 02:17:33.490
Yeah, it seems like something

3839
02:17:33.490 --> 02:17:34.890
that should be a social mandate.

3840
02:17:34.890 --> 02:17:36.330
I mean, you would have to suspect

3841
02:17:36.330 --> 02:17:38.270
there's hundreds of thousands,

3842
02:17:38.270 --> 02:17:40.700
if not millions of people walking the earth

3843
02:17:40.700 --> 02:17:43.440
who are brilliantly talented in some way or another

3844
02:17:43.440 --> 02:17:46.690
who are never able to tap into the right vein

3845
02:17:46.690 --> 02:17:48.390
in order to become fully expressed in that.

3846
02:17:48.390 --> 02:17:51.980
And it's almost a miracle when that person

3847
02:17:51.980 --> 02:17:55.550
who has that talent is able to figure out

3848
02:17:55.550 --> 02:17:59.560
how to get the resources to be fully expressed in that.

3849
02:17:59.560 --> 02:18:01.570
Like that's almost the fluke

3850
02:18:01.570 --> 02:18:02.403
when it should be the other way around.

3851
02:18:02.403 --> 02:18:03.397
That's exactly right.

3852
02:18:03.397 --> 02:18:07.100
And you're dealing with a kid in a small village

3853
02:18:07.100 --> 02:18:08.790
in a remote part of the world,

3854
02:18:08.790 --> 02:18:12.054
the chances that that person is gonna find

3855
02:18:12.054 --> 02:18:15.180
what they need to support them in that expression

3856
02:18:15.180 --> 02:18:17.020
is extremely limited.

3857
02:18:17.020 --> 02:18:17.853
It really is.

3858
02:18:17.853 --> 02:18:20.250
And the key is, and at least from my perspective

3859
02:18:20.250 --> 02:18:21.720
is let's start with ourselves.

3860
02:18:21.720 --> 02:18:23.390
We have so much inside of ourselves.

3861
02:18:23.390 --> 02:18:25.540
I mean, if an average guy,

3862
02:18:25.540 --> 02:18:26.760
I had an average upbringing.

3863
02:18:26.760 --> 02:18:27.830
I was an average student.

3864
02:18:27.830 --> 02:18:29.383
I was an average athlete.

3865
02:18:29.383 --> 02:18:31.777
If an average guy like me can-

3866
02:18:31.777 --> 02:18:34.130
All you SEALs guys with this average stuff.

3867
02:18:34.130 --> 02:18:34.963
Come on now. (laughs)

3868
02:18:34.963 --> 02:18:35.796
(laughs) Yeah.

3869
02:18:35.796 --> 02:18:36.629
But I mean, if you can affect,

3870
02:18:36.629 --> 02:18:38.890
I mean we're all in some ways, unless we're Mozart,

3871
02:18:38.890 --> 02:18:40.240
we're all average, at least a little bit, right?

3872
02:18:40.240 --> 02:18:42.480
But if I can affect,

3873
02:18:42.480 --> 02:18:44.410
again, environment matters certainly.

3874
02:18:44.410 --> 02:18:46.390
But if I can start digging into my own attributes,

3875
02:18:46.390 --> 02:18:48.217
can people start to think about,

3876
02:18:48.217 --> 02:18:50.387
"Hey, it can start inside."

3877
02:18:51.502 --> 02:18:52.335
It always does.

3878
02:18:52.335 --> 02:18:53.168
It always does.

3879
02:18:53.168 --> 02:18:57.320
And how do we best understand ourselves first?

3880
02:18:57.320 --> 02:18:59.940
But you need to understand the engine

3881
02:18:59.940 --> 02:19:02.060
before you start tweaking it

3882
02:19:02.060 --> 02:19:04.840
and putting putting high-speed stuff on it, right?

3883
02:19:04.840 --> 02:19:07.720
So, I sometimes I get frustrated

3884
02:19:07.720 --> 02:19:09.967
with all these gimmicks and hacks that say,

3885
02:19:09.967 --> 02:19:11.525
"Oh, do this, do that."

3886
02:19:11.525 --> 02:19:12.358
It's like, well,

3887
02:19:12.358 --> 02:19:14.360
if you don't understand your own engine first,

3888
02:19:14.360 --> 02:19:17.280
then you might put something on it that's gonna blow it.

3889
02:19:17.280 --> 02:19:19.570
Yeah, you're just revving it in park.

3890
02:19:19.570 --> 02:19:20.899
Yeah, you're revving it in park.

3891
02:19:20.899 --> 02:19:24.350
So, what are some ways we can begin to understand ourselves?

3892
02:19:24.350 --> 02:19:28.650
And the best news of all is we all have uncertainty

3893
02:19:28.650 --> 02:19:30.700
and challenge and strife in our lives.

3894
02:19:30.700 --> 02:19:32.490
And those are wonderful crucibles

3895
02:19:32.490 --> 02:19:34.230
inside of which we can start understanding ourselves.

3896
02:19:34.230 --> 02:19:35.063
Yeah.

3897
02:19:35.063 --> 02:19:37.490
And the good news is 2021 is here.

3898
02:19:37.490 --> 02:19:38.420
2021 is here.

3899
02:19:38.420 --> 02:19:39.727
Congratulations. (laughs)

3900
02:19:39.727 --> 02:19:40.560
You explore that, right?

3901
02:19:40.560 --> 02:19:44.720
You got a whole year to dive into that hole.

3902
02:19:44.720 --> 02:19:47.190
And the best way to begin that process

3903
02:19:47.190 --> 02:19:49.840
is to pick up Rich's new book, "The Attributes."

3904
02:19:49.840 --> 02:19:51.582
